/** OC siman 467 batch A — small commentators (542 keys) */
export const t = {
  "ateret-zekenim:1:_": `Grain that was submerged, etc. — if a sheet of water or snow fell upon the grain before grinding, one discards the upper wheat kernels and the rest is permitted; and specifically a small amount of water or snow, and its place is known; but grain placed in an attic and much rain descended through the roof and those that became moist became moistened — those that became moistened are forbidden; and in other places it is permitted to keep them on Pesach, but to eat them on Pesach is forbidden (Beit Yosef). Some are stringent even regarding honey that they extract from the hive before a Jew eats it raw, because of a decree lest it be confused; so too my teacher in the name of Maharshal that he too received thus; and specifically before Pesach one may make a drink from honey, for even if there were a mixture of flour in it, it was already nullified in sixty; but on Pesach itself it is forbidden because chametz on Pesach is in any amount. And some say that specifically the drink is permitted to make before Pesach, but the honey itself that a Jew cooked before Pesach — even though he strained it from the bees in it — they are forbidden to give it during Pesach into food; and one who is lenient has not lost, and one who is stringent — may he be blessed; and I already wrote in the name of my teacher in Bach that even honey that they draw from the hive is also forbidden because it comes to be confused, and so is the custom not to eat it; tsuker too is forbidden to eat. Tsuker kandil is permitted to keep — so proved Magen Avraham. A cooked dish that was cooked and wheat or barley was found in it, and also the spoon with which they stirred in the pot and placed it in another pot — all are forbidden (Tur, Rokeach). If a wheat kernel was found embedded in dough, see in responsum of Maharshal siman 79. If they scalded a chicken and a wheat kernel or barley kernel was found — it appears that even in great loss we forbid everything with a single uncertainty, and not as the author of the Hagah wrote to be lenient in this; and where there is a double uncertainty it is permitted to keep, and all the more so it is permitted to sell to a gentile, for it is not sold at a higher price on account of the wheat kernel, and it is found that he has no benefit from the wheat kernel at all; and a wheat kernel found in a vessel of water, an embedded wheat kernel and dough — both are equal, that while they are still in water they do not leaven, and when they come up from the water both leaven; and regarding practical halachah, as written in Hagahot Maimoni, if the wheat kernel softened they forbid the water, and all the more so full chametz or dough that leavened that fell into a pit — its water is forbidden and the cooked dish is forbidden too, and not as some are lenient — my teacher (in Bach):`,
  "ateret-zekenim:14:_": `And to permit the others, etc. — and some say everything by burning (Maharshal in responsum and also in Hagahot and in Avot), and so ruled Mahari Cohen in responsum She'erit Yosef, and he wrote that Margaliot also testified that he ruled thus; however, several later authorities among the lenient ones, like the opinion of the Rama — Maharam Padua in responsum and in responsum of the Mishna Berurah in the name of his son Mahari Padua, and my teacher in Bach wrote that he received from his rabbis like the opinion of the stringent ones, and so he was accustomed; and a wheat kernel found with the chicken in a bowl and not in the chicken itself is permitted by force of a double uncertainty — perhaps it was digested inside the chicken, and if you say it was not digested, perhaps it fell now into the bowl — Beit Yosef in the name of Mordechai):`,
  "baer-heitev:1:א": `In a river. In the Tur he wrote "and the chametz is not recognizable in them" — it implies that if the chametz is recognizable it is permitted to sell, for the buyer will see and not buy; Taz and Magen Avraham. But the Ran wrote that even if the chametz is recognizable it is forbidden to sell it to a gentile, for we are concerned lest he grind them and afterward sell to a Jew; see Chayei Adam (who rejected the reasoning of Magen Avraham) that since they do not buy flour from the market in a land, in a time of distress there is no concern — for that which is in siman 453 deals with matzah of the mitzvah specifically — see there; and he explains the intent of the Tur that it is a stringency, for even though the chametz is not recognizable and it is only a mere concern, nevertheless we are concerned; all the more so in certainty we are concerned for mishap:`,
  "baer-heitev:1:ב": `To keep them. And if one transgressed and kept them and planted them in the ground and their growth sprouted — their growth is permitted, as stated above in siman 443:`,
  "baer-heitev:1:ג": `And one informs him. But one does not rely on the proclamation; Shaarei Gibborim. And when one informs, even many are permitted to sell to a Jew; and even little by little — if he sells to a Jew he need not inform him — Chayei Adam see there. And Taz forbids selling much even when one informs, lest he forget to inform. And if he sold kav by kav it is permitted by force of uncertainty — perhaps he will not sell it to a Jew and perhaps he will eat them before Pesach — see there:`,
  "baer-heitev:1:ד": `So that it be consumed. Even if you say that also that little amount he will sell to a Jew, nevertheless that little amount the Jew will eat before Pesach; and therefore it implies that orally at the fifth hour regarding a matter where it is impossible that it be consumed before Pesach it is forbidden to sell to a gentile even a little, lest he sell to a Jew; and such is the agreement of Magen Avraham. Nevertheless it appears to me that if grain became chametz for a Jew — much grain on the eve of Pesach and it is in a matter where it is impossible for him to sell to a gentile so that it be consumed before Pesach and there is great loss — it is permitted to sell to a gentile who knows him and recognizes him that he will not touch it at all but guard it for him until after Pesach, and he is permitted to say to him "until you take it," etc., as written siman 448 seif 4; and then there is no concern for anything — Chayei Adam see there. And see Magen Avraham who raised that selling little by little is forbidden in other prohibitions — only here regarding chametz, for there are many uncertainties, they were lenient; and see Chayei Adam:`,
  "baer-heitev:1:ה": `To a Jew. And if the gentile said to the Jew even one word after he sold him wheat — "these wheats are for strawberries" — it is as written in Yoreh De'ah siman 17 that a gentile is not believed in what he said after he sold them and they left his possession; however, if he is trusted by him it is forbidden — Magen Avraham implies from his words that before he sold them, since they were in his possession, he is believed. And Chayei Adam left it as "this requires further study" see there; and see above siman 453 small seif 9:`,
  "baer-heitev:10:_": `To keep it. Because it is uncertainty of Yom Tov uncertainty of chol and mixture of rabbinic uncertainty — therefore we are lenient to keep it. And all the more so regarding noten ta'am le-fegam, for we are stringent on Pesach as written siman 447 — it is permitted to keep it on the last Yom Tov, for it is lenient regarding mixture of rabbinic uncertainty; but in full chametz that is forbidden d'oraisa even on the last Yom Tov it is forbidden in any case rabbinically; and specifically if it was found on the eighth day; but if it was found during Pesach one must burn and remove everything on chol ha-mo'ed; and if it was found on the seventh of Pesach — as written above siman 446 s.k. 4:`,
  "baer-heitev:11:א": `And so they are accustomed. Levush elaborated to challenge the Rabbi on this, and his opinion is to permit in dough in every matter that is not noten ta'am in cold see there; and Taz concludes that even though regarding other cold there is to permit according to Levush's opinion, nevertheless in dough — through engagement with the dough one compresses the wheat and it leavens and forbids the entire dough during Pesach if it softened; and so too in chametz bread one should be stringent even in cold, for there is concern for crumbs. And Ateret Zekenim raised thus, and such is the conclusion of Bach and Maharshal see Magen Avraham and Taz. And Chayei Adam wrote: nevertheless it appears to me according to what is written above that if the wheat is not embedded one may permit in benefit on the festival; if so, all the more so here in cold, for the opinion of many decisors is to permit in every matter; and one who is lenient even in eating has not lost, for it is possible that even when they split open one may be lenient in cold on the festival see there:`,
  "baer-heitev:11:ב": `Was. Meaning that we say: now it fell into this matzah and it was never in the dough. And Magen Avraham wrote: if the wheat was full of water and moist through and through — if so, it certainly was in water and in dough; if so, one must forbid the entire dough; nevertheless if there is to attribute that it was swallowed from another place from water and fell into the matzah — we attribute and everything according to the matter, for we attribute in the common whether to leniency or stringency, and it is not called uncertainty at all. And he further wrote in Magen Avraham: that which we say here "here it was found, here it was" — this is specifically from place to place or from piece to piece; but from time to time in one piece we establish presumptive prohibition. For example, if he has dough in his hand and cut from it and a piece of dough remained in his hand and a wheat kernel was found on it — all are forbidden, for we say perhaps it was there beforehand; and one may not say that it is a double uncertainty — perhaps it did not leaven — it is uncertainty of lack of knowledge and is not an uncertainty; and so Taz in s.k. 14 see there; and see Peri Chadash Yoreh De'ah siman 41 s.k. 11. And in responsum of Tzitz Eliezer siman 43. And in responsum Panim Me'irot siman 15. And in responsum Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 17 and siman 19. It is written in responsum of Maharam of Lublin siman 78 — an incident with one who took cabbage from the barrels in a bowl and brought it to another place and a wheat kernel was found on it, and he permitted the cabbage in the barrels, for we say "here it was found, here it was" — end quote. And Magen Avraham wrote: but if a wheat kernel was found in the barrels, even the cabbage in the bowl is forbidden, for we say perhaps the wheat kernel was there beforehand in the barrels, for from time to time in one piece we establish presumptive prohibition as written; and see small seif 25 in Magen Avraham who wrote: and if they removed it to another place and a wheat kernel was found on it — we say "now it fell," for it is from place to place — end s.k. for Mem-Dalet:`,
  "baer-heitev:11:ג": `Removal. Even in baked matzah, and even if there are sixty — we require the amount for removal:`,
  "baer-heitev:12:א": `From scalding. Plain scalding is in a second vessel — a cook. And see siman 447 seif 3 in the gloss. And it is possible here for the reason of pouring coming upon it, and the Mordechai did not read only on account of the bowl; and for practical halachah, if the second vessel is forbidden, for in chametz on the festival they forbid even in cold — Chayei Adam:`,
  "baer-heitev:12:ב": `Embedded. Since the Rabbi did not gloss here as above seif 9 to forbid even when it is not embedded, it is possible because here, since there is another uncertainty that perhaps it fell afterward, we do not stringency so much; but Taz wrote that the Rabbi goes according to his method to forbid even when it is not embedded — all that softened; but here it is permitted to sell to a gentile when it is not embedded, for it is a double uncertainty — perhaps it did not leaven and perhaps it fell afterward, for we do not stringency except in one uncertainty like that of seif 9 see there; and Chayei Adam is lenient even in that of seif 9 to sell to a gentile — see small seif 18:`,
  "baer-heitev:12:ג": `The chicken. For we say perhaps the wheat kernel was in water at the time of scalding — we do not say "here it was found and here it was," but from place to place or from piece to piece as written s.k. 21; but in one place, such as in scalding where there is room to doubt in time and to say perhaps the wheat kernel was in water beforehand — we do not say "now it fell" afterward. (For the presumption of the chicken is weakened, for it certainly was in water from leavening agents; and for this reason the cook forbids matzah [baked] in which a wheat kernel was found, and it is uncertain whether it leavened [while] [still] in dough — if so. And he permitted other matzot. (Chayei Adam) [Taz] see there):`,
  "baer-heitev:12:ד": `That forbids. See what is written in s.k. 20:`,
  "baer-heitev:12:ה": `Fell. And such are the words of Beit Yosef: Ravyah wrote in the name of Rashba — if a wheat kernel was found in a vessel in which they rinsed the chicken, we say "here it was found and here it was" — end quote. And Magen Avraham challenged from the words of Rashba himself who ruled that if a wheat kernel was found in matzah they forbid the entire dough and do not say "now it fell"; and he concludes: therefore it appears to me that it deals with where they salted the chicken and afterward rinsed it in a cold vessel and afterward a wheat kernel was found in the vessel, and there is concern that it was in the chicken and was salted with it; for this he wrote that we are not concerned for this, for since the wheat kernel was found in the vessels we do not say that it was in the chicken, for we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place; but in matzah it is from time to time as written; and if so, there is no dispute at all — unlike the opinion of Rama — end quote. And it should be said that what he wrote that in matzah it is from time to time as written — it deals with where dough remained in his hand as written s.k. 21, for otherwise it is from place to place — examine this:`,
  "baer-heitev:12:ו": `Uncertainty. Perhaps now it fell; and if you say it was previously in the chicken — perhaps it was digested — and see Magen Avraham:`,
  "baer-heitev:12:ז": `The chickens. And Magen Avraham wrote: it is not precise — chickens only; rather the same applies to meat, for here it deals with a whole wheat kernel where there is no digestion. And in Ein Mishpat he wrote: for amplification he cited chickens, even though wheat kernels are found in them — nevertheless no uncertainty emerges, and we say perhaps it fell afterward; also there is further room to doubt, perhaps he holds like one who says it is not noten ta'am at all in cold, which is like a doubt of a doubt; and Bach is stringent in these laws and challenged the Rabbi — see Magen Avraham. And the essential ruling is according to the Rabbi, and so agreed Ateret Zekenim and Taz; especially if the wheat is not split open it is plain that one need not be stringent as written; and without the wheat softening one may be lenient in every matter — and so agreed Bach and the later authorities:`,
  "baer-heitev:12:ח": `Rabbinic. Rokeach wrote: a piece of chametz was found in a bucket of water and it was not known when they took the water from the bucket whether there was chametz in it or not, and they cooked in it and they permitted the cooked dish; but the water in the bucket became forbidden because a gentile who brought the water perhaps put chametz in it at that hour — end quote. And Taz wrote: these words have no explanation, and it appears there is a scribal error and it should read: when they took water from the well; and the intent is that they drew water first from the well to cook, and afterward drew water in a bucket and chametz was found in the bucket and it was not known whether there was chametz in the well at the hour they drew to cook — from that itself they cooked in sour water; in this he ruled that the cooked dish is permitted, only the water in the bucket alone is forbidden; but the cooked dish is permitted because the gentile drawing to the bucket put the chametz at that hour, but that chametz was not in the well at all. And it is astonishing on Beit Yosef who brought it and did not notice this — and how can it be difficult, for it implies if the chametz was before it was cooked it would be forbidden, but chametz in cold is not forbidden; and it is possible he holds like Rashba who holds that also in cold it forbids; but we hold like Rosh and Tur that it does not forbid in cold, and it appears that if the chametz soaked in cold water from time to time it is forbidden for all, for soaked is like cooked — end quote. And so too Bach and Rashal. An incident with one who took into his mouth a piece of matzah and a piece of chicken and ate both together, and he felt in his mouth that there is a wheat kernel and removed the food from his mouth and saw a wheat kernel, and it is not recognizable whether it was inside the matzah or inside the chicken. The chicken may be permitted by force of double uncertainty — perhaps now it fell to the chicken in cold, and if you say it was previously — perhaps it was digested; but if it was in the matzah — necessarily it was inside it at the time of baking, for if it fell on it in cold it would roll and fall off; nevertheless it appears to me the matzah is also permitted, since rabbinic prohibition of uncertainty we attribute to leniency that it was in the chicken; nevertheless for removal by law it is forbidden from that matzah from the place he took the piece. And further it appears to me that even a piece of meat is permitted, for we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place, and we say "now it fell on the meat while it was still cold"; and if so, the matzah is also permitted; but if the meat was placed in a bowl and he ate from it — the meat is forbidden as written, for from time to time we establish presumptive prohibition — perhaps it was in the bowl at the hour they poured the meat into it; and a second vessel forbids on Pesach. And if we say it is double uncertainty — perhaps it was in the matzah, and if you say in the meat — perhaps now it fell — this is not an uncertainty, for if you say it was in the matzah you must forbid the matzah, and we do not say this even in rabbinic prohibition and even in double uncertainty; and if so, the meat and matzah are forbidden; however according to the majority it is recognizable whether the wheat was cooked or baked with it or not. And nevertheless if they moved the bowl from place to place when it cooled, we attribute to leniency, for we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place — Magen Avraham:`,
  "baer-heitev:12:ט": `That fell. See Magen Avraham and in Chayei Adam:`,
  "baer-heitev:13:א": `A day old. But if it is not a day old, even the water itself that was boiled is permitted on Pesach, since they boiled it before Pesach and they are not a day old, as explained in siman 451 — Chayei Adam:`,
  "baer-heitev:13:ב": `The acid. And Bach wrote that even if they became mixed before Pesach in a matter where they were nullified in sixty, and even though it is also noten ta'am bar noten ta'am of the permitted — nevertheless one may not use that water l'chatchila, since it is possible to find other water easily; and so too in Yoreh De'ah siman 95; and Taz wrote: in my humble opinion it appears that here sometimes it is forbidden even b'dieved even before Pesach, even if the sour water remained above and did not become mixed, for they stood and persisted and are as if in their own form; unless he drew from it beforehand several times, for thereby it becomes mixed see there. (And from what is compared to "he did not shake and cover" in Yoreh De'ah siman 92 see there.) And Chayei Adam wrote that one does not forbid b'dieved even though it was not nullified; nevertheless since it occurred before Pesach it is noten ta'am bar noten ta'am of the permitted, and one does not forbid b'dieved see there:`,
  "baer-heitev:13:ג": `That became mixed. Meaning: even to permit in cold here it is forbidden, since the leavening substance itself became mixed. And if a wheat kernel or other chametz is found in this pit — it depends on the dispute that the Mechaber wrote seif 12 whether it is forbidden in cold during Pesach; and see above s.k. 20 and s.k. for Mem-Dalet from what is written; and see Chayei Adam. Taz wrote: once a servant brought a vessel with honey called mead to drink, and one wheat kernel was found in it; I said that it does not forbid in cold, even though it leavened, and even though it is written in Shulchan Aruch seif 12 "and there is one who forbids during Pesach" — we have already proven that we did not find this distinction, that cold does not impart taste at all, and even on Pesach it is permitted; and also there is no concern here that perhaps the wheat kernel was at the hour the honey was boiling, for if so it was already nullified before Pesach from what was expelled from it, and it does not return and revive even though the wheat kernel is still inside it — nevertheless it was cold at that time and does not impart taste — end quote. And it is difficult — perhaps it was soaked inside it from time to time as written in s.k. 30; and it should be said that the incident was on the first night of Pesach — examine this:`,
  "baer-heitev:14:א": `By rinsing. For cold without gravy is not forbidden according to all, even in chicken where it is impossible without a little moisture — nevertheless in cold it does not absorb according to all, except through soaking a little. (In responsum of Tzitz Eliezer he was stringent regarding a little beer remaining in a vessel and they took water and rinsed meat in it — one may compare it to the law of a wheat kernel salted with chicken; and Chayei Adam struck a spark on this see there.) Or through engagement with dough as written:`,
  "baer-heitev:14:ב": `Salting. Meaning while he was still salting it; but if it was found after he salted and rinsed, we say "now it fell" — for if it exists that it was on it beforehand it would fall off in rinsing when he rinsed it; and specifically when found on the chicken, but if found inside the chicken it certainly was there beforehand — Magen Avraham; and Chayei Adam wrote that in siman 447 I wrote that pure salted and impure rinsed — also on Pesach there is to permit; and here it deals where the wheat kernel was also salted with the chicken; and I saw meticulous ones careful on Pesach before scalding and salting to open the chicken well or cut it in two and check well lest a wheat kernel be found inside it. And it is written in Shachnahag: one who does so is diligent and rewarded:`,
  "baer-heitev:14:ג": `All of them. Meaning when the wheat kernel is found on the chicken, for there is room to doubt that perhaps it touched all of them; but if found inside the chicken the others do not require peeling — Magen Avraham:`,
  "baer-heitev:14:ד": `They are stringent. Because we estimate in all prohibitions salting in sixty — if so, also in chametz it forbids all of it (see Yoreh De'ah siman 105):`,
  "baer-heitev:14:ה": `The essential, etc. Because he holds that we do not say in rabbinic prohibition that prohibition goes out from piece to piece through salting; and what they estimate in other prohibitions in sixty is because of a definitive matter that we need not estimate between fat and lean — we go with this even to leniency as written in Yoreh De'ah siman 105; if so, also in chametz sixty suffices. And further that specifically in a prohibition where greasiness applies do they estimate in sixty, but not in chametz prohibition where spreading does not apply. And further that where there is another side to permit we rely on his question that he holds chametz is nullified in sixty — and you have no greater side of permission than this, that many gaonim hold that taste does not spread in salting; and one may make a compromise to forbid that piece and no more; and one who wishes to be stringent may decide to burn that piece and keep the others. And if they were cooked without peeling they are permitted, for peeling is only a stringency; nevertheless it appears to me that the piece requires peeling, only the peel does not forbid the piece even though it was cooked with it. And specifically where there is no doubt that perhaps it touched the other pieces; but if there is doubt that it touched the other pieces — by law all are forbidden; and even though we know with certainty that it touched only one of them and we do not know which one it touched — by law all are forbidden and require peeling, for chametz on Pesach is not nullified in the majority; and if so, if it was cooked without peeling it forbids their mixture; for specifically where peeling is only a mere stringency the peel does not forbid the piece, but where peeling is required by law — if it was cooked without peeling it forbids the piece — Magen Avraham; and see what is written in Yoreh De'ah siman 91 s.k. 7; and see what is written above siman 447 s.k. 6; and Taz wrote: since we saw in this law dispute between early and later — Rashal in responsum siman 79 rules to burn everything, and so Darkei Yosef and Bach. And Mishna Berurah disagrees to rule like the lenient and is lenient in the other pieces even without peeling. And Rama in responsum siman 108 wrote: one who wishes to be stringent may decide to burn that piece and keep the others until after Pesach. Therefore it appears to me to practice practically in this manner: if a wheat kernel is found on salted meat dry without brine — one may permit keeping everything; only the place where it was found one peels, and the rest is permitted to keep until after Pesach. But if the wheat kernel is found inside the brine of meat or fish — then it is considered as cooked, for in a small measure it is like cooked in brine or in vinegar, as explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 105; and all the more so if it rested there from time to time — then its law is that everything resting in the brine is forbidden, for it is as if cooked in one pot and requires removal; but what rests above the brine one may also keep until after Pesach, for taste does not spread in brine upward, as explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 68. And therefore what touches the meat in the brine one peels, and the rest one keeps or does as Rama wrote at the end of the seif — to sell everything to a gentile — meaning what is not in the brine; for what is in the brine is forbidden to sell, for it is full chametz — end quote; and Chayei Adam agreed with him see there. And it is plain that all this is when the wheat softened as written s.k. 18 see there. It is written in responsum of Tzemach Tzedek siman 434: if a little beer remained in a vessel and they took water and rinsed meat in it after salting on Pesach — even though water nullifies the power of salt, nevertheless since it weakened it a little and absorbed somewhat, it has the law of a wheat kernel salted with chicken for each of the opinions in this seif see there; and see Chayei Adam s.k. 46. It is written in responsum of Maharam of Lublin siman 91: a wheat kernel placed in a barrel of salted cabbage — the entire barrel is forbidden, for soaked is like cooked see there. And Magen Avraham wrote: and it should be said that it deals where the brine rises above it; and if the brine does not rise above it, it has the law of salting. And so I heard that a wheat kernel was found in a barrel of fish salted for Pesach, and the Gaon Rabbi Binyamin Av Beit Din of the holy community of Poznan commanded to throw that layer into the river and the rest to sell to a gentile — and see Magen Avraham:`,
  "baer-heitev:15:א": `Split open. Plain for us the law is thus when it softened, even though it is not split open — Taz. And in Shachnahag he wrote: since the Rabbi did not gloss here "even when it is not split open," it appears he holds that when it is not split open one does not forbid in roasting, only the amount for removal see there; but the opinion of other later authorities is not so; and see what is written in s.k. 18 in the name of Chayei Adam and s.k. 24:`,
  "baer-heitev:15:ב": `Its spreading. And it deals with a lean chicken, for otherwise it spreads and all of it is forbidden as written siman 447 seif 1:`,
  "baer-heitev:15:ג": `Spreads. Specifically regarding rabbinic prohibition of uncertainty we are concerned for this; unlike other prohibitions — see Chayei Adam and in Magen Avraham; and Ateret Zekenim wrote: and if he does not turn the spit, it appears from the opinion of Rama that roasting has the law of salting — end quote:`,
  "baer-heitev:15:ד": `One in another. But if they touch one another they do not forbid, and we do not say that absorption goes throughout the entire vessel, as written in Yoreh De'ah siman 92 — there it deals with cooking. And if so, if the chicken is fatty, even if they do not touch they forbid everything — Magen Avraham. Nevertheless when they are not split open it appears to me one need not be stringent — Chayei Adam. It is written in Shachnahag: an incident came to me on the seventh night of Pesach — they roasted two chickens and a barley kernel that is not split open was found in one of them, and I commanded to cut away the place of its spreading according to estimation and throw it away and eat the rest; and they said they were already cut into pieces with the others and do not know where it is — and I permitted everything see there. And according to his method it goes as written in small seif 40; but the opinion of other later authorities is not so as written. And see in responsum of Chacham Tzvi siman 66 and in Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 17:`,
  "baer-heitev:16:_": `Permitted. And even peeling is not needed, for the crop is in place of peeling. And Bach ruled to be stringent to forbid all of it. And Taz decided to forbid in eating and permit in benefit; and other later authorities agree to permit completely with scalding; and see above s.k. 36 from what is written. It is written in Sefer Tanya: all matters of Pesach one should not do through a gentile:`,
  "baer-heitev:2:א": `In a river. And if it is permitted to nullify this grain in the majority or in sixty before Pesach, since it is still a time of permission — the later authorities elaborated on this, and Chayei Adam raised that it is permitted to nullify l'chatchila see there; and see Magen Avraham:`,
  "baer-heitev:2:ב": `Rested at ease. For specifically regarding soaking it is not forbidden unless they split open, since one is engaged with it. (And for the Tur and Rosh who are stringent regarding soaking — all that are close to splitting, like Mar Ukva — therefore the reason that from resting at ease we forbid even if it did not soften; but according to the opinion of Shulchan Aruch we require at least close to splitting — and see Shaarei Teshuvah.) And it should be said that since from resting in the cooked dish it boils and it is like scalding, we do not stringency so much, and especially since it is only a rabbinic matter. And see Chayei Adam:`,
  "baer-heitev:2:ג": `Leavening. And if it had not yet rested the measure of a mil it had not yet leavened; and if so, even if it occurred on Pesach it is permitted to sell it to a gentile before it rested the measure of a mil — Magen Avraham:`,
  "baer-heitev:2:ד": `Forbidden. Even if part of them was eaten before Pesach, likewise one may not permit eating the rest; for specifically dry with dry when one of them was eaten, or in three mixtures, there is permission to eat two as explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 110 see there — unlike moist with moist there is no permission in this, and flour is called moist with moist. Bach and Magen Avraham and Chayei Adam see there:`,
  "baer-heitev:2:ה": `Uncertainty. Taz raised that in a place of loss or prevention of Yom Tov joy, such as when he has no other, there is to permit even in eating see there, and Chayei Adam agreed with him see there. And if a little snow or water fell on grain and its place is known — one scrapes off the upper ones and the rest are permitted — Ateret Zekenim:`,
  "baer-heitev:3:_": `We establish presumptive prohibition. See above siman 453 small seif 10 from what is written:`,
  "baer-heitev:4:_": `For ordinary. It is written in Shachnahag that practically he permitted buying wheat for Pesach even though they were stored in pits, as long as we did not see change in their appearance and they are not moist see there. And if there are wheat that sprouted in them — see above siman 453:`,
  "baer-heitev:8:א": `In barrels, etc., that they make, etc. Explanation: two things are required — specifically from that which is in the barrel it is permitted to make a drink; for even if you say there is a little flour in it, it is nullified in sixty at the time of cooking before Pesach; but those that they bring in small vessels are forbidden even to make a drink from them, and many times it was clarified to me that they mix much flour in them. And to eat the honey in its own form in any case is forbidden, for it comes to be confused; and so too Maharil and Maharshal. And even to make the drink on Pesach — Bach; however honey that they bring with small hives whole as it lies in the hive, not yet crushed — they practice permissively according to all, for there is no way to mix anything in it when they shake them from the hive to the hive; and see Magen Avraham. And Taz wrote that the implication of the Rabbi's words implies those they bring in barrels from the hive — that even to eat it thus in its own form is permitted; therefore it appears to me complete permission to cook on chol ha-mo'ed a drink in honey that is recognizable as coming from a hive, as long as it is fit for drinking on Yom Tov; even though Mo'ach wrote to forbid making the drink on the festival from concern for mixture — nevertheless it appears to me that one who is lenient for honor of Yom Tov in these lands where we have no other drink on Pesach has not lost. See there and see in Shachnahag and Chayei Adam:`,
  "baer-heitev:8:ב": `Dried fruits. But they are accustomed to make from them a drink — that is, they pour cold water on them and soak them until it becomes a drink; and so testified Magen Avraham that such is the custom in the holy community of Kalish; but one may not cook with them — see there that he elaborated on the reason for the custom:`,
  "baer-heitev:8:ג": `And tsuker. Ateret Zekenim wrote: I saw that a question came before the elders, the Gaon Maharash, may his memory be blessed, on the eve of Pesach — hot tsuker had rested, and he ruled to sell to a gentile — end quote. And Taz and Magen Avraham wrote that there is no prohibition in hot tsuker or in what we call kandil tsuker to keep it in one's house; nevertheless they are accustomed to sell it to a gentile l'chatchila, but b'dieved it is permitted — and this is the intent of the Rabbi in the gloss: tsuker kandil. And not what we now call tsuker kandil — that one has more concern and is full chametz, for they cook white tsuker in sour vats and it becomes red, and even to keep it is forbidden because there is a mixture of chametz in it. And all the more so types of tsuker confections in which they coat types of fruits and spices — this has full leavening. And Chayei Adam wrote that even l'chatchila one should be careful regarding keeping these things; nevertheless if one transgressed and kept those mixtures and there is great loss or prevention of Yom Tov joy, one does not forbid b'dieved, for there is much uncertainty and concern — perhaps it was not mixed in it; and if you say it was mixed in it, perhaps it was nullified before Pesach; and if you say it was not nullified, perhaps they did not mix wheat flour in it for strawberries; and fruit juice does not leaven; and if you say for soaking — perhaps it did not leaven through this soaking. Nevertheless before the decisor rules he needs investigation by a sage how and what was done, for this matter varies according to place and time; and l'chatchila they are accustomed to be stringent in every matter. But what we now call tsuker kandil — many common people are lenient and this error is drawn for them from the language of the Rabbi in the gloss; and the later authorities already agreed that it is an error in their hands, as written. And see in Shachnahag that he wrote that also tsuker that is ground very fine has also concern for leavening; and also the drink made from tsuker that they call sharpi or lat and arig — they are accustomed to be stringent; and see in Sefer Bnei Chayya:`,
  "baer-heitev:8:ד": `Zafarin. And it is written in Magen Avraham and Tov Ani Keneged Kulam that the way is to soak it in beer and one must seal it in a room or make a partition before it; and if so, the same applies to saffron see there; and see above siman 451 from the law of dyed vessels in saffron that one may not use it on Pesach:`,
  "baer-heitev:8:ה": `Their mixture. This refers to all that was mentioned above, and even in less than sixty it is permitted. And in tsuker too one does not forbid the mixture even in types of tsuker confections on the festival; and such one may be lenient as written in s.k. 15 see there; however regarding honey Darkei Moshe wrote that if a little of it became mixed in a cooked dish on Pesach one does not forbid it — it implies that if it became mixed in less than sixty it is forbidden. (And see what is written small seif 7.) And such is essential; but in saffron and the rest one need not be stringent even in less than sixty see there; and see Chayei Adam:`,
  "baer-heitev:9:_": `Everything. The cover and the spoon and also the vessels that were used with them — and the same applies to other cooked dishes that became mixed with that cooked dish or were used with those vessels — all are forbidden; and so they practice, even though there are two rabbinic uncertainties — unlike Taz, as written siman 447 s.k. 20 see there. And Chayei Adam wrote: since the Rabbi did not write here "burn everything" as written in seif 10, it certainly appears that the Rabbi's opinion is where they did not actually split open, according to the opinion of all great decisors to permit the cooked dish even in eating — if so, this stringency alone to forbid in eating and not in benefit see there; and see Taz siman 7 and s.k. 15; and Bach wrote that in any case the wheat softened — we require this, for otherwise one does not forbid at all; and so too Taz:`,
  "beer-hagolah:1:_": `The wording of the Rambam in chapter 5 from the incident of the boat that sank, etc., Pesachim page 40, and according to his version there in the Gemara`,
  "beer-hagolah:10:א": `Mordechai in chapter 3 of Pesachim`,
  "beer-hagolah:10:ב": `Tur in the name of R' Eliezer Mimitz and Shiltei HaGibborim`,
  "beer-hagolah:11:א": `Tur in the name of his father the Rosh in chapter 2 of Pesachim`,
  "beer-hagolah:11:ב": `There in the name of others and so too the Rashba in responsum 177`,
  "beer-hagolah:11:ג": `Tur in the name of some great ones`,
  "beer-hagolah:12:א": `Tur in the name of Ra'avan`,
  "beer-hagolah:12:ב": `There in the name of Avi HaEzri`,
  "beer-hagolah:12:ג": `That two things are required — that there be more than sixty and that the water be cold`,
  "beer-hagolah:12:ד": `So is the explanation of the Beit Yosef on the words of the Tur that he permits according to the opinion of the Rosh.`,
  "beer-hagolah:13:א": `Tur and the Meiri`,
  "beer-hagolah:13:ב": `In chapter 2 of Pesachim — Beit Yosef`,
  "beer-hagolah:13:ג": `Beit Yosef`,
  "beer-hagolah:14:א": `Rabbeinu Yerucham`,
  "beer-hagolah:14:ב": `Tur in the name of the Rosh, Rashba in the name of Ra'em`,
  "beer-hagolah:14:ג": `Kol Bo in the name of Ra'avad`,
  "beer-hagolah:15:א": `Tur in the name of the Rosh in the name of R' Eliezer Mimitz to the version of the Beit Yosef, and so wrote Orchot Chayyim`,
  "beer-hagolah:15:ב": `Tur in the name of Rokeach`,
  "beer-hagolah:15:ג": `There in the name of his father the Rosh`,
  "beer-hagolah:16:א": `The Ran end of chapter 2 of Pesachim, and he explained there that peeling suffices, and the skin of the crop is considered peeling`,
  "beer-hagolah:16:ב": `Tur in the name of his father the Rosh that one may not derive prohibition from impurity, for regarding prohibition it is not considered digestion; and so wrote Tosafos in Chullin 96 and the Mordechai in chapter 7 of Pesachim and Hagahot Maimoni in chapter 1 in the name of the Hagahot Maimuni`,
  "beer-hagolah:2:_": `Tur in the name of R' Efrayim and Hagahot Maimoni in the name of R' Chananel:`,
  "beer-hagolah:3:_": `Tur in the name of the author of Halachot Gedolot and in the name of R' Tzemach Gaon:`,
  "beer-hagolah:4:_": `Responsum of the Rashba`,
  "beer-hagolah:5:_": `Rashba in responsum from that which is in Pesachim 33`,
  "beer-hagolah:6:_": `Responsum of the Ran`,
  "beer-hagolah:7:_": `In responsum to the Ramban siman 128`,
  "beer-hagolah:8:_": `Tur and Hagahot Maimoni in the name of Ra'avan and Rosh in responsum general 24:`,
  "beer-hagolah:9:_": `Tur in the name of the Rif and so too the Ramban in chapter 5`,
  "beur-hagra:1:א": `Seif 1 — just as, etc. Since Rava needed to sell it:`,
  "beur-hagra:1:ב": `Rather he sells it. The Rambam's version: keva keva so that, etc.; and there is an alternate version "to an Israelite," and it refers to what was said initially to sell to Eretz Yisrael; but to an Israelite, since he informs him, it is permitted in any amount, as written in chapter 3 of Demai mishnah 3 R' Yehuda certainly permits, etc.:`,
  "beur-hagra:10:א": `Seif 10 — chicken, etc. As written in Avodah Zarah 65b — that crate of wheat, etc., and Mordechai chapter 2 and so too Ramban there from Yerushalmi chapter 3 of Orlah on mishnah: a garment dyed with orlah peels — it must burn; and we learned: if one removed from it a saddlecloth and wove the garment — forbidden in benefit; and we learned: if yayin nesech fell into a pit — all is forbidden in benefit; and we learned Rabbenu Shimon says: sell all of it to a gentile except the value of the yayin nesech in it — R' Yirmiyah bar Acha questioned: who is like that? There gentiles are not accustomed to buy wine from gentiles, but here gentiles are accustomed to buy garments from an Israelite — therefore anything that is not customary to buy from a gentile is permitted in all prohibitions, and so is the opinion of the Rosh in Orlah there, and Tosafos Zevachim 72b s.v. kulam and Yevamos chapter 1 s.v. kulam, etc., except that Mordechai challenged that the drop around the wheat kernel is chametz and it is like wine in wine that is permitted only for yayin nesech of rabbis and not d'oraisa as written above; but in Milchamot there he wrote that other prohibitions even if their prohibition is d'oraisa nevertheless for stam yeinam we compare them, for we are more stringent with stam yeinam than all prohibitions since it is in any amount, and we say in chapter 2 of Pesachim and chapter 2 of Avodah Zarah, etc., that this d'oraisa, etc., whatever the rabbis enacted, etc., and specifically complete yayin nesech which is among prohibitions atop prohibitions we are stringent, etc., and so wrote the Ran in Avodah Zarah there — except that he wrote regarding redemption that even in stam yeinam specifically barrel in barrel and not wine in wine — see what is written in Yoreh De'ah siman 142:`,
  "beur-hagra:10:ב": `That it is not sold, etc. — meaning one need not say except the chametz, etc., and as in that cellar of wheat in chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah that Rava permitted to sell them, etc., anonymously — Mordechai there siman 491 in the name of R' Yechiel of Paris — see there, and Tosafos Chullin 99 s.v. vehalachta, etc.:`,
  "beur-hagra:10:ג": `And in these countries, etc. — for they hold specifically in stam yeinam the rabbis were lenient, as written above in Yoreh De'ah letter 1 practical halachah, etc., and see Mordechai double uncertainty 491 and siman 493, and even though in other matters we do not distinguish between stam yeinam and chametz as written there 34a and chapter Kol Sha'ah, etc., whatever the rabbis enacted, etc., here the Gemara distinguishes explicitly:`,
  "beur-hagra:10:ד": `And if it was found on the eighth day, etc. — Magen Avraham, and the same in seif 9 in a cooked dish wheat and barley were found:`,
  "beur-hagra:11:א": `Seif 11 — if it was found, etc. — for if it is not soaked, per his words it is permitted, for presumably it was not in water since they are guarded; and if it was in flour or dough it does not hurry to leaven more than the dough — soaked is not chametz but only hurries to leaven from what is written there: one soaks in vinegar, etc. Rosh there:`,
  "beur-hagra:11:ב": `One removes, etc. As written in chapter Keitzad Tzolin 97b: one takes its place — a drop from its gravy on the flour, one scoops, etc.:`,
  "beur-hagra:11:ג": `And there is one, etc. — Magen Avraham. And further that the water in the dough, etc., and according to what the Rosh wrote regarding a se'or house that kneading is like hot with gravy that forbids in all of it, as written in Chullin 96b and 108a:2, and all the more so in matzah that was baked:`,
  "beur-hagra:11:ד": `And they permit, etc., and we say. As written in Kesubos 75b:`,
  "beur-hagra:11:ה": `And if it was found — specifically from place to place we say here it was found, but not from time to time as written there while still in her father's house we do not say it was born now, and in Niddah 3:2 one who wrings out, etc., — he did not say except, etc., even though from place to place they argue — Chizkiyah and R' Yochanan, for R' Yehuda holds in impurity we are stringent — see Tosafos there 4a s.v. ve'mai. And one can say Chizkiyah holds that even in impurity we do not establish presumptive prohibition, and nevertheless Chizkiyah concedes to R' Mona as written there — and Magen Avraham note 18:`,
  "beur-hagra:11:ו": `But before. It is nullified in sixty, and even though according to the first reason of Rashba that it touched here and here one should forbid even before Pesach — one need not be concerned for this since it is cold — see Yoreh De'ah siman 105 seif 9 in the gloss, and if he does not know, etc., and all the more so here; nevertheless for removal one needs even before Pesach, for we consider it chametz at the place of contact as above — only in its uncertainty we are not stringent:`,
  "beur-hagra:12:א": `Seif 12 — if they scalded, etc. — for we do not say after they removed the chicken it fell, for we do not say from time to time as above in seif 11:`,
  "beur-hagra:12:ב": `And if an embedded wheat kernel was found, etc., there is nothing in this. So wrote the Tur: since the water is cold the wheat kernel does not impart taste, and even complete chametz that fell into a pit is permitted for this reason, and Beit Yosef wrote — even though from the words of the Rosh in chapter Kol Sha'ah in that case in seif 11 that a wheat kernel was found in dough he wrote one should not be concerned perhaps the wheat kernel was in water — forty days — for if it was in water it forbids in cold — there it deals during Pesach in any amount, and here it deals before Pesach; and proof from what the Tur wrote that in cold water the wheat kernel does not impart taste, etc., and if during Pesach why mention imparting taste — it forbids in any amount; and therefore he said there is nothing in this because the reason that it does not absorb in cold is because it is any amount — therefore some forbid during Pesach, and their reason is that they hold as Ribba in Tosafos Pesachim 76a s.v. tania that both sixty and cold rinsing are required — only its prohibition is slight unlike during Pesach; and it deals even in less than sixty, not like Magen Avraham as above:`,
  "beur-hagra:12:ג": `And there is one who permits. Meaning since any amount is rabbinic we are lenient in its uncertainty as written in Shabbos 34a and Eruvin 5b and other places, and as the Rif wrote in a cooked dish where a wheat kernel was found as above in seif 9 — even though Rama does not hold thus, he wrote this opinion here because in cold or b'eyna one relies on this opinion as written:`,
  "beur-hagra:12:ד": `And there is one, etc. — meaning the double uncertainty that perhaps after they removed the chicken it fell, and if you say it was in the chicken, perhaps it was digested in the chicken as written in chapter 1 of Ahilot: we learned regarding digestion how long it remains in the intestines, etc., and in birds and fish for the time until it falls to fire and burns, and we derive prohibition from impurity as written at the end of Avodah Zarah as they said in purity, so in prohibition, and see siman 462 seif 9, and as the Rif wrote in chapter 3 of Pesachim we learn that anything that does not become impure with impurity of foods, etc.; but Tosafos Menachos 69a and the Rosh in Ahilot questioned from Bechoros 7b that a kosher fish that swallowed an impure fish is forbidden in eating, and answer in the name of R' Hai that one distinguishes between prohibition and impurity, as written in chapter 1 of Yevamos — therefore R' Yehuda [disagrees] only regarding, etc.; and they further answered that fish are hard, and as in Menachos that meat is soft, etc., that bones are hard — therefore they said there wheat in cattle dung, etc., for eating it becomes impure with impurity of foods, even for meal offerings only because of "present it now" — acceptably to your offering; and they further answered that perhaps that case in Bechoros is according to Ribba who said in Ahilot regarding birds and fish from the outset without waiting it deals. And Ra'avan wrote that even according to the first tanna perhaps the fish swallowed a moment before it was caught and did not wait the measure of digestion, and as in Chullin 91a, and Rashba there s.v. mi lo iyakvi, etc., and digestion is only from the living as in Tosefta there, and the Rosh brought it: a dog that ate nevelah meat, etc., if it died — if it did not wait in its intestines, etc., from the outset the house is impure, and in birds, etc., and therefore he said from the force of double uncertainty and according to Ra'avan:`,
  "beur-hagra:12:ה": `And regarding practical halachah. The conclusion of Tosafos there and Rosh there that whether for prohibition or impurity in anything whole that is recognizable that it was not digested and not chewed in his mouth, such as whole wheat in cattle dung and whole fish — therefore he cited fish that swallowed while whole, and see there and forty days from Tosafos in conclusion they are not concerned for these resolutions — therefore he ruled here in cold or b'eyna as the first version that we are lenient in uncertainty of any amount since it is rabbinic, and in a broken wheat kernel in a chicken he cited as Tosafos' conclusion that there is digestion only that he was concerned for Ra'avan's uncertainty and permits through double uncertainty; but in whole or in meat, since there is only one uncertainty and it is in hot and b'eyna is not stringent per Tosafos' version and their conclusion as above:`,
  "beur-hagra:13:א": `Seif 13 — day-old. Otherwise remove and it is permitted according to Shulchan Aruch in siman 467 seif 10. Beit Yosef:`,
  "beur-hagra:13:ב": `During Pesach. Before Pesach it is nullified in sixty in the pit. Beit Yosef. And even though hag'alah does not help during Pesach as in siman 452 seif 1 in the gloss, and even according to Shulchan Aruch who permits remove — nevertheless for day-old it does not help, and if so as Magen Avraham in the name of Darkei Moshe that he hag'eled before the time of prohibition and they descended into the pit during Pesach — even though it is taste imparted by taste imparted, nevertheless l'chatchila it is forbidden as Yoreh De'ah siman 95 seif 2 in the gloss, apart from the matters being strained in themselves — one for Shulchan Aruch there who permits even l'chatchila, and even for those who forbid there it is only because of custom, and here from the law it is forbidden; and further even for those who forbid there in such a case it is three taste imparted by taste imparted that is permitted as Tosafos end of Avodah Zarah and Rosh chapter Kol Sha'ah and as written above siman 432 seif 1 — except they hold like those who say that whatever is boiling releases, boiling absorbs, does not absorb — and according to this even during Pesach one may hag'el in any case only that they should not rest from their boiling, and as written there:`,
  "beur-hagra:13:ג": `Because. Magen Avraham:`,
  "beur-hagra:14:א": `Seif 14 — if it was found, etc. Magen Avraham and as written in Pesachim 76a: cold into hot, etc., and see there:`,
  "beur-hagra:14:ב": `And if after. Salting is like boiling, roasting is like boiling as in Chullin 97b, and peeling suffices as in Pesachim there — one peels its place, and even for the stringent in roasting — as much as removal; and he compared to a drop from gravy, etc., there in salting they agree peeling suffices — Rosh in Chullin there, and see Yoreh De'ah siman 91 and siman 105, and all the more so per his words they objected in Chullin 112a, but roasting requires peeling — boiling is discussed, not like Tosafos there 96b s.v. ad and Rashi Pesachim 76a that cold is discussed, and so in Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah siman 91 that in roasting boiling is meant, and if so even in roasting peeling suffices — see below siman 467 seif 1 and what is written:`,
  "beur-hagra:14:ג": `And there are those who are stringent, etc. Since its prohibition is in any amount and specifically around it one is concerned for that any amount. Kol Bo in the name of Ra'avad, and he holds the peel forbids again, not like Tosafos Chullin 96 s.v. afilu except that in other prohibitions since the prohibition absorbed in the peel does not forbid because it spreads in its depth as written here — and there are views against it unlike here — and one asks: let it make its depth nevelah and forbid again as Rabbenu Tam wrote there 101 in s.v. shekadam. And Or Zarua, etc.:`,
  "beur-hagra:14:ד": `And if they were salted, etc. — meaning and he does not know perhaps it touched all of them — Magen Avraham and as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 seif 9 and if it is not known, etc.:`,
  "beur-hagra:14:ה": `And there are those who are stringent, etc. Magen Avraham and as in Chullin 97a — salting is different because it bubbles and salting is like boiling like roasting, and even though taste does not pass from piece to piece without gravy as in Chullin 108a:2 and Tosafos there s.v. tipach, etc., here there is concern perhaps it touched all of them and forbids all of them in any amount:`,
  "beur-hagra:14:ו": `The essential ruling to forbid, etc. — for by law it is not forbidden even for those who require sixty in salting because we are not expert — only in something to which greasiness applies, unlike chametz as in Yoreh De'ah there in the gloss — only because of stringency therefore they do not forbid in uncertainty all of them that perhaps it touched them, only the peel that is forbidden by law — Magen Avraham:`,
  "beur-hagra:14:ז": `And if he wishes, etc. — for otherwise he permits in Shulchan Aruch above seif 11, and even for those who forbid there because its prohibition is d'oraisa, but for rabbinic prohibitions not, and all the more so here which is only a stringency in itself:`,
  "beur-hagra:15:א": `Seif 15 — chicken, etc., one cuts, etc. As in Chullin 96b and Pesachim 76b, and as Ra'avad wrote in siman 14 that one deepens, etc., as written there:`,
  "beur-hagra:15:ב": `And there are those who forbid, etc. — for in their days they did not turn the spit as in Chullin 111a, and Rashba there s.v. halachta, etc., and he holds turning the spit is like something that bubbles that forbids all of it as written 97a, but taste does not pass from piece to piece as written there 108 and Tosafos s.v. tipat:`,
  "beur-hagra:15:ג": `And so there is, etc. — for he holds turning the spit is like shaking and covering that spreads through all the pieces as written 108a:2 — see there in Tosafos:`,
  "beur-hagra:16:א": `Seif 16 — the bird is permitted. Taz and Magen Avraham:`,
  "beur-hagra:16:ב": `And the wheat kernel, etc., not like, etc. — for one does not derive prohibition from impurity. Tur. Therefore there is no distinction between whole or broken, and Tosafos Bechoros 7b and Chullin 94 s.v. amar Rava — and he cited this version because all other resolutions are strained when they distinguish whole from broken — for then what is challenged there from bones perhaps they were whole — and even though they strained there in Tosafos and similarly Ra'avan's answer that for it to fall to fire is a slight matter, and so in Tosefta it said in birds immediately — only answer that one must be stringent like Ribba that in Torah law we follow the stringent as Tosafos Chullin there, and Hagahot Ashiri there since it is hard it is only rabbinic, and all the more so to forbid roasting as the Tur wrote — but there is no need to distinguish prohibition from impurity, for Tosafos too in conclusion in Menachos do not hold thus. And proof they said Chullin 116b but they soak, etc., and in the stomach of a kosher animal, etc., and the Rif there, and Tosafos who wished to distinguish prohibition from impurity follow their views that distinguish clear from thick, and the essence is as they wrote that they distinguish hard from soft, and as stated there in the Gemara explicitly — therefore fish is forbidden and all the more so wheat that is firm — and therefore it said there wheat, etc., for eating it becomes impure, etc.:`,
  "beur-hagra:2:א": `Seif 2 — this grain. Therefore Rava did not ask whether they split open. Hagahot Maimoni and Ra'avan there:`,
  "beur-hagra:2:ב": `From the force of a double uncertainty. As written in Kesubos 9a:`,
  "beur-hagra:2:ג": `But not to eat it. Since he has permissible grain, a double uncertainty does not help, as written at the beginning of Yoma 3b; and its uncertainty is forbidden, and if it became mixed, etc. Mordechai end of chapter 3 of Avodah Zarah, and see Yoreh De'ah siman 110.`,
  "beur-hagra:3:_": `Seif 3 — wheat, etc., presumptive prohibition, etc. As written in chapter 3 of Gittin: one who lends money, etc., even though death is common, as written, and in Sukkah 24a:`,
  "beur-hagra:4:_": `Seif 4 — a year, etc., for presumptive prohibition, and we follow the majority, as written in Gittin there: one who brings a get and left it, etc., and there in the Gemara that most sick people [recover] to life:`,
  "beur-hagra:5:_": `Seif 5 — grain, etc. As written in chapter Kol Sha'ah that it leavens while attached, and therefore in such a case:`,
  "beur-hagra:6:_": `Seif 6 — wheat, etc., presumptive prohibition, etc., as above, and as written in Gittin there: one who leaves produce, etc., if they were lost, etc., and the conclusion there in the Gemara from the outset of inspection, and Rashba there s.v. mial'el, etc.:`,
  "beur-hagra:7:_": `Seif 7 — an Israelite, etc., for it is like chametz upon which rubble fell, as written in chapter Kol Sha'ah, and as Rashi wrote in chapter 1 regarding one who makes his house a storehouse; and further that there is a double uncertainty perhaps they did not leaven, and if you say they leavened, perhaps they spoiled — therefore it is permitted to retain, as written in seif 2. Rashba:`,
  "beur-hagra:8:א": `Seif 8 — honey, etc., presumptive prohibition, etc., as above; and further that even if flour became mixed in it there is no prohibition, as the Rosh wrote on the Yerushalmi mentioned above R' Chananel, etc., it would be forged honey with flour, etc., and the concern is distant that perhaps water became mixed in the honey:`,
  "beur-hagra:8:ב": `However there are those who are stringent. That they are accustomed to mix flour into it for sweetness, and therefore the Yerushalmi said one must burn because of the honey, not like the Rosh, and as Tosafos wrote in chapter Kol Sha'ah and in Menachos on red vinegar because they leaven barley in water first and we establish presumptive prohibition in such a case because they profit from forgery, as written in Avodah Zarah 34b — that cellar of morayis, etc., lest because he sealed it the wine prices equalize, etc., and see there in Tosafos 32a s.v. mai'otzar. But if he did not see him drawing, etc., and so too here since they profit from it:`,
  "beur-hagra:8:ג": `And figs, etc., hanging, etc. — that figs are accustomed to mix flour into them for sweetness; and grapes are accustomed to dry them in an oven near bread or after they removed the bread from the oven; and in places where they do not mix flour and dry in the sun it is permitted — see in Tosafos Avodah Zarah there s.v. hai chala, etc., therefore, etc., since there are places, etc., and so is implied in the Gemara hai chala, etc., that they mix — meaning they are accustomed to mix — it implies only in places where they are accustomed:`,
  "beur-hagra:8:ד": `And therefore they were accustomed. Like grapes as above:`,
  "beur-hagra:8:ה": `And tsuker. That they mix flour into it:`,
  "beur-hagra:8:ו": `But on Yom Tov. However on other days, even those who do not mix, it is forbidden because of confusion:`,
  "beur-hagra:8:ז": `And the custom. That they forge with flour to establish its appearance, and so they place on se'or to restore its appearance:`,
  "beur-hagra:8:ח": `negeliach. For they soak it in barley water:`,
  "beur-hagra:8:ט": `However. For regarding rabbinic any-amount we do not establish presumptive prohibition b'dieved:`,
  "beur-hagra:9:א": `Seif 9 — cooked dish. In chapter Kol Sha'ah 40a — taught: one may not soak, etc., R' Chananel: they did not split open, etc., and Shmuel said, etc., and we conclude in chapter 1 of Avodah Zarah regarding Torah law we follow the stringent and regarding scribal law we follow the lenient — and so too here since mixtures of any amount is rabbinic:`,
  "beur-hagra:9:ב": `Or wheat. To exclude from Ra'em who forbids wheat per his words even if they did not split open at all since they have splitting, and so wrote Eliyah Rabbah there s.v. ve'yeish omrim, etc., and he brings proof from the latter chapter of Avodah Zarah 65b — but it is not so, for the conclusion there of Kol Sha'ah is that wheat is lighter than barley, and that case in Avodah Zarah is not relevant here; and further he brings proof from that cellar steeped in chaff and they did not require there split open, and the Ran wrote that is not proof since there they were split open — and see below seif 2; and also one should not read that wheat even if split open is permitted, for if so the guarding below is unnecessary. But in Tosefta chapter 3 it implies a distinction between wheat and barley, for it teaches there: one soaks wheat and barley in water and they leavened — forbidden; R' Yose says: one who soaks barley in water and they split open — forbidden, and in vinegar, etc., and it teaches it in the name of R' Yose from the latter part — and in vinegar, etc., for he stated it as written in the Gemara:`,
  "beur-hagra:9:ג": `The cooked dish is permitted. For in rabbinic law we follow leniency, as written in chapter 1 of Avodah Zarah, even though its essence is from Torah law as written in chapter 5 that we say it is different since it does not exist as in normal circumstances — even though its essence is from Torah — Ran:`,
  "beur-hagra:9:ד": `And the custom is not, etc. That they conduct like Mar Ukva and we are not expert in the measure fit for splitting. Semag and Mordechai. And it appears to me the reason for the custom as written in seif 2 this grain, etc., and the Rif holds that even in such a case we require split open, and in the incident of the cellar as the Ran wrote that there they were split open as above, and so in Tosefta one soaks barley, etc., as above:`,
  "biur-halacha:11:_": `And we say "here it was found," etc. — Chemed Moshe wrote: if it has not yet been shaped and rolled and a wheat kernel was found on top of the piece of dough in his hand — since we say "here it was found," etc., if so it has not yet given taste in this piece and it is permitted; and this is if it was found on top of the piece and even stuck to the dough; but if it was found inside the dough one must forbid the entire dough, for if we say it fell in this piece, since it has not yet been shaped on the piece it should be found on the piece and not inside it; rather it was certainly in the complete dough, and if so the entire dough is forbidden, and it is not applicable here to say "here it was found," etc.:`,
  "biur-halacha:12:א": `If they scalded, etc. — see in the Taz that he wrote that ordinary scalding is in a second vessel; and behold according to what the Mechaber wrote in Yoreh De'ah siman 68 that b'dieved in a second vessel it does not absorb — at first glance it must be that it deals with a first vessel; and if because of blood prohibition it deals that they salted with the feathers before scalding, as I cited in Mishna Berurah in the name of Peri Megadim; but it is possible that the Mechaber's opinion is like those who are stringent that the Rama brought above in siman 447 seif 3 in the gloss that even in a second vessel it is forbidden, and so I found in Peri Chadash who is uncertain about the Mechaber's opinion, and regarding essential law the Peri Chadash is stringent on Pesach regarding a second vessel — see there; and see above in siman 461 in Mishna Berurah seif 21:`,
  "biur-halacha:12:ב": `And if an embedded wheat kernel was found, etc. — and barley in water, see above in siman 447 in Magen Avraham seif 6 that it imparts taste for spoilage, and the Acharonim do not disagree there except regarding wheat — see there:`,
  "biur-halacha:12:ג": `If the mixtures became intermingled and there is great loss, etc. — it implies from this that a primary cooked dish may not be permitted even through great loss, and so is among the Acharonim; however in the Biur HaGra it appears that because of great loss alone one permits, even in hot food — then we rule like the first opinion in the Rama that permits in scalding; and it must be according to him that what the Rama mentions mixtures is because through mixtures there is commonly great loss; and if so the Rama who wrote a cooked dish of meat that remained in water — meaning in cold, and there he permits even if the wheat kernel was found inside while the meat was soaking in it — the main reason is also because of great loss; and if so if it happened that there was great loss through a primary cooked dish, it is also permitted in cold, for we rely on the first opinion in the Mechaber that permits in cold; and with this the difficulty of Magen Avraham above in siman 447 seif 9 can be resolved — there in the gloss it appears that in cold it is permitted and here he forbids in cold, for there it deals in a place of great loss — uncertain:`,
  "biur-halacha:13:_": `Into the pit during Pesach — see Mishna Berurah; and I did not cite what the Taz wrote that sometimes even b'dieved it is forbidden where they settled and remained, etc., for in Sefer Nehar Shalom he questioned his words, and also in Sefer Bigdei Yeshua he challenged this, that in a clear liquid this is not applicable, and his opinion there is that this matter did not come from the mouth of the Taz — see there:`,
  "biur-halacha:14:א": `Split wheat kernel — see in the Taz that he wrote that according to the Hagah above in seif 9, even if it did not split open but only softened, this entire seif applies, and so is the opinion of the rest of the Acharonim (Bach, Pri Megadim, Peri Chadash, Ma'amar Mordechai, the Gra, Chayyei Adam — unlike the Shulchan Gavoah who tends to say that in roasting and salting the Rama agrees that splitting open is required, and so is the opinion of Kaf HaChayyim regarding salting); only that the Rama relied here on what he wrote in seif 9. And behold in great loss, such as when he is poor and it is a large important piece, or prevention of Yom Tov joy — it appears from the Gra that one may be lenient and rely on the Mechaber's opinion that peeling suffices, and so is the opinion of Peri Chadash to peel deeply and discard all around it and it is permitted in eating; and behold all the other Acharonim did not mention for practical purposes this opinion; however if it softened and did not split open, perhaps one may rely on this; and it is possible that also the Gra's opinion to be lenient in great loss is specifically when it softened; and see in responsum of the Rama siman 128, it is proven there that even when softened they are accustomed to forbid that entire piece, and perhaps in great loss he too agrees to be lenient — uncertain. And know further that in uncertainty whether it softened one should not be stringent at all (Acharonim):`,
  "biur-halacha:14:ב": `But the essential ruling to forbid, etc. — behold above we wrote that for us even if it is not embedded, only softened, and the Acharonim wrote (Kaf HaChayyim siman 447 seif 6 and Shulchan Aruch HaRav) that specifically if we see the wheat kernel is moist; but if we see it did not become moist from the salt, such as that it is dry — even though it softened it does not forbid, for it does not release at all, for it is pure salted and impure bland; and similarly if chametz fell on salted meat, even on Pesach one may be lenient in a place of great loss as with other prohibitions:`,
  "biur-halacha:14:ג": `That entire piece and, etc. — see Mishna Berurah; and see Magen Avraham — he heard that a wheat kernel was found in a barrel of fish salted for Pesach, and the Gaon R' Binyamin commanded to throw that row into the river and the rest to sell to a gentile; and see in Chemed Moshe who wondered at this, and it would suffice to forbid only that fish on which the wheat kernel was found (and similarly wondered Machatzit HaShekel there); and he answered: perhaps it deals with a case where there is concern perhaps the wheat kernel touched the entire row; for otherwise we do not establish presumptive prohibition from piece to piece, and we say "here it was found, here it was" — end quote; and according to the words of the Taz that we wrote in Mishna Berurah, even in this manner one should not forbid the entire row:`,
  "biur-halacha:14:ד": `Through some peeling — see Mishna Berurah that this is when there is concern perhaps the wheat kernel touched them; and Olat Shabbat wrote: a piece where there is uncertainty whether it touched at all that piece in which the wheat kernel was found — even peeling is not required:`,
  "biur-halacha:2:_": `This grain that was submerged, etc. — behold in the Beit Yosef it is found that some divide between wheat and barley in this matter, and from the Mechaber's silence it appears that he holds that all types of grain have the same law, and so too the Taz; and therefore know that what the Rama wrote "and therefore one must be careful," etc., "or rain dripping on the wheat" — he mentioned the usual way of speaking because presumably one buys wheat for matzah; but the same applies to all types of grain. And know further that in the Beit Yosef it is found that some poskim hold that there is a distinction between much soaking and a little dripping; however the Mechaber was silent on this, and the Taz wrote that since the Mechaber cited the view of Rabbeinu Efrayim that therefore splitting open is not required because it rested at ease (and according to him one need not distinguish between little dripping and much soaking), it implies that even a little dripping forbids; and the Gra cited it and so I cited in the Mishna Berurah, and so I found also in Bach and the Pri Megadim cited it; however in Bach it appears that his opinion is that only l'chatchila one must be careful with a little dripping not to buy them to eat on Pesach, and so too is the opinion of the Peri Chadash on what the Rama wrote "that they should not come," etc., "for then even if they did not split open they are forbidden" — the Peri Chadash wrote and this is its language: meaning when abundant rains descended until the stack was soaked in water, etc.; but l'chatchila it is obvious one must be careful even with a little dripping — end of his words; and it appears from Beit Me'ir that b'dieved in a place of great loss one may rely on this:`,
  "biur-halacha:9:א": `If they actually split open — even a crack of any size (Peri Megadim):`,
  "biur-halacha:9:ב": `The entire cooked dish is forbidden — see Mishna Berurah what he wrote that it is forbidden in benefit, and this is from the words of the Acharonim; but in truth according to what the Mechaber ruled below in the next seif that it is permitted to sell to a gentile, etc., here too like him it is permitted to sell the cooked dish to a gentile, for it is not sold for more on account of the release of any amount absorbed from the wheat kernel; and it appears to me that here it deals when the wheat kernel is still inside it, for from the end of the Mechaber's words it appears at the beginning that he forbids the cooked dish even after removing the wheat kernel from it — uncertain:`,
  "biur-halacha:9:ג": `Even if they did not split open — see Mishna Berurah what he wrote in the name of Kaf HaChayyim; and behold although some Acharonim cited the words of the Taz for practical halachah, nevertheless it appears to me that one who is lenient like the words of Kaf HaChayyim and the Acharonim with him has not lost, after in truth most of the Rishonim and almost all of them agreed to the words of the Rif to permit the cooked dish even in eating, and the Peri Chadash also agreed thus — at least we permit in benefit; and especially where it was not close to splitting but only softened — it appears to me we can rely to be lenient, and we can also combine the opinion of the She'iltot and Razah and Rabbeinu Tam who always hold in sixty — end quote:`,
  "chatam-sofer:1:_": `In Magen Avraham beginning of the siman — proclamation Shulchan Gavoah chapter 5, etc. — see there page 62 letter 1 and see there the reason to distinguish, for regarding kosher meat proclamation in a place where they proclaim helps:`,
  "chatam-sofer:10:_": `(There seif 22 in Magen Avraham end of chapter 3). I would say: see Tosefet Yom Tov chapter 2 of Zavim mishnah 3, s.v. "Behold he," see there:`,
  "chatam-sofer:11:_": `(There in the Taz seif 13) The Tur wrote. I would say: see above in Magen Avraham seif 12 that he disagrees with the Taz on this; and see what is written there on the margin:`,
  "chatam-sofer:2:_": `(There seif 2) end quote, and uncertain, for if so why does he say, etc. — I would say that everywhere he holds as Magen Avraham wrote at the end of his words in the name of Mahari\`t that one does not make a double uncertainty by handiwork; therefore he holds one must combine with this the reasoning of the primary law 121 that one who makes his house a storehouse requires it; and this reasoning alone does not help, for behold even in one kezayit we are concerned in any case; this reasoning helps that one makes a double uncertainty by handiwork — end quote:`,
  "chatam-sofer:3:_": `(There in the Mechaber seif 3) Wheat that came. I would say: nevertheless for matzah of the commandment one should not take from them, as written above siman 453 on the margin — see there for its reason; and see Peri Chadash — proof for permission from the boat that sank in Chizisa, and in my humble opinion there is proof from Menachos page 69b:`,
  "chatam-sofer:4:_": `(There Mechaber seif 6) Wheat that was found. I would say: see responsa Orach Chayyim siman 136; and one who bought on Pesach wheat and split kernels were found in them, see responsa Chacham Tzvi Orach Chayyim siman 134 — one does not want an prohibition to acquire — see there:`,
  "chatam-sofer:5:_": `(There in the Mechaber seif 8) Honey of a gentile. I would say: regarding hatred of tobacco for the nose and there is concern they drip beer on it — one must examine what the Sha'ar HaMelech wrote in chapter 1 of forbidden foods, s.v. "However," etc. — see there, and uncertain; and see in Magen Avraham end of seif 40:`,
  "chatam-sofer:6:_": `(There in the Mechaber seif 8 in the gloss) And tsuker is forbidden to eat it. I would say: see responsa Orach Chayyim siman 135:`,
  "chatam-sofer:7:_": `(There in Magen Avraham seif 12) To check if there is, etc., and above siman 465; and see Yeshuot Yaakov siman 279 — three errors were found; and see responsa Chacham Tzvi siman 129 and siman 136:`,
  "chatam-sofer:8:_": `(There in Magen Avraham seif 13) Because of any amount. I would say: and in Sefer Avnei Nezer in siman 447 he rejected all the words of the Nehar Shalom in correct matters; and similarly in Peliti siman 92 seif 17 he brought that in any case one should combine the opinion of the Taz with the lenient ones; and so it appears in my humble opinion that for moist with moist the Taz too agrees that it is forbidden — two any-amounts, and a vessel that absorbed any amount is forbidden in any amount as well, as Avnei Nezer wrote; but a dry thing that absorbed any amount and fell into more than sixty — if there are sides to permit, one may combine the words of the Taz — examine well:`,
  "chatam-sofer:9:_": `(There in Magen Avraham seif 16) "Here it was found, here it was," etc. — and see Tosefet Yom Tov and Tosafot Chadashot end of chapter 9 of Taharot, s.v. "And it was found in its filth," and uncertain there; and see further what Tosafot Chadashot wrote in mishnah 7 chapter 10 of tractate Taharot:`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:א": `Or that fell upon them. And this is the language of the Ran in chapter Kol Sha'ah: even if they split open such that the leavening is recognizable in them, nevertheless it is forbidden to sell to a gentile, for we are concerned lest he grind them and afterward sell to an Israelite — end of his words. However the Tur wrote and the chametz is not recognizable in them — it implies at first glance that if the chametz is recognizable in them we are not concerned at all, and it is difficult, for there is still the concern of the Ran. And similarly sensed this difficulty on the Tur in Olat Shabbat — see there — and Magen Avraham answered and this is its language: we are not concerned lest he grind them and sell them to an Israelite since one does not buy flour from the market except in a time of distress as written in siman 453, unlike Olat Shabbat — end of his words. But in truth he did not correct anything with this answer, for that one does not buy except in a time of distress means specifically for matzah of the commandment as written above siman 453 note 15, and even for us who are accustomed to perform guarding for all the matzos — nevertheless when an Israelite buys that flour before Pesach and retains it in his house he transgresses "it shall not be seen," since the chametz was recognizable in them — and this is plainly the opinion of the Ran. Therefore it appears to me the main intent of the Tur in what he wrote and the chametz is not recognizable is for amplification: even though the chametz is not recognizable and only a general concern of leavening is known in it (as the Tur wrote explicitly), nevertheless we are concerned for mishap in the matter of selling, and all the more so when chametz is known and recognizable with certainty we are concerned for mishap — and it is clear:`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:ב": `Forbidden to retain it. If he transgressed and retained it and sowed it in the ground and growths sprouted, the growths are permitted as written above siman 453:`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:ג": `And informs. So is the language of the Rambam, and the Tur challenged him, for since our Talmud's version is "we sell it keva keva to an Israelite" so that it finishes before Pesach, and since it is permitted only a small amount — if he informs, even much is also permitted — see in Beit Yosef and other Acharonim how they reconcile the words of the Rambam and the Tur, and through this they raised several laws; and in truth this question was already asked before the Rashba in responsum siman 254, and he answered that it deals with informing and there is no difficulty even for much as well, since an Israelite does not buy much since it is forbidden to retain it on Pesach (the opposite of my purpose — why should he be forced to sell to a gentile little by little near Pesach, for a gentile is found at all times in every place who will buy from him and return to him after Pesach as stated above siman 448 — see there), and as we say in Nedarim that an Israelite does not benefit from him — he buys for less and sells for more — only they do not listen to him; the same here — end of his words. It is found that the words of the Rambam are correct without doubt, and one need not corrupt the versions before us as the Maggid Mishneh wrote, and what astonished Beit Yosef and other Acharonim that escaped them the words of the Rashba; and so is the essence that when he informs, even much is permitted to sell to an Israelite, unlike the implication of some Acharonim; and even little by little, if he sells to an Israelite he must inform — and so agreed the Acharonim:`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:ד": `So that it finishes before Pesach. Explanation: even if you say that also that small amount he will sell to an Israelite, nevertheless that small amount the Israelite will eat before Pesach; and according to this it implies b'eyna on the fifth hour in a matter where it is impossible that it finish before Pesach it is forbidden to sell to a gentile even little by little from concern lest he sell to an Israelite — and so is the implication of the language of the Talmud and Rambam and other poskim, even according to the Maggid Mishneh per his version, according to the Rambam in our Talmud, and so agreed Magen Avraham, unlike many, and so is the implication of the Mechaber here. Nevertheless it appears to me that if it leavened for an Israelite — much grain b'eyna and it is a matter where he cannot sell to a gentile that it finish before Pesach and there is great loss — it is permitted to sell to a gentile who knows it and recognizes him that he will not touch it at all but guard it for him until after Pesach, and he may say to him until you take, etc., as explained above siman 448 seif 3 and 4 — then there is nothing to be concerned for. And what Magen Avraham wrote that selling little by little is forbidden in other prohibitions only here regarding chametz, since there are many uncertainties they were lenient — his words are correct, and he aligned with this what is also written in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 46 note 34, unlike Shach and Taz there, also in Peri Chadash siman 84 note 17 he aligned with my opinion — see there:`,
  "chok-yaakov:1:ה": `And he sells it to an Israelite. And if the gentile said to the Israelite even a word after he sold him wheat that these wheats are for sweets — it is like what is written in Yoreh De'ah siman 216 that a gentile is not believed in what he said after he sold them and they left his possession; however if he is believed, etc., it is forbidden — end quote — Magen Avraham. It implies before he sold them, since it is in his possession he is believed — and this is not so, for since one can say he intended to enhance his merchandise he is not believed as stated in Yevamos page 22, and so in Yoreh De'ah siman 116, and Shach and Taz, and so I raised in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 59 note 7 — see there; and it is possible this is not enhancement of the merchandise, for on the contrary greater enhancement is to wheat if they are not yet for sweets and are clean in themselves — therefore he is believed if it is in his possession — uncertain:`,
  "chok-yaakov:10:א": `Permitted to sell it to a gentile. So Bach wrote in the name of Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah who wrote thus in the name of Mahari of Paris, and so in Maharil in the name of the Rosh; however Mordechai himself challenged this and forbids, and so in Agudah, and like the Rav's ruling in the gloss, and see above siman 467 notes 2 and 3 — all this specifically with a split wheat kernel as written in note 26:`,
  "chok-yaakov:10:ב": `And if it was found on the eighth day. It implies even if the mixture occurred during Pesach (and even if he transgressed and retained intentionally as explained above siman 467) they are lenient in a mixture of any amount on the last day which is rabbinic to retain l'chatchila, and specifically if found on the eighth day, but if found during Pesach one must burn and destroy everything on chol ha-mo'ed, and if found on the seventh of Pesach see above beginning siman 466 how one should conduct — see there the implication of the Acharonim as written here:`,
  "chok-yaakov:10:ג": `Permitted to retain it. Meaning in a mixture of any amount remove and it is permitted; but in something that is complete chametz forbidden d'oraisa, even on the last Yom Tov it is forbidden in any case rabbinically — so agreed the Acharonim, and it is plain:`,
  "chok-yaakov:11:א": `And so they are accustomed during Pesach. Levush expanded to challenge the Rav on this, and his opinion is to permit in dough in every matter that is not taste imparted in cold, and Magen Avraham wrote in note 19 and this is its language: and I found written that the Gaon Maharal of Prague ruled to permit, but Maharash of Austria wrote to Rosh of Lublin to forbid, and so is the custom of most of the world, and in Krakow it appears to me it depends on custom — end quote. But in truth even though Levush reconciles the Tur's words not to forbid in cold, behold Mordechai forbids in meat salting even if a wheat kernel was found after salting — see there; and it is possible he forbade because there were cracks as written siman 465, and so Bach wrote here in the name of the Geonim; therefore he wrote regarding meat that has cracks it is forbidden by law in cold, and similarly in uncertainty whether it was salted from above it is also forbidden by law — end quote Magen Avraham. And still the obligation of explanation is not discharged, for since he forbids in meat because there are cracks, from this we say the wheat kernel imparts taste in any amount in cold and enters the cracks in the meat — if so all the more one should forbid in cold water or in dough more than cracks in meat; and this distinction is not applicable except regarding another solid lump like meat that has no cracks and is not a soft penetrating thing. And Taz concludes that even though for other cold one may permit like Levush, nevertheless in dough through handling in dough the wheat kernel is crushed and leavens and forbids the entire dough during Pesach if it softened (and similarly in chametz bread one should be stringent even in cold lest one be concerned for crumbs), and so the Rav's relative raised in his book — thus appears the conclusion of Magen Avraham and Bach and Maharshal. Nevertheless it appears to me according to what is written above note 18 that if the wheat kernel is not soaked one may permit in benefit b'eyna — if so all the more here in cold, for the opinion of many poskim is to permit in every matter; and it is possible the Rav's words and so they are accustomed also refer only to a soaked wheat kernel, as the Mechaber mentioned — if not he should have glossed here too that even if it is not split like in seif 9; rather it must be that since it is a cold thing his intent is not to be stringent at all if it is not soaked, since there is another side to be lenient; however Bach and other Acharonim who wrote one forbids only if the wheat kernel softened — from this they hold it is forbidden here even if it did not actually split open; and it is possible the Rav's gloss is also thus, and he did not gloss here because he relies on what he glossed in seif 9 — the same for all of them; nevertheless this is only to forbid in eating and not in benefit as written above note 26, and one who is lenient even in eating has not lost, for it is possible even if split one may be lenient in cold b'eyna, as the Rav wrote in the gloss and so they are accustomed — meaning one may be lenient b'eyna or in a time of distress, and so appears to me the essence:`,
  "chok-yaakov:11:ב": `Here it was found and here it was. Magen Avraham wrote: if the wheat kernel is full of water and moist through and through, if so it appears it was in water, and in dough one should forbid the entire dough; nevertheless if there is room to attribute that it was swallowed from elsewhere from water and fell into the matzah, everything is permitted according to the matter, for we attribute in common cases whether to leniency or stringency, and it is not called an uncertainty at all — end quote, and see in my pamphlet of uncertainties; and he further wrote in Magen Avraham: that which we say here it was found is because we do not establish presumptive prohibition specifically from place to place or from piece to piece, but from time to time in one piece we establish presumptive prohibition (and so Peri Chadash in Yoreh De'ah siman 91 note 18), such as if he has dough in hand and cut from it and a piece of dough remained in his hand and a wheat kernel was found on it — all are forbidden, for we say perhaps it was previously (and also one cannot say it is double uncertainty that perhaps it did not leaven because it is uncertainty of lack of knowledge, and so agreed Taz in note 14) — see in responsum Mishna Berurah siman 69 and responsum Tzemach Tzedek siman 43. Responsum Maharam of Lublin siman 78 — incident: one took cabbage from barrels in a bowl and brought it elsewhere and a wheat kernel was found on it, and he permitted the cabbage in the barrels, for we say here it was found, here it was; but if afterward a wheat kernel was found in the barrels, even the cabbage in the bowl is forbidden, for we say perhaps the wheat kernel was previously in the barrels — end quote:`,
  "chok-yaakov:12:א": `If they scalded the chicken. Rokeach siman 291 and so in Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah and this is its language: an incident occurred that they scalded a chicken in its mouth with its feathers in boiling water in a chametz second vessel, and they forbade eating it as we say in chapter Dam Chatat one pours boiling into it — end quote; therefore Taz wrote here that even in a second vessel it is forbidden, and this is also the Rav's opinion above siman 466 seif 3 in the gloss where he wrote two views regarding a second vessel on Pesach — see there. However in my humble opinion there is no dispute at all, for from that he brought proof from one pours boiling into boiling it implies it deals by law of pouring, and nevertheless it is called a second vessel regarding the bowl, as also written above siman 461 note 8 and so is the implication and meaning of the great poskim in their language — see there, and so is compelled, for from their words it implies we are not stringent because of Pesach stringency, for they learned from chapter Dam Chatat and Rokeach siman 431 ruled regarding other prohibitions to permit in a second vessel — rather it certainly deals by law of pouring; nevertheless for practical halachah one should be stringent as Rama decided above siman 467 that one should be stringent if the hand recoils in chametz, for the opinion of some poskim is to forbid even in cold; also I wrote above siman 461 note 8 that the opinion of some poskim is to forbid in a second vessel; also the words of Rokeach and Mordechai can be reconciled with strain that it deals with a second vessel — therefore one may not be lenient at all as stated:`,
  "chok-yaakov:12:ב": `Soaked wheat kernel. It is possible since the Rav did not gloss here as below seif 9 to forbid even if not soaked, because here since there is another uncertainty that perhaps afterward it fell we are not so stringent; however Taz wrote the Rav follows his view to forbid even if not soaked, only whatever softened — permitted to sell — end quote, and see above notes 18 and 22 — see there:`,
  "chok-yaakov:12:ג": `The chicken is forbidden. Agreement of Rishonim and Acharonim that we do not say regarding a chicken here it was found here it was, and now it fell; and Magen Avraham wrote to distinguish that we do not say it was born now where there is no presumption of permission previously as stated in chapter 1 of Chullin and Niddah, and so Taz — on the contrary all impurities as at the time they are found (and see responsum Mishna Berurah and Tzemach Tzedek that I hinted above note 32); and even for Beit Shammai in chapter 1 of Niddah one may be stringent here since there are two adverse factors — wheats in the body and wheats that fell from outside, as the Talmud answers there page 3a regarding a rodent found in an alley — see there, and see Rambam chapter 15 and chapter 18 of Laws of Impurity, and see in my book Torat HaShelamim:`,
  "chok-yaakov:12:ד": `And there is one who forbids. See above note 31:`,
  "chok-yaakov:12:ה": `For we say perhaps afterward it fell there. Bach wrote and Rivash wrote in the name of Rashba: if a wheat kernel was found in a vessel in which they rinsed the chicken, we say here it was found here it was as with blemishes, and we do not say it came from the chicken — and so he ruled in practical halachah — end quote; Magen Avraham challenged this from Rashba's own words that he ruled if a wheat kernel was found in matzah they forbid the dough as written in note 11, and we do not say it fell now, and he expanded further to challenge this — many difficulties, and concludes it deals when they salted the chicken and afterward rinsed in a cold vessel, and afterward a wheat kernel was found in the vessel, and there is concern it was in the chicken and was salted with it — therefore we are not concerned for this since the wheat kernel was found in the vessel we do not say it was in the chicken because we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place; but in matzah which is from time to time as written — there is no dispute at all, unlike Rama's opinion — such is the content of his words there; even though his words at first glance are correct and he follows his view that from time to time aliba di-chol everyone establishes presumptive prohibition as he wrote in note 32; however in what he concluded that in matzah it is from time to time as written, and his intent to explain thus Rashba's opinion — this is astonishing, for on the contrary in matzah it is from place to place if not that it deals that dough remained in his hand as written in note 32 in his name, and this is against Rashba's language and implication, and the Rav's relative wrote in the name of Darkei Moshe many supports for this view — see there:`,
  "chok-yaakov:12:ו": `From the force of double uncertainty. See above note 10:`,
  "chok-yaakov:12:ז": `Or in a whole wheat kernel. Tur wrote here, and the Rosh agreed that we do not derive prohibition from impurity, and it is like digested; and in Yoreh De'ah end of siman 83 he wrote: pure that swallowed an impure fish is forbidden, for it is not considered digested since it is whole — from this Tur holds that when it is not whole he holds it is like digested; and that which he wrote here we do not derive prohibition from impurity that it is like digested is specifically when it is whole before us, unlike when it is broken we see it was digested before us — for presumably when the chicken ate it the wheat was whole, as Agudah chapter 1 of Bechoros, and so Shach explained Yoreh De'ah there the Tur's words (unlike some Acharonim there who explained the Tur's words per R' Meir of Pontise) — and this explanation is correct and essential, and so is clear from Tosafos' rulings chapter 1 of Bechoros, even though they seized in plainness that we do not learn prohibition from impurity for digestion — nevertheless Tosafos wrote there briefly that one forbids only when a whole wheat kernel is found, and it is found he ruled well here to be lenient through digestion when the wheat kernel is not whole — see there in Peri Chadash:`,
  "chok-yaakov:12:ח": `And chickens were salted there. Magen Avraham wrote: not specifically chickens but the same applies to meat, for here it deals with a whole wheat kernel where there is no digestion — end quote. And in Olat Shabbat he wrote: for amplification he cited chickens even though wheat kernels are commonly found in them — nevertheless no uncertainty emerges and we say perhaps afterward it fell; there is also another uncertainty that perhaps he holds like those who say it is not taste imparted at all in cold, which is like double uncertainty; Bach is stringent in these laws, and challenged the Rav — and the essence is like the Rav's words and so agreed the Rav's relative in his book and Taz in particular if the wheat kernel is not split open — plainly one should not be stringent as written above note 31, and without softening the wheat kernel one may be lenient in every matter, and so agreed Bach and the Acharonim:`,
  "chok-yaakov:12:ט": `In uncertainties of rabbinic prohibition. See Bach what he wrote on this, and see what is written nearby note 40; and Nekudot HaKesef wrote chapter Orlah and he is Shach in Yoreh De'ah siman 111 regarding two pots one of permission and one of prohibition, and before him two pieces one of permission and one of prohibition and this fell into that — if the prohibition is rabbinic it is permitted, for we say I say permission fell into permission, even if the permission in the pot is not majority — on this Nekudot HaKesef wrote: it is possible that for stringent rabbinic any-amount prohibitions we do not say I say, even in rabbinic prohibitions in less than majority — end quote; and Peri Chadash there challenged at the beginning of siman 111 without proof and wrote: since rabbinic any-amount prohibition we follow leniency in every uncertainty like all rabbinic uncertainty, and his words are not compelled to be lenient against Nekudot HaKesef's opinion, especially since we find many stringencies they were stringent on Pesach even in mixtures of any amount even in uncertainty; and Magen Avraham wrote: one who took into his mouth a piece of matzah and a piece of chicken and ate both together — one may permit the chicken or meat, for we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place, and we say it fell now in cold; and similarly the matzah one may permit; but if the meat rests in a bowl and he ate the meat from it, it is forbidden, for from time to time we establish presumptive prohibition, for perhaps it was in the bowl when he poured the meat into it (nevertheless according to majority it is recognizable whether the wheat kernel was cooked or baked with it or not) — however if they shook the bowl from place to place when it cooled we attribute to leniency, for we do not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place — end quote:`,
  "chok-yaakov:12:י": `That was rinsed in water. Magen Avraham wrote: explanation — even though the wheat kernel was found inside while the meat was still soaking, nevertheless it is a rabbinic uncertainty, for there are those who permit in cold, and his words are not compelled to explain the Rav's opinion — see note 38; or scalded — meaning and afterward the wheat kernel was found, for one can say it fell now. And that which Rama ruled above seif 2 that double uncertainty does not help — that is for eating the wheats themselves, but the mixtures are permitted. Bach wrote in one uncertainty even to retain it is forbidden, even b'eyna, and in the end permitted to retain, and all the more permitted to sell them to a gentile and not for eating, for that uncertainty that perhaps it was digested is not a full uncertainty, for one can say digestion does not help in prohibition as written; and specifically complete chametz such as bread or dough, but a wheat kernel found in water permits the cooked dish cooked in it beforehand, for it is double uncertainty that perhaps it fell now, and if you say it fell before, perhaps it did not soften until now, and without softening there is no prohibition at all even according to our custom — end quote Magen Avraham:`,
  "chok-yaakov:13:א": `Day-old. But if it is not day-old, even the hag'elah water itself is permitted on Pesach, since they hag'eled before Pesach and they are not day-old as explained above siman 461, and even fat that one melted before Pesach in a chametz vessel that is not day-old is permitted on Pesach as ruled in siman 467 seif 5 in the gloss, even though the Rav decided above that remove is forbidden on Pesach — all this is plain, and Bach toiled in vain on this — see there:`,
  "chok-yaakov:13:ב": `During Pesach. Bach wrote that even if they became mixed before Pesach in a matter nullified in sixty (even though it is also taste imparted by taste imparted of permission) nevertheless one may not use that water l'chatchila since one can easily find other water easily, and it is not for a cooked dish which is b'dieved, and if we forbid eating it on Pesach — end quote; Magen Avraham did not cite his language accurately; Taz wrote that sometimes even b'dieved it is forbidden in such a case if the sour waters remained above and did not mix, for they settled and stood and are as if in their own form unless he drew from it beforehand several times that through this it mixes — end quote. In my humble opinion one should not forbid b'dieved even though it was not nullified, nevertheless since it occurred before Pesach it is taste imparted by taste imparted of permission, and one should not forbid b'dieved — see siman 467 in the gloss and what is written there and siman 461 and 462 — see there:`,
  "chok-yaakov:13:ג": `Because they became mixed. They become one lump — if so even for those who permit in cold here it is forbidden since the body of leavening became mixed; and if a wheat kernel or other chametz is found in this pit, it depends on the dispute the Mechaber wrote above seif 12 whether he forbids in cold during Pesach — see above note 31; and see Taz, Beit Yosef, Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah, Rokeach siman 286, and Shulchan Lechem siman 63 who forbid if chametz is found in a well — and it appears it deals in a matter where there is concern for crumbs as written above; and so in responsum Rashba siman 176 — see there; and Bach wrote in this end in the name of Maharil that one should be careful for one who used on Pesach wells of gentiles or other wells where they are not careful all year that one should strain the water in a clean garment each time he draws — end quote; and see above siman 467 from the law of straining regarding vinegar and wine, and above siman 455 from the law of drawing water:`,
  "chok-yaakov:14:א": `Rinsing suffices. In general in cold without gravy, all agree it does not forbid — end quote Magen Avraham; even though in a chicken it is impossible without a little moisture, unlike in cold it does not absorb according to everyone except through a little salting or through handling in dough and such; Responsum Tzemach Tzedek: question — if a little beer remained in a vessel and he drew water into it and rinsed other meat after salting on Pesach — it is close to absorption and has the law of a wheat kernel salted with the chicken for each opinion in this seif. I already challenged him in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 6 note 23, for water nullifies the power of salt and it has only the law of cold and is not forbidden through rinsing alone, even if there are cracks, for the Rav ruled above siman 465 to forbid in goose — specifically there where he rubs well in its crop or scalds inside as written above note 31 in the name of Mordechai, and see above siman 465 note 6 — unlike mere rinsing (and with this one can also explain what Bach challenged here on the Rosh — see there); and mixture of any amount — nevertheless because of Pesach stringency I raised there one should peel a little of the meat he rinsed, and it is a stringency in itself:`,
  "chok-yaakov:14:ב": `After salting. Magen Avraham wrote: explanation — while still in its salt; but if found after it was salted and rinsed, we say it fell now, for if you say it was on it beforehand it would have fallen during rinsing when he rinsed, and specifically when found on the chicken, but if found inside the chicken it was certainly previously — see Niddah page 3 regarding a box that has rims — end quote; and see siman 467 note 6 where I wrote pure salted and impure bland is also permitted here, and it deals when the wheat kernel was also salted with the chicken — see there; and I saw meticulous people are careful on Pesach before scalding and salting to open the chicken well or cut it in two and check well that no wheat kernel is found inside, and Knesset HaGedolah wrote one who does so is zealous and rewarded — end quote:`,
  "chok-yaakov:14:ג": `And to permit the rest through peeling. And if he transgressed and cooked without peeling, see siman 467 note 9; and in Magen Avraham he wrote here various distinctions in this law, and the essence as written there; and regarding the essential law many Acharonim in their responsa, for some ruled to burn everything and some Acharonim permit the rest of the pieces entirely, and so the Rav himself ruled in responsum siman 108; and the essence appears to me like Taz's conclusion and this is its language: and in my humble opinion for practical conduct one should act thus: if a wheat kernel is found on salted meat dry without gravy, one may permit retaining everything, only where it was found one peels, and the rest may be retained until after Pesach; but if the wheat kernel is found in the gravy, then it is considered like cooked, for in a slight measure it is like cooked in gravy — then everything resting in the gravy should be forbidden, but what rests above the gravy one may retain until after Pesach or do as Rama wrote at the end to sell to a gentile what is not in the gravy — end quote; and his words appear correct, and all the more if the wheat kernel is not split open one should not be stringent in salting at all as written above note 26; and it is possible the Rav's gloss too is only stringent with a split wheat kernel that the Mechaber mentioned, even though Taz follows his view that it deals even if not split open — see notes 26 and 31, and see responsum Maharam of Lublin siman 91 where his opinion also appears to be lenient regarding salting even during Pesach — see there:`,
  "chok-yaakov:15:א": `Split wheat kernel. Knesset HaGedolah wrote: since the Rav did not gloss here too even if not split open, it appears he holds if not split open one forbids only in roasting as much as removal — end quote; however the opinion of other Acharonim is not so — see above note 26 and Machatzit HaShekel:`,
  "chok-yaakov:15:ב": `Place of bubbling. It deals with a thin chicken, for if not it bubbles and the whole thing is forbidden as explained above siman 467 — see there:`,
  "chok-yaakov:15:ג": `According to how they turn the spit. See in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 28 note 2 what I challenged on the separation, and I wrote specifically for rabbinic any-amount prohibition we are concerned for this, unlike other prohibitions — see there; and I saw Magen Avraham also aligned with this distinction literally as written there:`,
  "chok-yaakov:15:ד": `When they turn the spit. The Rav's relative wrote in his book: if the spit is not turned, it appears from Rama's opinion that the law of roasting is like the law of salting — end quote:`,
  "chok-yaakov:15:ה": `Touching one another. Magen Avraham wrote: if the chicken is fatty, even if they do not touch, it forbids everything — end quote; nevertheless when not split open it appears to me one should not be stringent as written above:`,
  "chok-yaakov:16:_": `The bird is permitted. And even peeling is not needed, for the removal is in the place of peeling; Bach ruled to forbid the whole thing, and the Rav's relative decides to forbid in eating and permit in benefit, and other Acharonim agree to permit entirely in scalding:`,
  "chok-yaakov:2:א": `This grain that was submerged in a river. And whether it is permitted to nullify this grain in majority or in sixty before Pesach, since it is still a time of permission — the Acharonim expanded on this, and see above siman 442 note 18 and siman 467 note 15 what emerges from their words for practical halachah:`,
  "chok-yaakov:2:ב": `For since it rested at ease they come to leavening. So wrote the Rosh and Tur in the name of R' Efrayim, and according to what the Tur and Rosh ruled regarding all wheat for sweets — even though it did not rest at ease, for eating a wheat kernel they require it — even if they did not actually split open but are close to splitting, they are also forbidden, and as I wrote — if so, we need here the reason that it rested at ease, even if the wheat kernel did not soften, it is also forbidden; but for those poskim who ruled like Shmuel that we require actual split open while not for sweets — if so, perhaps where it rested at ease it is only stringent on one side and we require at least close to splitting, and otherwise they are permitted. And the Mechaber holds that for eating the wheat kernel in its own form it is an uncertainty of Torah law and to be stringent as explained in seif 9 — therefore he ruled here where it rested at ease, even if it did not soften and is not close to splitting, it is also forbidden like the Tur and Rosh. And this is why the Mechaber was precise and wrote here even though it did not split open it is forbidden anonymously, and did not write as below in seif 9 even if they did not actually split open — rather to show that even though it is not close to splitting, they are also forbidden here for eating the wheat kernel in its own form; but in what is found inside a cooked dish the Mechaber ruled below seif 9 that if they did not actually split open, even though they are close to splitting, it is also permitted, even though it rested at ease inside the cooked dish — nevertheless one can say since the cooked dish is boiling it is like absorbed and does not leaven according to the Tur siman 461 and 467 — see there — only that there is concern it was in water before boiling, therefore we are not so stringent, and see above siman 454 and see there, and this is the essence that in a cooked dish which is only a mixture of any amount rabbinic we are not so stringent as Magen Avraham wrote in note 12 — see there; and according to what the Rambam wrote in chapter 5 of Laws of Chametz it appears to me the reason is simple: since grain that was mixed or cooked and did not actually split open is not Torah chametz, and also any amount rabbinic we do not forbid two rabbinic laws — and similarly explained Hagahot Maimoni there the words of the Rambam; and in this way one can explain the words of the Rif to the Rambam without doubt, unlike Beit Yosef who strains greatly in the intent of the Rambam's words here; according to this reason this grain mixed species in species even in less than sixty is only rabbinic prohibition, and one may also be lenient according to the Mechaber, and I expanded on all this to reconcile the poskim and the Mechaber with correct reasons — but for practical halachah Rama already concludes in seif 9 in the gloss that they are accustomed to forbid in every matter — see there. And it is clear from the Mechaber's words in seif 1 and this seif that even if they did not split open it is forbidden to sell to a gentile before Pesach unless he sells little by little, unlike that which is in Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah who permits through double uncertainty — see Shach Yoreh De'ah end of siman 57:`,
  "chok-yaakov:2:ג": `They come to leavening. And if it has not yet waited the measure of a mil, as Magen Avraham wrote — even if it occurred on Pesach it is permitted to sell it to a gentile before it leavens:`,
  "chok-yaakov:2:ד": `But not to eat them on Pesach it is forbidden. Even if some of them were eaten before Pesach, one may not permit eating the rest as Bach concludes here, unlike Ravyah who specifically dry with dry and one of them was eaten, or in three mixtures one may permit eating two as explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 110 — unlike moist with moist there is no permission in this, and so Magen Avraham; (and see above siman 453 where it was explained that flour in flour is called moist with moist) and so I also raised in my pamphlet of uncertainties note 19, unlike Shach — see there:`,
  "chok-yaakov:2:ה": `And a double uncertainty does not help. In truth in this seif and in several places it is explained that we rely on double uncertainty even in eating — see Taz and Shach who wrote the reason: since according to Mar Ukva even if they did not split open it is forbidden, it is only a double uncertainty of dispute and not in the reality of the matter — we do not lenient so much in such an uncertainty; and see in my pamphlet of uncertainties newly 2 note 2 where it was explained that on the contrary, uncertainty of dispute is better than uncertainty of the act itself; and also one cannot say that therefore the Rav was stringent in such an uncertainty because all are one uncertainty whether these wheats leavened from the water or not — I already rejected this reasoning too in pamphlet of uncertainties siman 120, and see there what the Rav wrote many reasons on this, and the essence appears to me like the conclusion of the Taz that here for this reason they were stringent because there is no loss in retaining it — but in truth in a place of loss or prevention of Yom Tov joy and the like one may permit even in eating:`,
  "chok-yaakov:3:א": `Dry and hard. However if water came upon them one should forbid the wheat kernel itself even though it did not change at all, as written in note 7, and even in cordovan wheat as stated in Pesachim in the case of Rav Gidal — see there in Rashi:`,
  "chok-yaakov:3:ב": `And their appearance did not change. But if their appearance changed or they are moist, it is forbidden even to retain them — Tur in the name of BeHag:`,
  "chok-yaakov:4:_": `We are not concerned for ordinary wheat. And Knesset HaGedolah wrote in practical halachah that he permitted buying wheat for Pesach even though they were stored in pits, all that we did not see in them a change in appearance and they are not moist — see there where he expanded, and if there are among them wheats that sprouted — see above siman 453:`,
  "chok-yaakov:6:_": `The upper part of the stack and its lower part. Rokeach wrote in siman 263 and this is its language: and if a little dew or water and snow on his grain, he scrapes those wheat kernels and takes what is beneath them as stated in chapter Kol Sha'ah in Yerushalmi — end of his words:`,
  "chok-yaakov:7:_": `Split wheat. But if they are not split open one should not be stringent here at all, for even split open there is no complete leavening here, only spoilage and loss, as the Rashba wrote in responsum, and see above siman 476 and what is written, and siman 466 from the law of sweat of heat:`,
  "chok-yaakov:8:א": `We do not establish prohibition on it to say it is forged. So is the language of the Tur, and it implies that if we know flour is mixed in it we must burn as stated explicitly in Yerushalmi end of chapter Kol Sha'ah, and one may not rely on the permission the Rosh wrote in the name of R' Yonah, and this is its language: and if we come to forbid the honey because of concern for mixture, that honey is forbidden all year, for they put nevelah meat into it and it transforms into honey — and R' Yonah wrote that even if they put nevelah meat into it, since it dissolved and became honey it is permitted, for we follow the present — end of his words. From this, according to his words, even if we know with certainty flour is mixed in it one may permit it; nevertheless one may not rely on this permission against the Yerushalmi who are stringent against R' Yonah's opinion, for the Rosh himself in chapter Keitzad Mevarchin doubts this view of R' Yonah, and also in Knesset HaGedolah he wrote one may not rely on this permission lest it returns and revives during Pesach — see there where he expanded; nevertheless it appears to me all this is if we know with certainty flour was mixed into it — but from the ordinary, even though they are accustomed to forbid eating them in their own form, nevertheless if it became mixed in a cooked dish one may permit b'dieved even in less than sixty, or even if we know flour was mixed into it it is permitted to retain in his house, or if it became mixed in a cooked dish if there is great loss or prevention of Yom Tov joy, even in less than sixty one who relies on R' Yonah's words has not lost, and as also written in Ma'adanei Melech chapter Keitzad Mevarchin page 53b to uphold R' Yonah's words — see there, and so is implied in Knesset HaGedolah itself; and even though in Darkei Moshe it implies one should forbid the cooked dish b'dieved if there are not sixty against the honey, and Magen Avraham agreed with him in note 11 — it appears because he left R' Yonah's words in uncertainty, and see in his words siman 456 note 3, and the main difficulty is from that which we say in Bechoros page 16 that milk of an animal is a novelty, since blood congealed and became milk it should be forbidden — we learn we follow the initial state, unlike R' Yonah's opinion, etc. — and if because of this it does not apply, and from honey there is no difficulty, for there it is different with milk, for one could say there should be no permission for any milk since all milk was formed from the prohibition of blood in the initial prohibition, even though it changed in appearance it did not change to a permitted matter — therefore they say it is a novelty that the Torah innovated. Unlike honey where the honey itself is permitted — if so, if they placed a forbidden thing in it that returns and transforms to make honey, we follow the present when it became a permitted thing which is honey — and why from Torah should we forbid it like other honey, since its taste and appearance are equal and it changed to a permitted thing — and it is clear. It is found there is no contradiction at all from that case of milk to R' Yonah's words regarding honey; also regarding musk (meaning what they call in foreign language pezum) that he wrote there R' Yonah — one can strain and distinguish similarly, but from there in any case there is room for doubt as Magen Avraham himself in siman 456, and from honey he left in uncertainty there is room for doubt, and as written — examine:`,
  "chok-yaakov:8:ב": `Only that which they bring in barrels. The implication of the Rav's words is that even to eat it thus in its own form it is permitted in such a case, and so the Taz compelled from the Rav's language, unlike Magen Avraham who wrote the Rav's opinion is like some Acharonim that even honey brought in barrels is also forbidden to eat thus in its own form, only to make a drink from it before Pesach they permitted — and this does not fit well; and for practical halachah the Rav's relative concludes in his book that this law changes according to places and countries, and so I heard that honey of the land of Piham which is good and select and has no concern for forgery — but what is brought from other places such as the land of the Ger and Bruna or from Poland they are accustomed to forbid it except that which is brought in barrels while still with the comb on it — each place has its custom and its practice, and this is also the Rav's intent in what he wrote and the custom in these countries, and in Knesset HaGedolah he expanded greatly and was stringent regarding honey because he investigated in countries that they mix much flour and water into it until there are not sixty against the flour they mix into it — see note 16; and he also wrote there it became clear to him with certainty that when they extract honey from the comb it is impossible that they do not put a little water into it, and therefore one may not knead with it at all, for it is fruit juice with water — end of his words, and all this follows his view and other Acharonim as above siman 462; however according to what is written there that with a little water there is no concern for a house of concern if he performed guarding — yet we are not at all accustomed to rich matzah as explained several times:`,
  "chok-yaakov:8:ג": `And dried grapes. Magen Avraham wrote in the name of Maharil: if he wishes to retain the opening of the sambur he can retain with our grapes — end of his words:`,
  "chok-yaakov:8:ד": `Any dried fruits. But they are accustomed to make from them a drink — meaning they pour cold water on them and soak them until a drink is made, and so Magen Avraham testified that such is the custom in the community of Kalish; but one may not cook with them, and see there where he expanded on the reason for the custom:`,
  "chok-yaakov:8:ה": `In a manner where there is no concern for chametz. And we do not decree this because of that, and similarly with honey in Bechoros it is permitted and we do not decree, because these things are common to bring honey from the hive and similarly fruits that an Israelite dries in his house he is careful about leavening — unlike saffron which is not common to grow in his garden, therefore it is forbidden even what grew in his garden, as Levush wrote:`,
  "chok-yaakov:8:ו": `Even to drink it is forbidden. The Rav's relative wrote in his book: and I saw a question came before our elder the Gaon Maharash z"l after Pesach that he retained hot tsuker, and he ruled to sell it to a gentile — end of his words. And Taz and Magen Avraham wrote there is no prohibition in hot tsuker, or what we call clear tsuker — it is permitted to retain in his house; nevertheless they were accustomed to sell it l'chatchila, and b'dieved it is permitted, and that this is the Rav's intent in the gloss tsuker kandil, not what we now call tsuker kandil, and all the more so types of night tsuker that they coat with types of fruits and spices which have complete leavening — such is the content of their words. But in truth even l'chatchila one should be careful regarding retaining these things; nevertheless if he transgressed and retained them, or mixtures, and there is great loss or prevention of Yom Tov joy, it is possible one should not forbid, for behold in the Talmud itself it implies it is only prohibition l'chatchila, and he wrote and this is its language: and similar that the world is not accustomed to forbid — end of his words; also Maharil Laws of Pesach implies it is only a distant concern, and in responsum siman 25 he wrote and this is its language: and they are accustomed to be careful with tsuker if it is not a great need for healing, for merchants mix several types into it — end of his words; and the reason appears to me there is uncertainty and many concerns: perhaps it was not mixed in, and if you say it was mixed in, perhaps it was nullified before Pesach, and if you say it was not nullified, perhaps they did not mix flour from wheat for sweets, and fruit juice does not leaven, and if you say for sweets, perhaps it did not leaven through this soaking — nevertheless before the decisor rules he needs investigation by a sage how and what was done, for this matter changes according to place and time, and l'chatchila they are accustomed to be stringent in every matter; but what we now call tsuker kandil many common people are lenient and this error draws them from the Rav's language in the gloss, and the Acharonim already agreed it is an error in their hands. And see in Knesset HaGedolah that he wrote also tsuker that is very fine has concern for leavening, and also the drink made from tsuker called syrup or latverig — Ashkenazim are accustomed to be stringent, and see above siman 466 notes 1 and 2; and on the last Yom Tov they eat, etc. — see Maharil that it was an incident that was so, but one cannot prove there is a distinction between the last Yom Tov and the first:`,
  "chok-yaakov:8:ז": `That they call saffron. And see Yoreh De'ah note 114 from the law of saffron all year, and Magen Avraham wrote and Turei Zahav: the way is to soak it in beer and one must seal it in a room or make a partition before it, and if so the same applies to saffron — end quote, and see above end of siman 461 from the law of vessels dyed with saffron that one may not use them on Pesach:`,
  "chok-yaakov:8:ח": `However we do not forbid their mixtures. It implies even in less than sixty it is also permitted, and see in note 16 from the law of honey, and in tsuker too one should not forbid mixtures even in types of night tsuker b'eyna, and in such a case one may be lenient as written in note 28 — see there:`,
  "chok-yaakov:9:א": `And if they did not actually split open. Even if it softened until close to splitting it is also permitted, and see above note 7 regarding the matter in its reason — see there:`,
  "chok-yaakov:9:ב": `The cooked dish is permitted. Magen Avraham wrote and this is its language: Bach wrote in the name of Kol Bo that if three barleys were found in a cooked dish, there is a presumption there are many and perhaps they split open; and that which the Mechaber omitted in Shulchan Aruch is because we do not hold thus, for the Taz wrote in Yoreh De'ah siman 84 seif 9 in the name of Maharshal that we do not say presumption except regarding worms that multiply from it, but regarding something that comes from outside we do not — end quote Magen Avraham. Behold what he wrote that therefore the Mechaber omitted because we do not hold thus is well aligned with halachah, for the Rif and Rosh and Mordechai chapter Kol Sha'ah in the name of BeHag and Agudah in the name of many Geonim wrote explicitly, and we do not forbid this cooked dish where two or three barleys are found that did not split open, and the same applies more — only there should be sixty in the cooked dish — see there; therefore the Mechaber omitted the opinion of Kol Bo in the name of Ra'avad who is alone against all these great ones. However what is supported from the Taz's words in the name of Maharshal — behold Maharshal's words on this are not compelled at all, and this distinction is not found in any posek, for anonymously we say three times is a presumption, even though it is something from outside, nevertheless this cooked dish is presumed that wheat or barley fell into it; and there is some proof for this from that which we say three [graves] — here is a presumption of burial, even though it is something from outside — see chapter 9 of Nazir and Rashi there; and that which the poskim I wrote hold to be lenient here — one can say since two or three were found and did not split open, we say even if there are more they also did not split open, and they were not so stringent in rabbinic any-amount prohibition — all this appears to me plainly the opinion of the poskim and the Mechaber, and for practical purposes according to the Rav's gloss there is no relevance in all this:`,
  "chok-yaakov:9:ג": `Rather they forbid everything in any amount. Since the Rav did not write this gloss after what the Mechaber wrote, and those wheats or barleys they burn — he should have written burn everything as written nearby seif 10 in the gloss; rather it certainly appears the Rav's opinion where they did not actually split open is the opinion of all the great poskim to permit the cooked dish even in eating — if so, this stringency suffices to forbid in eating and not in benefit, and see beginning siman 467, and even in actually split open the opinion of many poskim is to sell to a gentile as the Mechaber's opinion in seif 10 — therefore in any case one should not be so stringent when it is not split open. And further the main reason for the custom to be stringent is per what Mordechai wrote in chapter Kol Sha'ah, and he is stringent because of she'ilamuta, and it was already explained above siman 467 that we hold chametz is not called she'ilamuta; also what Mordechai further wrote there and this is its language: and so in Semag one should be stringent since we are not expert in what is called fit to split open, and because of custom it does not forbid in uncertainty, and one who is lenient for Yom Tov joy has not lost — end of his words. It appears his intent is to rule like Mar Ukva that close to splitting is also forbidden in a cooked dish — but in truth the Rif and Rambam and BeHag and Rosh and Tur and Ra'avad and Agudah and Rokeach and Kol Bo all permit the cooked dish as long as they did not actually split open — in this one need not be expert, for all are expert in this to see if they actually split open or not; therefore it seems fit to rely on them and permit at least in benefit, and similarly Taz wrote in this end; however in Taz note 15 it implies forbidden in benefit, and in my humble opinion in a place of great loss and the like one may permit at least in benefit, and especially since Mordechai himself concluded one who is lenient for Yom Tov joy has not lost — it implies even in eating one may permit; but Bach wrote that nowadays even for Yom Tov joy they are not accustomed to be lenient, and so wrote Rabbeinu Yerucham; nevertheless for permission of benefit it appears to me as written, and see siman 467 notes 1 and 2. And Bach wrote that in any case we require that the wheat kernel softened, for if not there is no prohibition at all:`,
  "chok-yaakov:9:ד": `Everything in any amount. And also the vessels used with them and the cooked dishes used with those vessels they also forbid, even though there are two any-amounts, as Magen Avraham wrote, unlike Taz as written above siman 467 note 5; also what Magen Avraham further challenged on Nehar Shalom I already wrote thus in my book Ma'adanei Yom Tov general rule 39, and I brought his words briefly in siman 466 — see there:`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:1:_": `Seif 1 — Grain that was submerged in a river, etc., rather one sells it to a Jew and informs him so that he eats it, etc. I would say: it implies that one relies on the admission, and uncertain from an explicit mishnah in tractate Demai chapter 3 mishnah 3 that the Tanna Kamma holds one does not rely on informing; however for the lenient ones only a little at a time works well, for here there are two factors for leniency; but to permit even much together through informing — very uncertain from an explicit mishnah, and in rabbinic law even according to R' Yosef one does not rely on informing — see there. And very uncertain, and it would be fit to expand on this but time does not allow — uncertain, end quote. (There in that seif) And if he sold it to a gentile before Pesach he sells a little to each one, etc. I would say: see in Magen Avraham that he brought in the name of the Kesef Mishneh that if it is little by little for his household he requires it, and Magen Avraham disagrees — see there. And it appears in my humble opinion there is proof for the Kesef Mishneh from the words of Tosafos beginning of chapter 2 of Pesachim in the passage of Chizkiyah — the Tosafos questioned how the mishnah "A person sends a thigh to a gentile" fits R' Yehuda who permits giving a gift to a gentile, etc.; and at first glance their words are astonishing — who told them that "a person sends a thigh to a gentile" deals with a gift? Perhaps it deals with a sale! Behold the main novelty is that we are not concerned perhaps he will sell it to a Jew together with its gid; and if so it holds that it deals with sale. And it is strained to say that "sends" implies for free; but according to the Kesef Mishneh it works well, for if it is through sale there is no need for the reason that its place is recognizable, for without this there is no concern he will sell it to a Jew since it is certainly little for his household; and why do I need the reason that its place is recognizable? And necessarily it deals with a gift, and they properly questioned the Tosafos, and it is proven like the words of the Kesef Mishneh, and it works well. And while I am on this I will mention what was difficult for me in the words of the Tosafos there, s.v. "And behold blood" — they questioned: let us say since the carcass was permitted its blood was permitted; and what is the question? Granted regarding milk and gid of any animal one can say when the carcass was permitted it and its milk and gid were permitted; but regarding blood, behold we say in the Gemara that blood from which the soul departs is called blood, and that from which the soul does not depart is not called blood; if so the carcass may have died by itself and has no blood, and if so it was permitted for that reason; but forever if there is blood from which the soul departs one can say it is forbidden in benefit — and what did they question? Uncertain, end quote. And behold regarding the law of tsuker that they put yeast at the time of making — see in my composition on Orach Chayyim Mahadura Kamma siman 82 in my responsum to the community of Tulshin where we wrote that one need not sell it on Pesach — see there, for I explained the reason of the matter — end quote.`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:2:_": `(There seif 8 in the gloss) And the custom in these countries not to eat honey, etc. I would say: behold an incident came before me from a certain wealthy man from this country who brought a drink called rum resembling arrack which gentiles are more sharp than wine, and he brought it from the city of London and also a kosher letter from the rabbi of the community of London, and there it says how this drink is made in the Indies in a manner that has no leavening concern at all; and in the community of London they were accustomed forty years to drink this rum on Pesach, and the rabbi of London and this wealthy man requested from me to give my agreement and the force of my decree to certify it also in our country on Pesach; and this is what I answered to your son among your people: far be it from me to put my hand in this to breach the fence and in my opinion it is forbidden on Pesach — not only according to Magen Avraham in this siman who holds that honey in its own form is forbidden if it was already crushed, and necessarily they decreed on this lest it be confused with other honey; even though in other honey there is no severe prohibition, only a distant concern — nevertheless they decreed on this lest it be confused; if so here too with this drink; and also according to the Taz who permits honey in its own form and is not concerned lest it be confused — from the place it appears that this drink is different from honey, for this resembles what Magen Avraham wrote in this siman seif 10 regarding saffron in the name of the Rashba, that even what he grew in his house is forbidden because of a decree, meaning since saffron is not common to grow in our countries and they will think it is saffron brought from afar; but fruits that a Jew dried are permitted and there is no concern for confusion (because it is common in our country, but this drink) and like honey from the hive as mentioned above — end quote. And if so this itself is the Taz's reason to permit honey and there is no concern for confusion because it is common in our country, but this drink which is not common in our country resembles exactly the law of saffron, and even though we know it was made with certification it is forbidden because of a decree lest it be confused; so too here; and why is it not similar to Pesach wine which they are accustomed to permit to drink and are not concerned lest it be confused with chametz wine — because the permission for this wine is common in our country, but these drinks which are not common in our country one must be concerned lest it be confused. And further it appears that also in wine they did not permit in early days except because of prevention of Yom Tov joy, for in their time good wine was not common in our country and wine was prevalent among everyone, and it was prevention of Yom Tov joy to forbid it; therefore because of this they established it on the law that it is permitted; but drinking this rum is not equal for everyone and there is no prevention of Yom Tov joy in this, and also now in our country wine is cheaper than these drinks; therefore it is fit to be concerned lest it be confused. And further it appears more — this resembles what the poskim wrote above siman 463 that one should not knead the pots themselves with baked matzah in a place where there are workers and breaches are common — all the more so here in our generation which is very unrestrained and breaches are very common, one may not be lenient in such a case, and it is more severe than a decree on baked matzah; and also resembles what the poskim wrote in siman 460 that one may not be lenient in what the early ones were stringent to make panadish or paladish — see there; and this too is only because of a decree and concern for confusion; and the same here; and also the matter of kitniyot species that the Sages forbade is only because of concern for confusion; and the same here. And further it appears that what they permitted Pesach wine is according to the majority, for the taste of Pesach wine differs from the taste of chametz wine and one can easily distinguish the taste between them; therefore they are not concerned lest it be confused; and behold nevertheless after they erred and deceived Israel — they lost their souls by not paying attention to distinguish between this and that; but at least the majority, since if they pay attention they can easily distinguish, therefore they are not concerned lest it be confused. But in this drink the taste is not recognizable between those from chametz and those from other things; also since it is not common in our country and they are not expert in its taste to distinguish between this and that — certainly one must be concerned lest it be confused; and one cannot say since arrack in general is made from rum as several people said, if so there is no decree on a drink that if arrack itself arrack is forbidden only because of the custom of kitniyot and it is a decree on a decree — this is not so, for apart from what we find on Pesach they were stringent stringency upon stringency and decreed on this a decree on a decree, as is clear from several places, one of them in siman 447 regarding taste imparted for spoilage, that even taste imparted by taste and any amount is forbidden — behold each one by itself is also not forbidden except because of Pesach stringency, nevertheless they decreed even when both combine together although it is a decree on a decree. And apart from this it appears it is one decree, for both arrack and this drink, even if made from rum, we decree on this lest it be confused with that made from chametz — all is one decree. And further it appears that its main prohibition here is because of concern for mixtures, lest chametz wine become mixed in, as they forbade gentile milk because of concern for mixtures of something impure, and they also forbade gentile morayis because of mixtures of stam yeinam; if so here too they forbade arrack because of concern for mixtures of chametz wine, and so with rum it is forbidden because of mixtures of wine; and if so since there is a trace of d'oraisa prohibition, even if one is known not to have mixed chametz wine in, it is forbidden because of concern for confusion, even though with morayis they did not decree on this; on chametz they were more stringent, and especially there stam yeinam is rabbinic unlike chametz which has Torah prohibition — and this is exactly like the law of saffron forbidden because they drip wine on it, and the Rashba forbade even what he grew in his house because of concern for confusion; and the same here; and it is not similar to Pesach wine for which they did not so decree for the reason mentioned; and further one can say on the contrary — chametz wine whose prohibition is more severe and it is known that it is chametz itself — if one were lenient with Pesach wine they would not err to permit chametz wine. And it resembles what we say in general: these are words of Torah and need no reinforcement; these are words of the Scribes and need reinforcement; so too here — arrack itself, since it is itself from kitniyot species, therefore it is not severe in their eyes and is only because of concern for mixtures of wine in it, and is only a rabbinic decree; therefore it needs more reinforcement in this, even when known not to have been mixed in; and if not this law does not stand; and especially in our generation, according to the breach of the generation — would that we had in our power to forbid even Pesach wine, how good it would be, and how many stumbling blocks already follow from this; only that it is not in our power to decree what is already breached; but since we are ashamed of the old ones, we are reluctant to add the new and breach further to permit also arrack — far be it; and in a small matter it will lead to leniency to drink all arrack on Pesach; therefore we are reluctant for our hand to be with this to uphold this certification and permit to conduct with permission what our fathers did not contemplate; these are the words of the speaker in truth for the honor of the Torah without destroying its foundation to dust — may the good God send us the teacher of righteousness and instruct us according to law and halachah, for the sake of the holy soul Shlomo Kluger, Sunday 28 Adar Rishon year "so that you may be vindicated in your words" here in Brod:`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:3:_": `(There seif 11) And there is one who forbids the entire dough, etc. I would say: see in the Taz and Magen Avraham that dough is worse than other cold cases; and see in my Chiddushim on Orach Chayyim year 567 in my responsum to the community of Stanislav in the omissions to the pamphlet of appetizers — see there, end quote:`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:4:_": `(There seif 12 in the gloss) And there is one who forbids in other meat and permits in chicken. I would say: the poskim wrote the reason — perhaps it was digested, and those who forbid hold it is not digestion. And behold I wonder at both of them that they did not give a boundary to the matter — in how much is there digestion; and the lenient ones imply they hold that even if found within the time of digestion for eating it is also an uncertainty perhaps it was digested, and those who forbid hold that even more than the time of digestion it is not digestion; and in truth an explicit mishnah in tractate Parah chapter 9 mishnah 5: a cow that drank waters of purification — its flesh is impure for the time of digestion; and the Ra'avad explained there because if it waited more than that it was digested in its intestines; if so it is proven that the measure of digestion is the time of digestion, and less than that is certainly not digestion, for if it were an uncertainty it would be to say its flesh is uncertain impurity; and from the fact that it says its flesh is impure it implies certain impurity. Also otherwise if it were only an uncertainty it should be pure entirely, for it is an uncertainty of impurity in something that has no knowledge to ask — its uncertainty is pure; and necessarily its impurity is certain; if so within the time of digestion is certainly digestion, and conversely after the time of digestion is certainly digestion, since it does not teach that because of uncertainty it is pure — for there is practical difference for several matters; and also if it came by human action it should be that its uncertainty is impure, such as if a person gave it to drink; and when it does not distinguish thus, necessarily it is certain digestion after the time of digestion. And it is possible there is a distinction between food and drink, that drink is digested faster; but at least from this it is proven that at least in drink the time of digestion is the measure of digestion, and I have no time now to expand further — examine well. And behold I was uncertain in my large composition on the laws of Pesach regarding a cooked dish cooked during the days of Pesach and remained until after Pesach, and afterward chametz was found in it — this proves it did not fall in afterward but was inside at the time it was Yom Tov; if we follow the time of cooking when its law was in any amount, or we follow the time of finding after Pesach when its law is like before Pesach, only in sixty or in majority. And the practical difference is whether the vessels are permitted afterward for the coming Pesach as with found before Pesach which is only in sixty; and we expanded there. And behold now a manuscript came to me that was in the possession of the Gaon R' Zalman Margaliot, copied from Sefer Rokeach, and its author Rabbeinu Elazar, etc., and he lists his family that he was a grandson of the author of Sefer Chassidim and one of the Rishonim; and I found in it in letter peh chapter 8 of the laws of Pesach, and this is its language: An incident where they cooked poultry after the seventh of Pesach and ate some of that poultry on that day it was cooked, and on the morrow on Shabbat which was the last of Pesach a wheat kernel was found in that portion remaining from the seventh day, and they asked whether to permit or forbid; some forbade from this that chametz in its time, whether in its species or not in its species, is forbidden like Rav — so I heard in Worms in the year they cooked on the seventh day of Pesach — end quote; behold it is proven that he holds that if it had been cooked on the eighth day it would be permitted in sixty, only since it was cooked on the seventh day, even though the finding was on the eighth day, we follow after cooking; and from this we learn for our case if found after Pesach and the cooking was on Pesach, we follow after cooking; and I benefited greatly from this with God's help — understand and examine well:`,
  "chokhmat-shlomo:5:_": `(There seif 16) A wheat kernel or barley kernel found in the crop of the bird, etc. I would say: behold what he permits afterward regarding found in the crop after roasting to discard it and not check it — see in my composition on Yoreh De'ah Mahadura Gimel in responsum to the community of Senatin where I wrote that that teacher ruled well, since there are several double uncertainties — perhaps in salting through peeling, and perhaps it is as if digested; and see in the Tur end of this siman and understand, end quote:`,
  "dagul-merevavah:1:_": `In my humble opinion it appears that if dry mixed with dry before Pesach and one of them was eaten, the remainder are permitted on Pesach even one by one, for behold this itself — that it returns and revives — is a dispute even in dry, as is clear from what I wrote in siman 447 seif 45 in Magen Avraham:`,
  "dagul-merevavah:2:_": `Meaning to eat two by two; and nevertheless in a place of great loss it appears to me, since dry with dry is a dispute even on Pesach and some say it is nullified — if so, when one of them is eaten one may rely in a place of great loss to permit even one with one:`,
  "dagul-merevavah:3:_": `And above in siman 447 he concludes himself that because of pouring it forbids — the Mordechai; and here too I do not know what compelled them to say that all the more so it is forbidden, for here too there is to forbid because of pouring:`,
  "dagul-merevavah:4:_": `The amount of peeling or the amount required for removal, and no more; see Magen Avraham beginning of siman 447 seif 4 and see siman 461 in Magen Avraham seif 37:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:1:_": `(1) [Levush] And it is not recognizable, etc. So wrote the Tur, and Magen Avraham wrote it implies that if the chametz is recognizable it is permitted and we are not concerned perhaps he will grind them and sell them since flour is not bought from the market except in time of distress — until here; and Chak Yaakov challenged that nevertheless when one keeps it on Pesach he transgresses bal yeira'eh, etc.; rather the Tur's intent in what he wrote "and the chametz is not recognizable" is for amplification that even when it is not recognizable we are concerned for mishap, and all the more so when recognizable. And in my humble opinion he holds that if because of this they did not decree — for one nullifies it in what he says "in something you do not see" — and so in Rabbeinu Yerucham it is explicit in concern lest one eat on Pesach; also what he wrote that the Tur's intent is for amplification is very puzzling — behold he concluded "since leavening is not recognizable" like Levush; also it seems to me the Tur according to his reasoning based on what Shach wrote in Yoreh De'ah end of siman 86 that the Tur holds one may sell a spoiled egg and we are not concerned they will put it in bread — see there, and there in my books on Yoreh De'ah I expanded; also I can compare to the Gemara Bava Metzia daf 60 — "my measure is known" — and if you say he added water, if so there is no end to the matter, and it is strained to distinguish:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:10:_": `(10) The beverage, etc. Bach forbids making the beverage on Pesach and Taz is lenient; and specifically in a barrel that comes from a hive; but those they bring in small vessels Magen Avraham forbids making a beverage even before Pesach since they mix much flour in them; and according to countries this matter changes — I shortened. And Chak Yaakov wrote he heard that honey of countries in which there is no concern of adulteration — but what they bring from other places they practice prohibition except those brought in a barrel with wax — until here; nevertheless everything is according to custom:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:11:_": `(11) [Levush] They permit, etc. And so is our custom to permit the black ones — Shiyurei Knesset HaGedolah; and in Prague they practice to forbid until the last Yom Tov of Pesach; but in Rezin they asked Mahari'l and he said one need not be strict — in response:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:12:_": `(12) Tsuker, etc. See in Terumat HaDeshen siman 119 that this was said only regarding spice compounds of quills or spice and fruit coverings; but hot tsuker they are clean and have no concern of mixture at all — so I heard from those engaged in tsuker work (Melechet Yom Tov). And behold this is difficult on Rama who forbids even to leave it; and Levush's opinion appears he holds from the side of custom one may not eat it but permits to leave it; but in spice compounds it all appears Levush too agrees they are forbidden to leave; and the same law applies to fine tsuker like dust — Shiyurei Knesset HaGedolah wrote forbidden; and tsuker kandil — Acharonim wrote forbidden to leave them; and some are lenient in tsuker kandil — meaning what is brought from Candia as Shiyurei Knesset HaGedolah and Magen Avraham wrote; and Taz wrote it is kaner or hot tsuker; and Shiyurei Knesset HaGedolah expanded to resolve Rama's words; and in the margin of Shulchan Aruch a great one expanded to analyze from Mahari'l who mentioned eating tsuker kandia — implying tsuker kandia is permitted to leave and not hot tsuker which has mixture concern; also I saw a question came before my grandfather the Gaon may his memory be blessed — they left hot tsuker and he ruled to sell it to a Samaritan; therefore it is fit to be stringent not to leave it l'chatchila; and b'dieved to sell to a Samaritan; and with loss even for eating:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:13:_": `(13) [Levush] Fruits that are dried, etc. And nevertheless places practice pouring cold water on them before Pesach until the beverage is made — for even though they dried in an oven it gives taste of imparting taste; and to cook with fruits they practice prohibition (Magen Avraham):`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:14:_": `(14) On the last Yom Tov, etc. It implies even hot tsuker; but tsuker kandia is permitted all days of Pesach; but in Rama it is explained kandia specifically on the last Yom Tov is permitted and does not forbid its mixture; but tsuker is forbidden on the last Yom Tov and also forbids its mixture; and so explained Shiyurei Knesset HaGedolah; however one may permit it in benefit for all these are only concerns. Acharonim wrote for medicine it is permitted on Pesach:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:15:_": `(15) Saffron, etc. I found Tuv Ta'am wrote their way is to soak it in beer and one must close it in a room or make a partition before it — until here; and if so the same law applies to saffron (Magen Avraham); and one may distinguish that regarding saffron some poskim permit — evidently it is not certain there is concern they soak in beer; and in Reinish they practiced as Mahari'l wrote and so Shach in Yoreh De'ah end siman 114 implies thus; and even though we practice prohibition, nevertheless to make a partition perhaps it is not needed; and if saffron or vanilla or dried fruits and figs mentioned above mixed in a cooked dish — one should not be stringent even in less than sixty:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:16:_": `(16) [Levush] To be stringent, etc. The cooked dish and vessel and spoon and cover on it; and every cooked dish that mixed from that cooked dish is forbidden in any amount (Bach); and at the beginning of siman 447 I explained all these laws:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:2:_": `(2) [Levush] Much together, etc. And Rashal and Bach forbade even a little lest he sell to an Israelite; and so implies the Gemara there daf 40; and so it appears to me proven from Rabbeinu Yerucham and Semag siman 222 and Sefer Tzeda Laderekh and Riaz in Shiltei Gibborim; and it is straightforward that one may sell to Samaritans who know him that they will guard it until after Pesach as written in siman 448; and even much chametz on the fifth hour on erev Pesach is permitted:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:3:_": `(3) [Levush] Even much, etc. And Rashal and Bach and Taz were stringent that even with informing it is forbidden in much lest he forget it. Samaritans — if he said to an Israelite after he bought wheat from him that they are for dates — he is not believed since they already left his possession and his hand; however if he is believed to him it is forbidden:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:4:_": `(4) For leavening, etc. And if not for the fact that this measure of a mil it has not yet leavened — one may sell it to Samaritans on Pesach before the measure of a mil:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:5:_": `(5) [Levush] If their appearance changed, etc. and such is the law, etc. And one should not challenge that above I explained regarding recognizable that if it leavened one may sell to a Samaritan — for here it deals only when their appearance changed and it is not recognizable that it leavened:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:6:_": `(6) [Levush] One may take, etc. Even for eating — see siman 466 s.k. 15 that there it is forbidden in eating; and one must say there it is closer to certainty more:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:7:_": `(7) Honey, etc. And if mixed b'dieved in a cooked dish — the opinion of Magen Avraham is to forbid when there are not sixty; but Chak Yaakov permits even in less for we rely here on Rabbeinu Yona that it dissolved and became honey; and if it is known certainly they mix flour in it he wrote it is permitted to keep it; and if mixed inside the cooked dish — if there is loss and prevention of Yom Tov joy, one who is lenient did not lose even in less than sixty. And in my humble opinion all this is when it is not known they mix much in it until there are not sixty against the honey — one should not rely to be lenient in eating at all; see in Shiyurei Knesset HaGedolah and Acharonim — I shortened:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:8:_": `(8) [Levush] Also water, etc. In Shiyurei Knesset HaGedolah he wrote he heard from honey owners when they remove honey from the hive it is impossible if they do not place it in hot water:`,
  "eliyah-rabbah:9:_": `(9) That they bring, etc. from the hive, etc. And he did not write in a barrel from the hive as Rama wrote — because that is forbidden in eating in its own form and is not permitted except what they bring with small combs intact as it lies in the hive that has not yet been crushed (Magen Avraham); and now it works well that Levush opened at the beginning of the seif "some forbid" — only there in a barrel; and Rashal and Bach forbade eating honey in its own form in every case lest it be confused; and it appears they agree in this that what they bring thus it appears to permit — for in this it does not come to be confused; and Taz is lenient to eat even in its own form when it comes in a barrel from the hive; and so ruled Chak Yaakov; and I already wrote everything is according to custom in this:`,
  "eshel-avraham:1:_": `Before Pesach. And Peri Chadash challenged why he did not make a tikkun to nullify like dry in dry that is nullified in 100 and two — for according to us who hold it returns and revives it does not help — see there:`,
  "eshel-avraham:10:_": `They practice burning. And Tur in Yoreh De'ah siman 175 explained practically that redemption suffices from the reason of a double uncertainty — perhaps the halachah is that redemption suffices for chametz; and perhaps the halachah is that something does not forbid in benefit — see there; and he also raised that chametz mixed with matzah dry — for everyone there is a tikkun in redemption; and he also raised that one should not distinguish between one wheat kernel or even much in a matter where there is not sixty against them — for still there is the reason it is not sold at a high price on account of this — see there; and in siman 176:`,
  "eshel-avraham:12:א": `Split open. See Be'er Heitev s.k. 24; and accordingly when that case is in Tur siman 451 and it is found on Pesach in cold water one wheat kernel and from those waters they already made a cooked dish and baked matzot in it — and there was uncertainty whether the wheat kernel was split open — they permitted by force of a double uncertainty that perhaps it was not split; and further behold there is a dispute of the great ones whether cold water forbids the cooked dish; and further he raised from R' Shmuel Gaon that even certainly split open one may permit; and even though Shulchan Aruch wrote "and there is one who forbids during Pesach" — this reasoning we do not find explicitly except because the Tur's words contradicted each other and in order to settle them he distinguished between found on Pesach and before it; but the Gaon resolves the Tur's puzzlements with a broad explanation that when the water is cold, even split open and even on Pesach it is permitted for the wheat kernel does not impart taste — see in Tur Rosh Yosef and Kaf HaChayyim:`,
  "eshel-avraham:12:ב": `Permitted. Magen Avraham explains this is in a little chametz and sixty in water against it; and know Levush expanded to permit in cold in the name of sages and great elders in the name of Mahari'l of Prague; but Maharash of Austria wrote to Maharam of Lublin for prohibition; and so they practice — not like Taz — see there:`,
  "eshel-avraham:13:_": `And the water descended. See Tur Beit Yehudah siman 14:`,
  "eshel-avraham:2:א": `Forbidden. And if they split open and their splitting is not recognizable. And if they became mixed in a pile they are forbidden even after Pesach and forbidden to sell to gentiles; and when he ate from it a kezayit it is permitted to eat them on Pesach — see Yoreh De'ah siman 96 that there is no matzah without more than the measure of prohibition and it is two d'rabbanan prohibitions — Bach in the name of Ra'avad, and he disagrees because that something remaining forbids all the matzah — see siman 461; and Magen Avraham — what he wrote against Kol Bo; and see Be'er Heitev s.k. 9:`,
  "eshel-avraham:2:ב": `But to eat them. Taz and Peri Chadash challenged — behold even in d'oraisa we rely on a double uncertainty, all the more so in something d'rabbanan; see siman 453 seif 3 — one must say it deals when there is not sixty against the prohibition — see there until he concludes they are forbidden to eat since there is no loss in leaving them; and accordingly when he has no other grain it is permitted. Shach in Yoreh De'ah wrote the reason — since according to Mar Ukva even if they did not split open they are forbidden and it is a double uncertainty of a dispute and not in the reality of the matter. And according to Chayei Adam siman 7 this is more lenient and he agreed to the opinion of Taz — see there. From his words — recall, I still remember what I heard from the mouth of the great famous Gaon Av Beit Din and Rosh Yeshiva Maharar Shaul Nerya of the Ashkenazic community in Amsterdam, may he live. Certain matters that were renewed for him in Beit HaMidrash Rishon L'Tzion — he delivered a sermon on what Magen Avraham brought siman 442 s.k. 1: the Tur wrote that every matter that is not forbidden in eating from the Torah is permitted to mix and leave — and so too Semag; but Tosafot at the beginning of Pesachim wrote it is forbidden, for the Rabbis did not distinguish, and they proved thus from the mishnah that se'or shall be burned — and even though it was hard and there is no d'oraisa prohibition in eating it, nevertheless it shall be burned d'rabbanan, etc.; and according to the Tur's opinion one must say that regarding se'or it deals on Yom Tov, etc. — end of his words; and it is clear to everyone this answer is very strained. But in my humble opinion I cannot understand the Tur's method; on the contrary Tosafot's words need explanation — for Peri Chadash from that which R' Gidal said, Rav said: one who consecrates from six and above — even in Cordovanian wheat we are not concerned for its sanctity, for it is hard chametz — and nevertheless we are not concerned for its sanctity; if so it is proven that even before its time — meaning from six and above until erev — the Sages forbade hard chametz even in benefit; and on daf 28 R' Yehuda holds before its time and after its time they are equal for every matter regarding full chametz from the Torah. If so, just as the Rabbis decreed on hard chametz before its time to forbid it in benefit — so too they decreed to forbid hard chametz after its time in benefit according to R' Yehuda; and since that regarding se'or shall be burned R' Yehuda taught it as brought on daf 48 — see there — there is no proof from there at all. For according to R' Yehuda even if one leaves it after its time it should be forbidden in benefit d'rabbanan as it is forbidden before its time d'rabbanan — therefore it shall be burned. But for us who hold before its time like R' Yehuda and after its time like R' Shimon — full chametz after Pesach is forbidden only because of a penalty where one transgressed bal yeira'eh upon it; but regarding hard chametz on which one did not transgress bal yeira'eh they did not decree, unlike before its time when full chametz is forbidden from the Torah — therefore they decreed on hard chametz d'rabbanan; therefore one who consecrates from six and above — even in hard chametz we are not concerned for sanctity; but after its time hard chametz is permitted even d'rabbanan since one did not transgress bal yeira'eh upon it; if so it is permitted to leave it even l'chatchila in order to eat it after Pesach — and the same through mixtures that are comparable to hard chametz; and there is no difficulty on the Tur at all; and what Tosafot wrote at the beginning of Pesachim needs explanation — I expanded elsewhere. This [and another matter]: siman 444 in Magen Avraham s.k. 9 — you may wonder on what he wrote above siman 249 that it is forbidden to make a betrothal feast on erev Pesach and it is from the Yerushalmi, etc. — end of his words — I do not know what difficulty at all; behold it is explicit in Shulchan Aruch there — feast and drinking that one is not accustomed on weekdays; but if one makes a betrothal feast in a manner one is also accustomed on weekdays it is permitted — and one may say here it deals thus; and see like what Hagahot Maimoni explained siman 554 s.k. 12 — see there. And further (the third matter) in 444 Magen Avraham s.k. 12 "if so it implies at the sixth hour when it cannot be nullified it revives," etc. — see in Sefer Even HaOzer newly printed in Magen Avraham Propes edition who expanded to distinguish on this; and one of his proofs from what is taught "one who goes to slaughter his Pesach" — and there even when it cannot be nullified, etc., for Pesach is more severe for it is a positive commandment with karet, etc., and certainly the positive commandment of ceasing work overrides it — and the same for all those explicitly taught there, etc., and I did not grasp his mind at all — for certainly it cannot slaughter Pesach while chametz is in his possession and he did not nullify it, at least "you shall not slaughter upon chametz the blood of My sacrifice" is written, and we hold even if it is not with him in the courtyard. If so, on the contrary, that regarding one who goes to slaughter his Pesach certainly deals at the hour when he can still nullify, and the same for the other matters explicitly taught — and this is straightforward. And four (I know) from the aforementioned famous Gaon and more than them — but the middle is too short to expand siman 451 seif 4 in the gloss — see Taz and what they expanded on this. And in my humble opinion the lenient ones are R' Eliezer of Metz and Avi HaEzri who hold chametz is called absorbed prohibition and hag'alah or light libun suffices as in Hagahot Maimoni chapter 2 of Laws of Chametz — end of his words; and on this Rama wrote we practice like the first reasoning to consider chametz like absorbed prohibition requiring complete libun — and specifically in matters requiring libun by law in other prohibitions, likewise in chametz we practice thus because we consider chametz like absorbed prohibition; but in matters one does not libun except out of stringency like that of seif 6 — and even though Rabbeinu Yoel holds there complete libun is required in other prohibitions except B"H — nevertheless since other poskim do not hold thus, even in other prohibitions to require complete libun; rather we are stringent to require complete libun in other prohibitions like Rabbeinu Yoel — nevertheless regarding chametz, for many poskim chametz is called absorbed prohibition, we practice with libun to be lenient with light libun in that of seif 6 as appears to me — Rama's intent is clear and correct for law; and what Taz wrote to distinguish between b'eyna and absorbed — see there — is difficult for me, for behold spits and stones of consecrated items they roast meat b'eyna upon them and nevertheless hag'alah suffices because absorbed prohibition as in end of Avodah Zarah — and from there he derives the law of absorbed prohibition; if so one cannot distinguish thus — requires study:`,
  "eshel-avraham:3:_": `Their appearance. And when they changed or became moist they are forbidden to leave — Tur; and when water came upon them one should forbid even in Cordovanian wheat; and if they did not change at all — such as those coming from the lands of Iran when they are not moist, even though they are not as hard as other wheat, they are permitted — and so Peri Chadash:`,
  "eshel-avraham:4:_": `A.C.H. That which Nachmanides distinguishes that from a fixed place it is forbidden to buy — Rashba goes according to his method that even heaps on which rain fell are permitted, for perhaps they entered only in the upper ones; and if you say it entered in the belly of the heap — perhaps it did not have time to leaven and it is a double uncertainty — and so in Beit HaGadol — see there:`,
  "eshel-avraham:8:א": `One does not establish presumptive prohibition. That which flour is mixed in it — one must burn it — Yerushalmi; even though the opinion of Ra'osh in the name of R' Yitzchak that we follow after the fact — nevertheless one should not be lenient:`,
  "eshel-avraham:8:ב": `Some are stringent. And in all this they permit mixtures less than sixty — even if certainly mixed in a cooked dish in less than sixty, one who relies on the words of Beit Yosef mentioned above because of Yom Tov joy did not lose; and even though Magen Avraham rejects the reasoning of Rabbeinu Yona mentioned above — for us we follow the beginning — see there; and Taz in this permission regarding saffron that Rashba wrote they mix meat in it — and regarding musk — see there; and so Peri Chadash agreed to the Mechaber unless it is known that one of the sellers mixed flour or water in it — then it is forbidden to buy from its fixed place:`,
  "eshel-avraham:8:ג": `That one need not be concerned. And Mahari'l was strict regarding small grapes that become mixed with figs and dried grapes from concern they scatter flour on them; and one who wishes to keep the sambur should keep it on our grapes:`,
  "eshel-avraham:9:א": `Or wheat. And Maharam siman 446 and Tur in Yoreh De'ah wish to distinguish and hold that wheat — since they are hard — do not soften except in hot water — see there; and in many places explicitly that one should not distinguish — see there; and so Peri Chadash:`,
  "eshel-avraham:9:ב": `They did not split open. Even if it softened — for it is uncertain whether the halachah is like Mar Ukva or like Shmuel; and in something it is an uncertainty d'rabbanan and for leniency — Rif; and in seif 2 he forbids even if they did not split open — meaning to eat the wheat in its own form; and when three came out — if it is a fixed practice see 465 s.k. 3 and Magen Avraham:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:1:_": `(1) [Seif 1] Grain that was submerged in a river, etc. and there is leavening concern in them and it is not recognizable in them. Tur. Levush. It implies that if the chametz is recognizable it is permitted (to sell even much to one gentile — R' Zalman osef 4) and we are not concerned perhaps he will grind them and sell them since flour is not bought from the market except in time of distress as written siman 453 — and not like Acharonim. Magen Avraham beginning of the siman; however Ran in chapter 25 wrote that even if they split open and leavening is recognizable in them — nevertheless it is forbidden to sell to gentiles for we are concerned perhaps he will grind them and afterward sell to an Israelite — end of his words; and so Acharonim osef 1; and he wondered at the Tur's language that implies if the chametz is recognizable it is permitted — see there; and this is what Magen Avraham wrote "and not like Acharonim." And the Ran's words mentioned above — Chayei Adam brought osef 1 and challenged Magen Avraham's words mentioned above — for nevertheless we are concerned perhaps an Israelite will take that flour k'zayit and keep it in his house and transgress bal yeira'eh; and he wrote the Tur's intent is that even though the chametz is not recognizable and one knows only there is chametz concern — nevertheless we are concerned for mishap regarding sale, and all the more so in known recognizable chametz we are concerned l'chatchila — see there; however Acharonim osef 1 wrote to resolve Chayei Adam's difficulty — for Magen Avraham holds that if because of this they did not decree — for one nullifies it in what he says "you did not see it" — see there; and Mekor Chayim in Biurim agreed to Magen Avraham's words — see there. And Erech HaShulchan osef 1 wrote to permit selling to a gentile by force of a double uncertainty — perhaps he will sell to a gentile; and if you say he will sell to an Israelite — perhaps he will sell it as wheat that is recognizable — see there; but Rav Beit Din siman 232 agreed to the Ran's words — see there. It is found this matter is in dispute — therefore l'chatchila if possible one should sell a little to each one or sell it in a manner they return it after Pesach as written above siman 448 seif 3; and if impossible and there is great loss — then one may rely on the lenient ones; and so R' Zalman osef 4 that with great loss one may rely on the lenient ones:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:10:_": `(10) There. Lest the gentile return, etc. And even though it is a double uncertainty — since it is a double uncertainty by handiwork one does not permit from the reason of a double uncertainty. Rashdam Yoreh De'ah siman 45; Maharimat Yoreh De'ah part 2 siman 2; Kenesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Taz; Acharonim osef 5; Magen Avraham s.k. 2; Zecher LeAvraham; and so in Sefer Zivchei Tzedek on Yoreh De'ah siman 110 in laws of double uncertainty osef 149 in the name of many poskim — and he agreed thus for law — see there:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:11:_": `(11) There. And he will sell it to an Israelite. An incident: an Israelite bought wheat from a gentile and afterward another Israelite came and asked the gentile to sell him wheat too; and he said "I have no wheat — for the Jew to whom I sold is my friend and I sold him the wheat I washed and asked to grind for my household needs and sold them to my friend." It appears to me it is comparable to what is written in Yoreh De'ah siman 16 seif 11 that a gentile is not believed in what he said after he sold them and they left his hand; however if he is believed to him it is forbidden — end of his words. Magen Avraham s.k. 4; Chayei Adam osef 5; Acharonim osef 3; Yad Ephraim in Hagahot Taz; Chak Yosef osef 4; Mekor Chayim osef 5; and he wrote there Chayei Adam implies before he sold them, since they are in his hand he is believed — and this is not so, for since it can be said he intended to improve his merchandise he is not believed — see there; but Chak Yosef there challenged Chayei Adam's words and wrote in the name of many poskim that while still in his hand he is believed to forbid even where it can be said he says thus to improve his merchandise — see there; and so in Sefer Zivchei Tzedek on Yoreh De'ah siman 16 osef 34 in the name of many poskim — see there:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:12:_": `(12) [Seif 2] This grain that was submerged in a river, etc. See Beit Yosef who wrote that some distinguish between wheat and barley — that in barley they require splitting open; but in wheat, since there is a crack as one who split open it is considered — only he concluded but the opinion of Rif and Rambam is that there is no distinction between wheat and barley, and so we rule — end of his words; and so appears from the silence of his words here in Shulchan Aruch that in all types of grain it is forbidden even though they did not split open. And so Acharonim osef 2; and so appears from the silence of the Acharonim who used the term "grain" — implying in all types of grain it is forbidden even though they did not split open:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:13:_": `(13) There. This grain that was submerged in a river, etc. And if it is permitted to nullify this grain in majority or in sixty k'zayit — since still the time of permission applies — we already wrote above siman 447 osef 156 and 157 and 158 — we took from there; and see further what Sha'arei Teshuvah wrote osef 6 in the name of Mahariatz and what we wrote above siman 466 osef 41 — see there:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:14:_": `(14) There. Or rain fell upon them. Even a little. Bach. Taz s.k. 3. Acharonim osef 2; R' Zalman osef 2; Mishna Berurah osef 6:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:15:_": `(15) There. Even though they did not split open — forbidden, etc. And even though regarding l'titah they are forbidden only if they split open — that case is different for one is engaged with it — Tur in the name of R' Efrayim. Magen Avraham s.k. 3; and that which is written below in seif 9 that if it did not actually split open the cooked dish is permitted even though they rested inside the cooked dish — nevertheless one may say since the cooked dish is boiling it is like pickling that does not leaven according to Tur siman 461 and 467 — see there — only there is concern it was in water before boiling; therefore we are not so stringent; and further one may say — and this is primary — that in a cooked dish that is only a mixture of something d'rabbanan we are not so stringent, as Magen Avraham wrote s.k. 12 — see there; and according to what Rambam wrote chapter 5 it appears the reason is simple — for grain that was kneaded or cooked and did not actually split open is not Torah chametz, and also something d'rabbanan they did not forbid two rabbinic prohibitions. Chayei Adam osef 7; and see there he wrote all this to settle the poskim's and Shulchan Aruch's opinion; but for law Rama already concludes in seif 9 in the gloss that they practice to forbid in every case — see there; and see in our words there b'siyata dishmaya:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:16:_": `(16) There. Even though they did not split open — forbidden, etc. Meaning that even though we do not see in it any badness and it is not close to splitting open at all — nevertheless it is forbidden, for it is not comparable to l'titah where one is engaged with it; and therefore even if one leaves it on erev Pesach on a barrel and the like alone to check if it leavened — it does not help. Ma'amar Mordechai osef 4:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:2:_": `(2) There. Thus it is forbidden to keep it. And if he transgressed and kept it and planted them in the ground and their growths sprouted — they are permitted as written above siman 443. Chayei Adam osef 2; and see in our words there osef 33:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:3:_": `(3) There. And he informs him, etc. And proclamation does not help here as it helps with meat in the chapter of gid hanasheh — Beit HaGadol chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah. Kenesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Taz. Magen Avraham s.k. 1. Chemed Moshe osef 1:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:4:_": `(4) There. And he informs him, etc. This is the language of Rambam chapter 5; and Beit Yosef wrote that for Rambam we do not say "we sell them keva keva" except when selling to a gentile; but to an Israelite — if he does not inform him, even keva keva is also forbidden for we are concerned perhaps he will leave it in order to eat it on Pesach; and if he informs him, even much is permitted — end of his words. But Maharshal wrote that even when informing him to sell much to him is forbidden; and Bach agreed to his words. And so Taz agreed s.k. 2. Acharonim osef 1; Acharonim osef 3; however Peri Chadash agreed like Beit Yosef's words that whenever he does not inform him, even keva keva is forbidden lest he guard it for Pesach; and when he informs him, even much is permitted. And so Shulchan Gavoah agreed osef 1; Chayei Adam osef 3; Chak Yosef osef 2; R' Zalman osef 3; Chayei Adam general rule 126 osef 2; Mishna Berurah osef 2:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:5:_": `(5) There. And he informs him, etc. And if it is recognizable that they leavened — one need not inform an Israelite since the chametz is recognizable. Chayei Adam there. And it implies even much; however Mishnat Hasofer osef 1 wrote regarding an Israelite a little is obvious that when recognizable one need not inform him — see there; it implies in much it is not obvious — therefore one should be stringent in much l'chatchila that he must inform him:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:6:_": `(6) There. And if he sold it to a gentile k'zayit he sells a little, etc. However Maharshal wrote that to a gentile even keva keva is forbidden; and Bach agreed to his words. And so Acharonim osef 2; but Taz osef 2 agreed like Shulchan Aruch's words. And so Peri Chadash agreed. Chemed Moshe osef 1; R' Zalman osef 2; Chayei Adam there:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:7:_": `(7) There. He sells a little, etc. And specifically in chametz a little at a time helps — for even if you say he will sell to an Israelite, he will eat it k'zayit; but in other prohibitions a little at a time does not help — for nevertheless we are concerned he will sell them to an Israelite or give as a gift to his Israelite friend. Magen Avraham s.k. 2. Chayei Adam osef 4; Mekor Chayim osef 4; Mishna Berurah osef 4; however see in Sefer Zivchei Tzedek on Yoreh De'ah siman 84 osef 33 that he brought a dispute in this regarding other prohibitions whether one may sell to a gentile a little at a time — and he concluded one should not be lenient except with great loss — see there:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:8:_": `(8) There. So that it will be consumed k'zayit, etc. Seif 8 — even if you say that little too he will sell to an Israelite — nevertheless that little an Israelite will eat k'zayit; and accordingly it implies on erev Pesach at the fifth hour in a matter where it cannot be consumed k'zayit it is forbidden to sell to a gentile even a little at a time from concern that perhaps he will sell to an Israelite — and so is the language of the Gemara and Rambam and other poskim; nevertheless if it leavened for an Israelite much grain on erev Pesach at the fifth hour and it is in a matter where it is impossible for him to sell to a gentile that it be consumed k'zayit and there is great loss — it is permitted to sell to a gentile who knows him and recognizes him that he will not touch it at all but guard it for him until after Pesach; and he may say to him "until you take it for a maneh," etc. as explained above siman 448 seif 3 and 4 — and then there is no concern for anything. Chayei Adam there. Chak Yosef osef 3; Mekor Chayim there; R' Zalman osef 4; Mishna Berurah osef 3:`,
  "kaf-hachayyim:9:_": `(9) There. So that it will be consumed k'zayit, etc. See Rav Beit Din siman 237 who leaned that if there is much time until Pesach, even much one can sell to a gentile — see there. Zecher LeAvraham:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:1:_": `In the Tur he wrote 26 and some say perhaps he will grind them, etc., for one does not buy flour from the market, etc., and the Ran who wrote that even if the splitting is recognizable it is forbidden to sell them to a gentile lest he sell them to an Israelite and the Israelite not intend to examine them. Or lest the gentile grind them and return and sell them to an Israelite — this is because the Ran wrote before this according to the Rif regarding guarding from harvest time in the passage "and a person needs to guard, etc." in the name of a Gaon that one does not buy flour from the market except in pressing circumstances. And this is only for matzah of the commandment; but other bread that a person eats on Pesach, etc. — it implies that it is permitted to buy flour from the market for other matzot. But the Tur — it is possible he holds that even for other matzot one does not buy from the market, for now the practice of gentiles is to moisten the wheat before grinding; and with this what Kaf HaChayyim questioned is not difficult at all, that the Israelite will take the flour and leave it in his house and make a prohibition in that he leaves chametz in his house, and even if it was nullified nevertheless there is a d'rabbanan prohibition — no difficulty here, for it is forbidden to make matzot from them; and it is a distant concern that an Israelite will buy flour about which there is concern for leavening, for from this reason he is not permitted to make from them since their practice is to moisten them; and from the Torah he will buy this flour and leave it without need; and it is possible that according to what is forbidden to make matzot from them for all the days of Pesach, since their practice is to moisten, it is possible that also to leave it in his house is forbidden; in any case it is a distant concern, end quote.`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:10:_": `(seif katan 10) Saffron, etc., and so in honey from a hive — meaning the Levush permitted above seif katan 7; and the Bach above was concerned for substitution. But the Bach above was concerned for substitution in honey too even though in other fruits he was not concerned for substitution:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:11:א": `(seif katan 11) They are not, etc., for it is fitting — the Rosh, etc., and so ruled the Mechaber: gentile honey one does not retain, etc., and the Rosh wrote the reason that it is not common that flour becomes mixed in it and also it was nullified before Pesach, end of the Rosh's words:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:11:ב": `And as written in siman 447, etc., also this is from the words of Darkei Moshe; and so in Shulchan Aruch above siman 447 seif 5: however if something of these things became mixed, etc., and see there in Magen Avraham:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:11:ג": `Siman 447 regarding dry meat seif katan 16 in the name of the Bach. And in Yoreh De'ah siman 69 seif 9 in the gloss: if meat was salted and not rinsed in the final rinsing and dried — even if placed in a kli rishon boiling it is permitted, since it dried there are certainly sixty against the blood:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:12:א": `(seif katan 12) They did not split open, etc., one authority says it is permitted — meaning Shmuel holds splitting open is specifically required; but Mar Ukva holds even if they did not split open, if when placed on the opening of the roaster they would split open — meaning they are close to splitting — they are forbidden; and since the halachah was not clarified like whom, all that did not split open literally is a safek. It implies if mixed in less than sixty it is forbidden, for then it is a Torah safek — for we hold the halachah is like all who say d'oraisa. See Yoreh De'ah siman 98 regarding a mixture that was poured out and we know the majority is permitted but we are uncertain if there were sixty — then if it is species in its species, which from the Torah is nullified in the majority, there were sixty d'rabbanan to be lenient; and if it is species in its non-species, which from the Torah requires sixty, it is a Torah safek and therefore we follow the safek to stringency and it is forbidden — the same here. For in seif 2 he ruled even if they did not split open it is forbidden, since it rests at ease; and here too the cooked dish rests at ease — why did he be lenient in the cooked dish:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:12:ב": `And it is difficult: since, etc., meaning still what he questioned is not resolved; for behold the Taz in Yoreh De'ah and so Magen Avraham above siman 465:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:א": `(seif katan 13) They forbid everything, etc., and the same applies to other cooked dishes, etc., even though the first cooked dish was forbidden only through any amount — nevertheless it forbids the other cooked dishes through any amount:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ב": `And in the Taz in Yoreh De'ah, etc., one cannot understand his words through purging — I must present his words briefly: in Chullin page 100 Rav taught: once taste was given in a piece, the piece itself becomes nevelah and forbids all the pieces because they are its species — Rav holds species in its species is not nullified, meaning such as when a pot was full of gravy and within the gravy were several pieces of permitted meat, and above the gravy was placed one permitted piece and on that piece a piece of nevelah; and one cannot combine the pieces within the gravy neither for prohibition nor for permission, since that piece on which the nevelah rests is above the gravy. Therefore if the nevelah piece gave taste in that permitted piece it becomes nevelah; and if it stirred again the whole pot until that upper piece too entered the gravy, it forbids all the pieces even though there are sixty against it because they are its species and for R' Yehuda species in its species is not nullified from the Torah. And the Gemara challenges: what is different — taste was given, etc., even if it did not give taste as well, since it stirred the nevelah piece itself forbids the other pieces; and Abbaye answers: when he preceded and removed the prohibition — meaning the nevelah piece before stirring the pot. And Tosafos challenged: still the difficulty remains — why taste was given in the piece; behold the nevelah piece with that upper piece is species in its species and should be forbidden in any amount and forbid the whole pot when stirred — because of any amount. And Rabbenu Tam answered: namely that we say also in other prohibitions a piece that becomes nevelah through any amount — nevertheless since that piece was forbidden only through any amount it does not forbid the other pieces in the pot through any amount, and it itself does not become nevelah; and so the Tur cited in Yoreh De'ah; and from this the Taz derived regarding chametz on Pesach in a piece forbidden through any amount: if that piece fell again elsewhere it does not forbid in any amount — a fortiori, for according to R' Yehuda species in its species from the Torah is not nullified — nevertheless in such a case it does not forbid, all the more so chametz on Pesach where what forbids in any amount is d'rabbanan — all the more so in such a case it should not forbid. And they loaded the Tur's language there and the Taz inferred from his wording: it does not forbid through purging — meaning as the Taz concluded there: specifically if he recognizes that piece and throws it outside — then it does not forbid except through purging — therefore it is permitted as stated. But if he does not recognize the piece, in any case he must nullify the piece itself, for it was forbidden; and also regarding wet in wet — see there:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ג": `And so wrote, etc., and even though the piece does not become nevelah — nevertheless the prohibition forbids in any amount — meaning the any amount of absorbed prohibition forbids. And this is the reasoning of Derishah that the Taz brought there; except the Taz rejected his words: if so the Tosafos' difficulties return to their place — why taste was given in the piece; behold the piece is not nevelah through any amount — nevertheless that absorbed any amount should forbid the whole pot when stirred because they are its species; and one might answer in Sefer Nekudot: according to R' Yehuda, since specifically species in its species is not nullified — if so specifically when the prohibition is in its substance then it is called species in its species and forbids in any amount; but when that any amount is absorbed in the piece and another force is mixed in that any amount, it is not species in its species with the pieces in the pot — therefore the any amount does not forbid the pot when stirred. Unlike for us in chametz, where even if not its species, that absorbed any amount forbids — see there:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ד": `And there is a practical difference, etc., meaning for us what difference does it make whether the piece became nevelah through any amount and the piece forbids the other pieces, or the any amount within it forbids — certainly for R' Yehuda there is a practical difference as stated; but for us too who do not hold like R' Yehuda there is a practical difference:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ה": `A thing that imparts taste, etc., meaning if at first it fell into a thing that imparts taste where sixty does not help and it was forbidden, and afterward that piece fell elsewhere where absorption does not impart taste there — if the piece had become nevelah it would require sixty against the whole piece; but since through any amount it did not become nevelah, it does not need sixty except against the any amount:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ו": `Chametz within its time, etc., Magen Avraham explained below his intent in this. If it became mixed in its species and not its species — we say remove, etc. — if so the intent is not as the simple meaning that it fell immediately into its species and not its species; for even though for R' Yehuda species in its species is not nullified, nevertheless we say seif katan: its species is as if it is not its species and what is not its species is greater and nullifies it, as we say in Chullin page 106 that this law has no application here for practical differences. Rather it must be that it fell first into its species alone and forbade it in any amount, and afterward that piece forbidden in any amount fell into not its species — for if the piece had become nevelah it would require sixty against the whole piece, and now that through any amount it did not become nevelah it does not need sixty except against the any amount. And what he concluded remove its species as if it is not — the Gemara used concise language; its intent is that piece forbidden in any amount because it was its species — we say remove, for it does not forbid through purging since it did not become nevelah; therefore he did not conclude in the Gemara's language and what is not its species is greater and nullifies it; but still the difficulty remains why it fell afterward into not its species — even if it fell again into its species, since he certainly holds as stated in Sefer Nekudot that since another force is mixed in it, for R' Yehuda species in its species from the Torah is not nullified by Torah decree — in such a case it is called not its species; and it is a great stretch to say his intent in what he wrote is truly thus — that even its species is called not its species. Rather without that it is strained in my eyes that the practical difference should be as the simple meaning according to R' Yehuda who holds species in its species is not nullified — for we do not hold like him; therefore in my humble opinion it deals with davar sheyeish lo matirin, for we hold species in its species is not nullified and in not its species it is nullified; and accordingly one understands why the Rama follows his view that he wrote in Turei Zahav: the reason they distinguish for davar sheyeish lo matirin between its species and not its species — Shach brought in siman 102 seif katan 4, and this is its language: the importance is not from the prohibition itself but because afterward he will have permission, etc., and whatever became mixed not in its species the permission is not called by the name of the prohibited thing but by the name of what it was mixed into — and it is as if it has no permitters, end quote. And if so in davar sheyeish lo matirin even not its species was not fit to be nullified except because the prohibition is called by the name of the permitted; if so in such a case even the any amount of absorption with another force mixed in is called not its species for R' Yehuda that species in its species from the Torah is not nullified by Torah decree, as stated in Sefer Nekudot; but for davar sheyeish lo matirin, since in any case it is called by the name of the prohibition even if not its species it is not nullified — therefore the Rama needed to say it became mixed afterward in not its species literally; and this is a straight reasoning in my humble opinion. But Sefer Nekudot — it must be according to his own view, for the Bach himself, etc., meaning the Bach too wrote this practical difference that the Rama wrote in glosses Orach Chayyim: chametz within its time, etc.:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ז": `In siman 447 — see there in Magen Avraham seif katan 42 who brought it. Permitted; and he wrote further there in Sefer Nekudot as stated that it is not davar sheyeish lo matirin because it returns to its prohibition next year, as written in Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah end of siman 102:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ח": `He did not look in the Mordechai, etc., they already challenged this above siman 447 seif katan 45 — see there:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:13:ט": `And even a piece forbidden because of any amount, etc., all of it is forbidden — meaning not as Sefer Nekudot holds that if it became mixed again in not its species it is nullified, for according to him the reason is davar sheyeish lo matirin that is nullified in not its species; rather even if mixed in its species it is nullified, for according to Sefer Nekudot that even for davar sheyeish lo matirin nevertheless that any amount with another force mixed in is called not its species as stated — if so it is nullified; but according to Magen Avraham that even in not its species nevertheless dry is not nullified — if so even if mixed in not its species literally it is not nullified, for the absorbed any amount is forbidden:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:14:_": `(seif katan 14) Permitted, etc., and also the Gemara, etc., because it is d'rabbanan — meaning the last Yom Tov is d'rabbanan, not because there are sixty — Yom Tov against chol:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:15:א": `(seif katan 15) All the dough, etc., meaning the Rashba came to resolve: since he does not press more than the amount of removal — if so why do some say all the dough or the matzah — behold it is explained above siman 447: its mixture is like other mixtures, etc., but if in other mixtures one did not need sixty but peeling or removal — so too in chametz; and since here in cold he does not press more than certainly the amount of removal — why did they forbid all according to one authority — on this he brought the Rashba's words and said: but here we find, etc., meaning now it fell and was not at the time of stirring within the dough — for it is explained seif 12 seif katan 18 that from time to time we do not say here it was found, etc.; but in a place of gravy, etc., and the water even though cold he holds the law of gravy applies to them — the Rashba holds that on Pesach even cold forbids, as Magen Avraham wrote in this seif katan:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:15:ב": `Nevertheless a small matter, etc., meaning if so one should not forbid because of the last taste — the bread itself leavened the matzah; nevertheless one should forbid because of the first tastes that he wrote:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:16:א": `(seif katan 16) Here, etc., now it fell, etc., meaning since it is from place to place we say: once found in matzah we say here it was found in matzah and was never in the dough at all:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:16:ב": `If so it appears it was in water, etc., meaning presumably it was in the water with which the dough was kneaded; and the Bach wrote: perhaps now it fell, etc., meaning even if the reasoning of here it was found, etc., was not accepted and one says because of this it certainly fell now — nevertheless one does not emerge from doubt to certainty; and with another safek added it becomes safek sefeika; and there is a practical difference between two reasons whether we permit through safek sefeika or through here it was found, etc. — Magen Avraham explained in this seif katan; but without softening everything is permitted — meaning even in hot, as Magen Avraham wrote seif katan 25 that we hold like Mar Ukva that even if they did not split open but are fit to split open they are forbidden; and since we are not expert whether they are fit to split open — but without softening it is certain they are not fit to split open — therefore it is permitted even for us:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:16:ג": `And it implies, etc., and he brought it to another house, etc., for it is from place to place; but if a grain was found in a barrel then it is from time to time. And according to the Bach's view, etc., and this is the practical difference between the Rama's reason and the Bach's reason that it is lack of knowledge — it should be that the safek the Bach wrote that perhaps it did not leaven is lack of knowledge, for one who understands and recognizes knows whether it leavened or not; nevertheless even if it certainly leavened behold there is a safek that perhaps now it leavened, as Magen Avraham wrote in the name of the Bach seif katan 25 that we say perhaps now it softened — and so in Sefer Ateret Zekenim; nevertheless there is a practical difference whether the grain was found in matzah that remained in his hand after baking — there is no need for perhaps now it leavened; but perhaps it did not leaven — one can say. But this is safek of lack of knowledge, for we do not call that safek. However Shach wrote in siman 453 seif katan 15 in the name of responsum of Mishna Berurah that in safek sefeika even two safeks from lack of knowledge — nevertheless in pressing circumstances we rely on this safek sefeika; and the reason Shach wrote in his general rules of safek sefeika in siman 110: in pressing circumstances when there is safek sefeika, even if it can be clarified, one need not clarify — if so lack of knowledge in safek sefeika does not impede; whereas Peri Chadash wrote there that Shach contradicts his own words in siman 455 seif katan 4 — there is resolution; and Peri Chadash himself holds safek sefeika does not help where there is lack of knowledge — he follows his view that where it can be clarified in safek sefeika one must clarify:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:א": `(seif katan 2) For if so, on the eve of Pesach at the fifth hour it is forbidden, for if he sells it then to an Israelite he will not finish it before Pesach:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ב": `And they challenged Rava from the mishnah: a garment in which kilayim was lost — behold it may not be sold to a gentile lest he sell it to an Israelite; since the kilayim are not recognizable — the same applies here to these grains:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ג": `For one does not buy bread, etc., and for us see below in seif katan 7:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ד": `In the incident in Pesachim page 40b regarding a ship of wheat that sank in the river — at first Rava also thought to sell them to a gentile, and Rava challenged him from this baraita of kilayim and retracted and permitted selling little by little as written in Shulchan Aruch; if so he already knew from the baraita of kilayim — how did he err a second time in the incident of stam yayin:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ה": `If so, why did he trouble them, etc. Granted, if both incidents did not come before Rava at one time — there is no difficulty why he did not permit in this incident of stam yayin to sell them little by little, for it is possible that at that time this permission did not occur to him and did not enter his mind; for such is the way of people that many times they understand things that previously did not enter their mind. But according to what Tosafos said that at one time he ruled on both incidents — since he ruled in this incident of chametz to sell them little by little, why did he trouble them in this incident of stam yayin and not rule for them also to sell little by little:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ו": `And it implies, etc., even one egg — for such is the language of the Gemara and the codifiers: one does not sell an egg of nevelah or tereifah to a gentile — they used the singular egg and not eggs plural. It implies there is no remedy to sell them little by little; and that which they did not give a remedy to grind them or bake them — it is possible that in their place the practice was to eat gentile bread; and then there is no remedy except to sell to a gentile a slice of bread as the Rama wrote at the end of siman 134 in Yoreh De'ah; and it is possible the wheat was abundant and it was not possible to sell so many slices, for buyers for slices of bread are not easily found; and to leave them a long time is forbidden because of spoilage:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ז": `And that which the Rama wrote, etc., in stam yayin that became mixed in water — for stam their wine is nullified in six parts water and then it is permitted even for drinking; and if it became mixed in water and there is not enough to nullify the wine — one sells them to a gentile except for the value of the stam yayin in it; and the Rama concluded: however one may not sell much together, etc. — it implies that little he does not have; if so it is proven that even in other prohibitions little by little is permitted:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ח": `They will taste the taste of wine. And even so, if an Israelite tastes them he will make a prohibition for the Israelite. This is not so, for one cannot say the gentile will sell it to an Israelite — the gentile knows if an Israelite tastes them he will perceive the taste of wine and the water will return to him, for he will claim it is a mistaken purchase. And even if you say nevertheless we are concerned — nevertheless this taste is forbidden only d'rabbanan, so there is no reason to be concerned for such a distant concern in a d'rabbanan prohibition:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:ט": `Rather his intent, etc., is that there are two remedies in the matter: one, the remedy of R' Shimon ben Gamliel mentioned in tractate Avodah Zarah page 74 — that one sells all of it to a gentile except for the value of the stam yayin in it. And the second, the remedy of R' Eliezer explained on page 49b — that one brings the benefit to the Salt Sea; see there in Yoreh De'ah siman 134 in the Tur and Beit Yosef and the distinction between these two remedies. For if one sells them except for the value of the stam yayin in it, one must sell everything at once; but through the remedy of bringing benefit to the Salt Sea one may sell them little by little; and for this R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim concluded: however one may not sell together; and that which the Rama concluded there lest he return and sell it to an Israelite is not from the words of R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim but the Rama added this from his own understanding according to the Rama's understanding. But Magen Avraham holds that what R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim wrote "however one may not sell together" means it is not permitted except through throwing benefit into the Salt Sea; and through that one is permitted to sell little by little. But the remedy to sell specifically all together — namely to sell it except for the value of the stam yayin in it — we do not hold this leniency (and according to the language of R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim that Rabbeinu Yosef brought there siman 134 it cannot be understood thus, and the text of R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim is not clear). But also his words require study, for Tosafos at the beginning of chapter 3 of Avodah Zarah, etc., means that R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim wrote thus in the name of Tosafos; and Tosafos' intent is not as R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim understood, for in Avodah Zarah page 49b: this man in whom a barrel of stam yayin became mixed in his donkey-loads came before R' Chiyya; he said to him: take four zuz and throw them in the river and I will permit you. And Tosafos wrote — and this is its language: take, etc., and sell them only by itself — he told him thus as I explained, end quote; and R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim understood that Tosafos' intent is specifically to sell them only by itself — namely through the remedy that one throws the value of the stam yayin — namely four zuz — and it is permitted for him; but he was not permitted to sell them at once through the remedy of except for the value of the stam yayin in it; and on this Magen Avraham wrote: on the contrary, Tosafos' intent is the reverse — for certainly if he wishes to sell them at once except for the stam yayin it is permitted; rather R' Chiyya came to be lenient for him and said throw four zuz in the river and through that he is permitted to sell little by little only by itself. But in the matter, etc., means according to what Magen Avraham explained the words of R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim — if so there is no proof from the words of R' Yosef Ish Yerushalayim to permit in other prohibitions to sell little by little lest he sell it to an Israelite; rather stam yayin is different, for an Israelite will not buy wine from a gentile except in pressing circumstances — unlike Shach in Yoreh De'ah siman 84 seif katan 17 who permitted also in other prohibitions to sell to a gentile little by little; see Peri Chadash. And see Yoreh De'ah siman 112 seif 1 who wrote: bread of an Israelite that a gentile baked without Israelite assistance is forbidden, and even to sell it to a gentile is forbidden in a place where they are accustomed to eat gentile bread lest he return and sell it to an Israelite — unless he cuts it into slices, for slices of bread are forbidden to buy from a gentile. It implies that even little by little it is forbidden to sell to a gentile in other prohibitions — since they needed to give the remedy to cut the bread into slices and did not write to sell little by little:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:י": `For it would be safek sefeika — meaning since he holds that there should be safek sefeika, it is like nullifying a prohibition l'chatchila:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:2:כ": `And it requires study, for here it deals with much chametz — for little by little is truly permitted; and therefore what he questions — behold it is safek sefeika — is to permit selling much together. And if so he used the safek that perhaps an Israelite will eat it before Pesach since it is much and will not finish before Pesach; for if it is possible it will finish before Pesach it is called little by little — it appears at first glance that he holds like the explanation of Rambam that if he holds like what Magen Avraham wrote that little by little is permitted — that even if he sells it to an Israelite the Israelite will finish it before Pesach — if so even little by little is not permitted except from the aspect of safek sefeika that perhaps he will not sell it to an Israelite, and even if he sells it perhaps the Israelite will finish it before Pesach; and he holds one may not make safek sefeika in one's hands. Or he holds like the explanation of Rambam that little by little is permitted, for we say he bought it for his own need and will not sell it at all; and according to this, for Magen Avraham who holds little by little is permitted because of safek sefeika — it must be said it is permitted to make safek sefeika in one's hands and he was not concerned for the questions of R' Yom Tov Lipmann that even much would be permitted lest the gentile sell to many Israelites little by little, for it is possible this is not a common safek — that presumably as he bought much together so he will sell it. And see Yoreh De'ah safek sefeika 57 there Shach in seif katan 91 on what the Rama wrote there: safek tereifah — there are those who permit it even though we hold to forbid; nevertheless it is permitted to sell it to a gentile, etc., since there are those who permit; and Shach wrote one may not be lenient in this except in pressing circumstances, for otherwise there are sides to permit; it implies but in another prohibition, even if there is a dispute in the matter, they do not permit selling it to a gentile; and the reason: even though there is one safek that perhaps the main law is like those who permit, and if you say like those who are stringent — perhaps he will not sell it to an Israelite — nevertheless it is safek sefeika made in one's hands and forbidden, for we do not say because it is safek sefeika of a dispute it is better than other safek sefeika; on the contrary, a dispute-safek is preferable to other safek sefeika; rather Shach's intent is because it is safek sefeika made in one's hands, as Rabbeinu Menachem proved in his questions on the novelties seif 2; and he wondered at what Shach wrote in siman 66 seif katan 10 that from his words it implies the reverse — see there; and see Peri Chadash in his questions general 18:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:3:_": `(seif katan 3) For since, etc., specifically regarding, etc., at end of siman 9 and in Magen Avraham there:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:א": `(seif katan 4) Lest, etc., even if one made it on Pesach it is permitted to sell it to a gentile, etc., even though it is forbidden to sell to a gentile lest he sell it to an Israelite — and it is that it has not yet leavened; nevertheless since it will leaven we are concerned lest he sell it to an Israelite. One might say from this Magen Avraham did not speak here but comes to inform us of permission for benefit, because before the measure of a mil it has not yet leavened and benefit is permitted; and regarding concern lest he sell it to an Israelite — from this he did not speak here and relied on what he wrote before that according to Rambam it suffices if he sells it to a gentile little by little. And according to Magen Avraham who requires that even if you say he will sell it to an Israelite the Israelite will finish it before Pesach — if so here where he sells it on Pesach he must sell it in a manner where there is no concern he will sell to an Israelite, such as that he grind it immediately before the selling Israelite:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ב": `But one does not buy flour from the market. Or he bake immediately — and certainly an Israelite will not buy leavened bread; and in any case, and according to the Tur one might say specifically above where it already leavened and the chametz is not recognizable — therefore they are concerned lest he sell it to an Israelite. But here where it has not yet leavened — if so it is safek sefeika: one, perhaps when it leavens it will be recognizable; and further, perhaps he will not sell it at all to an Israelite; and also see Magen Avraham in seif katan 12:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ג": `The incident, etc., a gentile is not believed even when speaking offhandedly:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ד": `After he sold them — and all the time they are in the gentile's possession the gentile is believed; but in what he says that he washed them to enhance his purchase — Shach brought there the dispute of the codifiers whether he is believed. But Kaf HaChayyim wrote here that it requires study whether what he says that he already washed them — one might say to enhance his purchase; on the contrary one might say in this his purchase is diminished rather than enhanced:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ה": `For after washing they are more beautiful than before washing; if so, if he had said he had not yet washed them — nevertheless they are beautiful on their own account — thus he would have praised his purchase:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ו": `As the Bach, etc., it is forbidden to leave them even if there are sixty, for chametz on Pesach in any amount; and even if he transgressed and left them they are forbidden because he transgressed Beit Yosef — meaning the prohibition in any amount is only d'rabbanan, namely for eating; but regarding that he transgressed Beit Yosef — for the chametz is in his possession — such is in the Bach; but we do not hold thus, as explained above siman 447; and so wrote Magen Avraham in this seif katan; and Magen Avraham did not bring all his words but only to learn from his words one law. And it is what he wrote in this seif katan: and one may learn that if, etc., as written in Yoreh De'ah siman 110: things that are not nullified because of their importance — nevertheless if one was lost or one was eaten of them, the remainder are permitted two by two, for there are two sides to permit: one, perhaps that one which was lost was the prohibition and there is no prohibition at all; and if you say the prohibition remained here — nevertheless since they eat two by two, in every eating there is one of them certainly permitted; and as one of them that was permitted, so too the second:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ז": `And in my humble opinion safek sefeika 447 in the name of Rashba that nevertheless it is like nullifying a prohibition l'chatchila, and Magen Avraham agreed there that even if he does not forbid more than the amount of removal — therefore it is properly two by two, for in every matzah there is more of the permitted than the prohibition, even though the baking of the permitted with the prohibition is in the same matzah; nevertheless he holds even if he does not do more than the amount of removal — nevertheless the permitted remains more than the prohibition and it is two by two. Unlike for us who hold that in its place it forbids everything; if so you are concerned the prohibition was not lost and the prohibition is in that matzah — if so there is no two by two, for the small amount of prohibition forbade the entire matzah. And according to what is written siman 447 seif 4 seif katan 11 and so safek sefeika 447: since it was already nullified in the majority and permitted to eat, so too before Pesach one may add to it up to sixty and it is not called nullifying a prohibition; and even though on Pesach it returns and revives — therefore he wrote grind them, and flour in flour is wet in wet; and we hold in siman 447 seif 4 that wet in wet does not return and revive; and grinding too is not nullifying a prohibition, for his intent is not to nullify but only to grind for the need of baking, as written above safek sefeika 447; and see there seif 453 and so in Yoreh De'ah siman 109 that flour in flour is called wet in wet:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ח": `And one may learn, etc., dry in dry, etc., and so each stands alone and was not baked together:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:ט": `For the third mixture — and see Shach seif katan 56:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:4:י": `But in wet it does not apply, etc., for once wet is blended — if so everything is forbidden and there is no permitted at all. And so three mixtures do not apply, for even in the third mixture there is certainly prohibition, since it is blended; and the taste of the permitted in the third mixture is because of safek sefeika: one, perhaps nothing of the prohibition fell into that mixture at all; and further, even if it fell perhaps what he eats is the permitted. But in wet that is blended — certainly some of the prohibition fell there and certainly he eats the prohibition:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:5:א": `(seif katan 5) We are not concerned that they go, etc. Know that what Rabbeinu Yosef wrote in seif 5: grain, etc., and one need not suckle, etc., and they receive leavening — these are the words of Rashba on what is written in Pesachim page 33a that if he separated terumah of chametz it is not sanctified, as written: you shall give to him and not to his fire. That is specifically when he had no opportunity, such as if it leavened while attached; and Rashba questioned: if so we would not eat matzah, for certainly rain fell on the grains while attached. Therefore he wrote specifically when they dried and one need not return to the ground — then they receive leavening. And in responsum of Me'il Tzedakah siman 69 he challenged Rashba with proofs and wrote: behold Abbaye said page 46a: this colander in which they roast (Rashi: as they dry nuts in an oven) upside down (meaning the mouth of the pot downward) is permitted, for the water coming out of the nuts does not leaven, for because of lifting them they do not leaven; but right-side up, where the mouth of the pot is upward, is forbidden, for water remains inside the pot and leavens. And Rava said even right-side up is permitted, for fruit juice does not leaven — meaning we hold like Rava; nevertheless it is possible Rava also agrees with Abbaye that because of lifting them they do not leaven; rather he added to permit even right-side up because fruit juice does not leaven. And if so one might say that even while the grains still need the ground they nevertheless receive leavening; only they stated generally that because of lifting them they do not leaven. Unless there was pouring rain until the crop bent and lay on the face of the ground — then there is no because of lifting them. Or we saw that it sprouted or split while attached — such is what the Gemara deals with when it said it leavened while attached, such as when we saw it sprouted while attached; but from the ordinary case, even if suckling from the ground is not needed, we are not concerned lest it leavened even if rain fell, for we say because of lifting them it does not leaven. And he wrote one may rely on this ruling, all the more so when it is a time of pressing need — see there where he expanded:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:5:ב": `And therefore wrote specifically when they dried and one need not return to the ground — then they receive leavening, as in Me'il Tzedakah responsum siman 69 challenging Rashba:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:א": `(seif katan 7) Only, etc., specifically from that which is in barrels; and he wrote in Sefer Ateret Zekenim and in Kaf HaChayyim: specifically when it is in a barrel with pitch — it is nullified in sixty at the time of cooking — meaning even though the honey was not sixty, nevertheless at the time of cooking they add water and there are certainly sixty; therefore they do not permit the honey in its substance even if it is in barrels; and so to make the drink if the honey was not in a barrel — namely that which they bring in small vessels — also forbidden, for even to make a drink they add water — nevertheless there is no sixty, for in those small vessels they increase flour there:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ב": `And to eat the honey in its substance in every manner is forbidden — meaning even if the gentile drew it from the hive before us and we knew he did not mix flour in it — it would be permitted by law; nevertheless it is forbidden because it comes to be confused with other honey, as in the Bach:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ג": `And it is forbidden to make the drink on Pesach. For on Pesach it is not nullified in sixty, for it is in any amount; and specifically if it was cooked before Pesach and nullified — it does not return and revive on Pesach, for wet does not return and revive — see Taz:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ד": `And the Levush, etc., but honey in its substance, etc., forbidden to eat since they did not add water — there is no sixty. And it appears to me one who is lenient has not lost, for according to the majority they mix only a little; and also the honey alone has sixty:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ה": `However honey, etc., also this is from the language of the Levush:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ו": `He inferred, etc., because that is forbidden, etc., and it is fitting to be stringent, as the Levush wrote above from this; and it appears to me one who is lenient has not lost and one who is stringent — may he be blessed; and it requires study:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ז": `Rather that which they bring in chararim, etc., and for the Bach this too is forbidden because it comes to be confused as stated; and the Levush was not concerned for this — see Magen Avraham seif katan 10:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:7:ח": `And there are those who practice, etc., and according to the Bach it is forbidden because it comes to be confused; and everything follows the custom. And in any case later authorities wrote that not all honey in all lands is the same — see Kaf HaChayyim and Ateret Zekenim:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:8:א": `(seif katan 8) And grapes, etc., they open the unripe cluster that refers to the exodus from Egypt; and they also say Shir HaShirim on Pesach:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:8:ב": `Here is a great difficulty: kalish, etc., and even though the verse, etc., they were not stringent from uncertainty — meaning even though above siman 447 seif 4 there regarding bread into which wine fell we are concerned lest crumbs remained, etc., and the same law here — this case is different, for there this safek of crumbs is close to certainty, unlike here where it is a full safek; and in a rabbinic prohibition in any amount they were lenient in safek. And as written in seif 9 in Magen Avraham seif katan 12; and what people practice, etc., and even apples cut with a knife — there is concern they were cut with a chametz knife; and it is explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 96 that apples are sharp things; nevertheless the water is permitted as Magen Avraham wrote above siman 447 seif 8 regarding olive water — see there:`,
  "machatzit-hashekel:9:_": `(seif katan 9) To leave it, etc., and this is the language of Maharil: Maharan permitted, etc., tsuker that he brought with him from Candia, etc., and this is the intent of the Rama; and tsuker kandil that has no concern for leavening — not our tsuker kandil; and so wrote the Taz — see there:`,
  "magen-avraham:1:א": `In the Tur it is written: and the chametz is not recognizable in them, etc. — it implies that if the chametz is recognizable it is permitted, and we are not concerned lest he grind and sell them, since buyers do not buy flour from the market except in time of distress, as in siman 453, as stated above, and not as the author of the Shulchan Aruch. And one informs him — but they do not rely on proclamation (Shulchan Gavoah chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah):`,
  "magen-avraham:1:ב": `So that it will be consumed. Meaning: even if you say he will sell it to an Israelite, the Israelite will eat it before Pesach; and the Kesef Mishneh wrote the explanation: when he buys only measure by measure for his household he needs it and it will be consumed before Pesach — there is nothing to fear — end of his words; and it requires study, for if so why does he say and it will be consumed before Pesach — it implies that if they know it will not be consumed before Pesach, such as selling it on the eve at the fifth hour, it is forbidden; and for the Kesef Mishneh it should be permitted, for he buys it for his household need; and further, in Avodah Zarah chapter 4 seif 5 it says regarding wheat over which rain fell, and Rava permitted selling to a gentile, and Rabah bar Rav Huna challenged him and he retracted and decided to sift and sell to a gentile not in the presence of Israelites (for they do not buy bread from gentiles); and Tosafos questioned: after Rabah already challenged him in the incident in Pesachim, how did he err here a second time? And Tosafos answered: both incidents came before him at once and he ruled in both to sell to a gentile, and Rabah challenged and he retracted — therefore it is difficult why he troubled them there to sift and sell — he should also have ruled to sell little by little to a gentile; and necessarily specifically for chametz little by little helps, for even if you say he will sell to an Israelite, the Israelite will eat it before Pesach; but for other prohibitions little by little does not help, for we are still concerned he will sell them to an Israelite or give as a gift to his Israelite friend; and further, you have nothing smaller than an egg, and nevertheless they do not sell an egg of nevelah or tereifah to a gentile; and it is implied from the Gemara's language and the poskim even one egg b'dieved siman 86, and in siman 84 the Rabbeinu Yosef brings flour that became wormy to throw into the river — it implies there is no remedy. And what the Rama wrote in siman 454 seif 5 regarding yeast that became mixed — nevertheless one may not sell much together lest he return and sell it to an Israelite — it implies little by little there is nothing to fear; behold these matters are taken from the words of R' Yehuda Vein, and I heard and did not understand — for R' Yehuda Vein never intended thus, for there they detect the taste of wine, and how will an Israelite buy from a gentile? Rather his intent is not to sell everything together to a gentile except the value of the yeast in it — see R' Yehuda Vein; and you will find like my words; but his words too require study, for Tosafos chapter 3 of Avodah Zarah only wrote that one need not sell it together except the value of the yeast, and if he wishes he may sell it together; and so too in Yevamot page 81b and end of Avodah Zarah and in Zevachim page 72 — and this is not the place; but for a matter where there is concern lest he return and sell to an Israelite, there is no permission in little by little except for chametz, as stated; and in Avodah Zarah siman 112 seif 1, and so in the name of Rashdam in Yoreh De'ah siman 45 and R' Yehuda Vein in Yoreh De'ah siman 2, and this is its language: and even though there is double uncertainty that perhaps the Israelite will eat it before Pesach, since it is uncertainty made by one's hands it is forbidden — end of his words; and it requires study, for it deals with much chametz; and one can say thus: perhaps the gentile will sell to many Israelites, to each a little — it appears he holds like the Kesef Mishneh's explanation that on little it depends that the gentile will eat it himself; and in Avodah Zarah double uncertainty 57:`,
  "magen-avraham:10:_": `Permitted to keep it. Because it is uncertainty of Yom Tov uncertainty of weekday (Tur); explanation: and rabbinic prohibition in any amount is rabbinic — therefore we are lenient to keep it; but full chametz even on the last Yom Tov is forbidden, as in siman 448; and also Hagahot Maimon who wrote the reason because it is rabbinic — they did not intend to be lenient except on rabbinic prohibition in any amount, siman 447 and Beit Yosef:`,
  "magen-avraham:11:א": `The entire dough. The Rashba siman 176 wrote: not in one place alone did the wheat kernel touch, but here and there through the rolling of the dough; and one should not say here it was found here it was (seif 12); and further the water in the dough carries what comes out from that grain throughout the dough — they did not say peeling except dry with dry; and so even in a place of moisture, most of the dough is like circumcision at the time of circumcision, but in a place of gravy they said gravy carries even a thing that does not spread; and so bread found in dough — the bread is apt to crumble and crumbs mixed in the dough, aside from that it itself may leaven others even though it is baked, only my mind does not lean that a small thing leavens all the dough, etc. — see there; it is proven he holds it forbids even in cold — see seif 12:`,
  "magen-avraham:11:ב": `Here it was found here it was. Explanation: we say now it fell into this matzah and was never in the dough; and if so, if the wheat kernel is full of water and moist through and through, it appears it was in water and in dough — if so one must forbid all the dough; and nevertheless one can say perhaps it was elsewhere swallowed from water and fell now into the matzah. And it appears all is according to the matter: if there is something to rely on, we rely — forever we rely on the common, whether to be lenient or stringent; and it is not called uncertainty except when there is equal reliance on this and that, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 55; and the Bach wrote, and this is its language: perhaps now it fell, and if you say it was before, perhaps it did not leaven — only if softened it is forbidden, but if not softened all is permitted — end of his words; and see seif 12. Mordechai Margaliot wrote in a responsum: one took cabbage in a bowl from a barrel and brought to another house, and there a wheat kernel was found on it — he permitted the cabbage in the barrel, for we say here it was found here it was — end of his words; but if a wheat kernel was found in the barrel, the cabbage in the bowl is also forbidden, for we say perhaps the wheat kernel was before in the barrel, as in seif 12 regarding the chicken; and similarly one who has dough in hand, cut matzot from it and gave to others, and one matzah remained in his hand and a wheat kernel was found on it — all are forbidden, for we say perhaps it was on it before; for specifically from place to place we do not establish prohibition; and according to the Bach who considers it single uncertainty — if so always permitted for this reason; and it appears to me this uncertainty did not leaven is not uncertainty of lack of knowledge, as the poskim wrote in Yoreh De'ah:`,
  "magen-avraham:11:ג": `Only the amount of removal. Even in matzah — and see siman 461:`,
  "magen-avraham:12:א": `The chicken is forbidden. For we say perhaps the wheat kernel was in water at the time of scalding — we do not say here it was found here it was, except from place to place or from piece to piece, as in siman 11; but in one place, such as scalding, where there is uncertainty in time and place — perhaps the wheat kernel was in water before — we do not say now it fell, for so we find chapter 1 of Chullin and chapter 1 of Niddah that we do not say now it deteriorated where there is no presumption; however what the Rabbeinu Yosef wrote in the name of the Mordechai: if a grain of wheat is found in a bowl near the chicken it is permitted because of double uncertainty (as the Rama wrote in the gloss) and not permitted because of here it was found here it was; and one can say: since it is common to find wheat kernels in chickens that eat wheat, we say perhaps it was in the chicken, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 57 we rely on the common — therefore it is permitted only because of double uncertainty — end of his words; Mishna Berurah siman 69 — and it is difficult, for if so even double uncertainty is not, for a common thing is not uncertainty at all, for even for leniency we rely on it, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 57, and so Orach Chayyim HaShalem; rather it must be specifically there it is very common, but a somewhat common thing we consider double uncertainty, as in siman 84; and if so the Mishna Berurah should not have brought from siman 57; and see in the author of the Shulchan Aruch siman 6 in the Beit Yosef that an uncommon thing is not considered double uncertainty; and it appears to me: even though it is common to find wheat kernels in chickens, when they scald them nothing remains inside, especially for Pesach need — therefore it is not called regular uncertainty; and that the Mordechai did not permit because of here it was found here it was, and in the bowl it fell — because the Mordechai holds a second vessel is forbidden on Pesach, as in siman 447 seif 3, and from time to time we establish prohibition; and if you say it was in the bowl when they turned the chicken into it, it was forbidden — therefore he permitted because of double uncertainty that now it is cold and does not forbid — it deals where there was no gravy there:`,
  "magen-avraham:12:ב": `And there is not enough to impart taste. It appears thus he says: the chametz is minimal and does not impart taste in the water — there is sixty in the water against it — therefore permitted; for since rabbinic prohibition in any amount we are not stringent in cold; and nevertheless some forbid, etc. And the Levush expanded to permit in cold, and wrote in all places he passed he saw great sages and elders who did not forbid in cold — end of his words; and Mahari Cohen that the Gaon Maharal of Prague ruled to permit, but Maharash of Austria wrote to Maharash of Lublin to forbid, and so most of the world practices, and in Krakow; and it appears it depends on custom — end of his words. And in truth, even though the Levush justified the Tur's words not to forbid in cold, behold the Mordechai forbade in roasting meat even if the wheat kernel was found after roasting — see there; and it is possible he forbade because there was gravy, as in siman 447; and so the Beit Yosef here in the name of geonim, and in siman 12 in the name of the author of the Or Zarua — see there; therefore it appears to me regarding meat where there is gravy it is forbidden by law in cold; and similarly if there is concern it dripped from above, also forbidden by law, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105; and in other cold cases it depends on custom or according to the matter of time of distress; and see seif 11 that in dough one should be stringent, and the same in dough because through kneading it leavens and imparts its taste throughout:`,
  "magen-avraham:12:ג": `And there is one who permits, etc. It appears from his words he holds the Rabbeinu Yosef forbids in cold even if found after removing the meat and it is not necessary:`,
  "magen-avraham:12:ד": `For we say perhaps afterward. The language is not precise — how is it permitted from uncertainty? Rather the reason: we establish presumption and say now it fell — so R' Yehuda Vein; and the Beit Yosef and R' Yehuda Vein wrote in the name of the Rashba: if a wheat kernel is found in a vessel in which they rinsed the chicken, we say here it was found here it was, like blemishes, and we do not say it came from the chicken; and so he ruled practically — end of his words; and it is difficult, for the Rashba ruled if a wheat kernel is found in matzah or dough they forbid the matzah or dough, as in siman 11, and we do not say now they fell; and it is difficult according to the Rama's explanation why he wrote we do not say it came from the chicken, etc. — he should have written we do not say it was before in water; and further it is not comparable to blemishes — therefore it appears to me it deals: they salted the chicken and afterward rinsed it in a vessel in cold, and afterward a wheat kernel was found in the vessel — there is concern it was in the chicken and was salted with it; for this he wrote we are not concerned, and brings proof from blemishes: we say in chapter 6 of Ketubot blemishes found in her father's domain — we say presumably there were blemishes already before she was betrothed; and if found in the husband's domain we say now they arose; here too, since the wheat kernel was found in the vessel we do not say it was in the chicken, for that is from place to place; but in matzah or dough it is from time to time, as written above; and if so there is no dispute at all — so it appears to me essentially:`,
  "magen-avraham:12:ה": `By force of double uncertainty. Perhaps now it fell, and if you say it was before in the chicken, perhaps it was digested; and those who disagree hold specifically for impurity digestion helps, not for prohibition, as written in Yoreh De'ah end of siman 83 — see there in Beit Yosef and so there in Shulchan Aruch; and in the Agudah in Bekhorot it is written: even for the answer of R' Meir of Pontremoli one may not permit a wheat kernel in a chicken, for presumably it swallowed it whole and it is not digestion — see there; it implies even if it broke afterward it is forbidden:`,
  "magen-avraham:12:ו": `And they roasted chickens there. It should read: not specifically chickens were mentioned, for here it deals with a whole wheat kernel that does not require digestion; if so there is no distinction between chickens and meat — if the wheat kernel is found after removing the meat from there, they do not forbid the meat:`,
  "magen-avraham:12:ז": `That dripped into water. Explanation: even though the wheat kernel was found inside while the meat was soaking in it, nevertheless it is rabbinic prohibition uncertainty, for some permit in cold — therefore one may permit other cooked dishes:`,
  "magen-avraham:12:ח": `Or was scalded. Explanation: and afterward the wheat kernel was found — one can say now it fell; and that which he ruled above seif 2 forbidden to eat because of double uncertainty — that is to eat the wheat kernels themselves, but the mixtures are permitted; and if so, if there is double uncertainty in matzah, forbidden to eat it. The Bach wrote: in single uncertainty even to keep is forbidden, even on the intermediate days; and in double uncertainty permitted to keep, and all the more to sell to a gentile, and not for eating that perhaps it was digested — this is not full uncertainty; some say digestion does not help in prohibition, as above; and specifically full chametz such as bread or dough, but a wheat kernel found in water — the cooked dish cooked in it before is permitted, for double uncertainty: perhaps now it fell, and if you say before it fell, perhaps it did not soften until now, and without softening there is no forbidding at all even according to our custom — therefore; and it is implied from his words that even in hot one does not forbid without softening — see there. An incident: one took into his mouth a piece of matzah and a piece of chicken and ate both together, and felt in his mouth there is a wheat kernel and removed the food from his mouth and saw a wheat kernel, and it is not recognizable if it was in the matzah or in the chicken — the chicken may be permitted by double uncertainty: perhaps now it fell to the chicken in cold, and if you say before, perhaps it was digested; but if it was in the matzah — necessarily it was inside at the time of its baking, for if it fell on it in cold it would roll and descend; and nevertheless it appears to me the matzah is also permitted, since rabbinic prohibition in any amount we suspend to leniency that it was in the chicken, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 111 regarding two pots: in one there is sixty — we rely on that which has sixty, and both are permitted; and nevertheless the amount of removal is forbidden by law from that matzah from the place he took the piece; and further it appears to me even a piece of meat is permitted, for we do not establish prohibition from place to place, and we say now it fell on the meat while still cold — if so the matzah is also permitted; but if the meat rested in a bowl and he ate from it, the meat is forbidden, as stated, for from time to time we establish prohibition — perhaps it was in the bowl when they turned the meat into it; and a second vessel forbids on Pesach; and if we say there is double uncertainty in the matzah and you say in the meat — perhaps now it fell — this is not uncertainty, for if you say it was in the matzah you must forbid the matzah, and we do not say this even for rabbinic prohibition, even in double uncertainty; and so Darkei Moshe siman 139; and if so the meat and matzah are forbidden; however according to the majority it is recognizable if the wheat kernel was cooked or baked or not — see Niddah page 3b regarding a box where a rodent was found, it implies if they cooked a cooked dish in a pot and removed it from the fire to another place and it congealed and a wheat kernel was found on top — we say now it fell, for we do not establish prohibition from place to place:`,
  "magen-avraham:13:א": `One-day-old. And it deals before its time of prohibition (Darkei Moshe); see siman 422:`,
  "magen-avraham:13:ב": `During Pesach. And the Bach wrote that even if it became mixed before Pesach it is forbidden, even though it is taste imparted by taste of the permitted; nevertheless in water it is not permitted for this reason, for easily he can find water; and so Yoreh De'ah siman 95; and it is possible even the Rabbeinu Yosef agrees to this, only before Pesach it is nullified in sixty:`,
  "magen-avraham:13:ג": `Because they became mixed. Explanation: even for those who permit cold, here it is forbidden because they became mixed — see siman 447 seif 5:`,
  "magen-avraham:14:א": `Rinsing suffices. For cold without gravy is permitted to all — see seif 11:`,
  "magen-avraham:14:ב": `After salting. Explanation: while he was still salting; but if found after he salted and rinsed, we say now it fell, for if so it had been on it before it would have fallen in the rinsing when he rinsed in water; and specifically found on the chicken, but if found inside the chicken certainly it was before; and see Niddah page 3 regarding a box that has rims:`,
  "magen-avraham:14:ג": `One peels all of them. It should read: the wheat kernel was found on the chicken — there is concern perhaps it touched all of them; but if found inside the chicken the others do not require peeling:`,
  "magen-avraham:14:ד": `And there are those who are stringent, etc. Because we assess in all prohibitions in salting at sixty, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105, because we are not expert between weak prohibition and fat — if so also in chametz it forbids entirely:`,
  "magen-avraham:14:ה": `But the essential ruling, etc. Because he holds we do not say in rabbinic prohibition that prohibition goes from piece to piece through salting; and what they assess in other prohibitions at sixty is because of the matter that ceased, that we need not assess between fat and weak — with this they are lenient, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105; if so also in chametz sixty suffices; and further, specifically in a prohibition where greasiness applies do they assess at sixty, but not in chametz where spreading does not apply; and further, where there is another side to permit, they rely on the question that chametz is nullified in sixty — you have no greater side of permission than that many geonim hold taste does not spread in salting; and one should make a compromise to forbid that piece, as in seif 11 to forbid that matzah and no more; and one who wishes to be stringent should rule to burn that piece and keep the others resting with it in the barrel; and further one may permit other pieces through benefit by redemption, as in siman 447 seif 1 — end of Rama in responsum siman 128; and so the Mishna Berurah siman 69; and he ruled if the others were cooked without peeling they are permitted, for it is only a stringency to peel them, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 91 — end of his words; and nevertheless it appears to me the piece requires peeling, only the peel does not forbid the piece even if cooked with it; and so is his intent in Yoreh De'ah there, as stated; and examine there that not like the later ones wrote. Also what the Mishna Berurah wrote here even without peeling is permitted — it appears specifically where there is no concern perhaps it touched the other pieces; but if there is concern perhaps it touched the other pieces, by law all are forbidden, as in Shulchan Aruch in Yoreh De'ah siman 105; and even if they know certainly it touched only one and we do not know which — by law all are forbidden and require peeling, for chametz on Pesach is not nullified in the majority, as in siman 447 seif 9; and if so if cooked without peeling it forbids in its mixture, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 69 and siman 98 — see there; specifically in the lower law the lenient prevails, for it is only a mere stringency, for at first they said in the Gemara peeling is not required, only they are concerned lest it absorbed a crumb — therefore the peel does not forbid the piece; but where peeling is required by law, if cooked without peeling it forbids the piece; and with this all the later ones' complaints on the Rama are resolved; and so in the Drisha. And it is written in responsum of Maharam of Lublin: a wheat kernel resting in a barrel of salted cabbage — the entire barrel is forbidden, for soaked is like cooked — end of his words; and it should read it deals where the brine rises over it; and if the brine does not rise over it, it has the law of salting, as in the Rama's responsum; and see siman 447 seif 5; and so I heard a wheat kernel was found in a barrel of fish salted for that purpose, and the Gaon R' Binyamin Av Beit Din of the holy community of Pozna commanded to throw that row into the river and the rest to sell to a gentile; and Mordechai Margaliot further wrote: before Pesach a full crop of wheat kernels was found in salted brine — even though the Rama ruled in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 that chametz on Pesach forbids only the amount of peeling, nevertheless I was not satisfied to permit that chicken, since there was much chametz there, etc. — end of his words; and the Bach's view is like the stringent in salting on Pesach, and so in Shiltei Gibborim; and in Darkei Moshe he wrote he heard in Krakow some are stringent and therefore did not wish to rule on this neither prohibition nor permission — end of his words; and the Mishna Berurah wrote he was in Krakow and did not hear they are stringent on this — therefore; and it is possible the Rama leaned toward that elder author of Shiltei Gibborim:`,
  "magen-avraham:15:א": `One cuts the place of its spreading. Question: behold he wrote in siman 447 seif 1 and there is no spreading thing, etc. — it implies if there is a spreading thing it forbids entirely; and it should read here too it deals that the chicken is weak:`,
  "magen-avraham:15:ב": `According to how they turn, etc. It requires study, for it is not so in Yoreh De'ah regarding other prohibitions; and one can say: even though it does not spread much, it spreads a little; and in other prohibitions it is nullified in sixty:`,
  "magen-avraham:15:ג": `Those touching one another. But if they do not touch one another they do not forbid, and we do not say absorption goes throughout the vessel, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 92 — there it deals with cooking; and if so if the chicken of its species even if they do not touch they forbid everything, as stated; and see siman 491 seif 12:`,
  "magen-avraham:16:_": `The bird is permitted. For roasting does not forbid more than peeling, and the crop is peeling — therefore one discards it; and the Bach wrote: according to what they practice to forbid in roasting, everything is forbidden — end of his words; and it appears to me in searing all agree it is permitted, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 68; and this is its language of Maharil: one of Maharash — women practice on Pesach to remove the crop before searing it, for they think searing is somewhat close to roasting, and if a wheat kernel is found in it there would be some concern for leavening; and some are not concerned — end of his words; and it is known Maharil holds to forbid entirely in roasting, and nevertheless he is lenient in searing b'dieved; it is written in Sefer Tanya: all matters of Pesach should not be done through a gentile:`,
  "magen-avraham:2:א": `For since they rested at ease. For specifically regarding what descends below it is not forbidden unless they split open, since it is under discussion:`,
  "magen-avraham:2:ב": `To leavening. And if it had not yet rested the measure of mil it had not yet leavened (and so in Talmud Yerushalmi siman 123 and in the Agur); and if so, even if it occurred on Pesach, one may sell it to a gentile before it rested the measure of mil. An incident: an Israelite bought wheat from a gentile, and afterward another Israelite came and asked the gentile to sell him wheat too, and he said I have no wheat, for the Jew I sold to is my friend and I sold him the wheat I washed and intended to grind for my household need and sold them to that friend — it appears similar to what is written in Yoreh De'ah siman 16 that a gentile is not believed in what he said after he sold and they left his hand; however if he is believed to him it is forbidden — end of his words; and see there siman 127. The Bach wrote in the Mordechai at length in the name of Ra'avyah: wheat that split open and their splitting is not recognizable that became mixed in a pile — forbidden to keep; and if he transgressed and kept them, they are forbidden after Pesach and forbidden to sell to a gentile; but if they ate from the grain before Pesach, permitted to eat on Pesach, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 110 seif 7 that one may eat the remainder two by two; and even if they did not eat from it, one may take from it and throw into the sea, and the rest are permitted; and there is no matter of nullifying prohibition l'chatchila, for Pesach has not yet arrived (and see siman 447 in the name of the Rashba); and even though one may eat only two by two, one by one is forbidden — here too you have no matzah that is not more than the measure of prohibition — two prohibitions — therefore all is in it he wrote that keeping mixed wheat is permitted; dry with dry is nullified — so the Bach for the law; and what Ra'avyah wrote when some were eaten that the remainder may be eaten on Pesach — because he holds it does not forbid more than removal; but for us who hold it forbids entirely — if so any amount of chametz remaining forbids all the matzah, for it imparts taste in any amount (Bach); and see siman 461; and according to what is written in siman 447 seif 9 that before Pesach it is nullified in the majority — if so permitted to grind before Pesach and nullify in sixty, siman 453 seif 3, and so Yoreh De'ah siman 109; and there is room to study in the Bach — see there. And one may learn that if dry mixed with dry on Pesach and one of them was eaten, it is permitted, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 110; and it is also possible that the third mixture is permitted, as written there; but in moist these permissions do not apply:`,
  "magen-avraham:4:_": `We are not concerned. For we follow the majority that they do not leaven:`,
  "magen-avraham:8:א": `There are those who are stringent. For we are concerned lest flour was mixed in; and even though honey is fruit juice, nevertheless the flour is full chametz, for they are accustomed to wash wheat:`,
  "magen-avraham:8:ב": `Only that, etc., that they make, etc. Explanation: two things are required — specifically from that in the barrel it is permitted to make a drink, for even if there is a little flour in it, it is nullified in sixty at the time of cooking before Pesach; but those they bring in small vessels are forbidden even to make a drink from them; and many times it was clarified to me they mix much flour in them. And to eat honey as is in any case is forbidden, for substitution will come; and so Maharil and Rashal; and forbidden to make the drink on Pesach (Bach, and so in Darkei Moshe); and the Levush wrote, and this is its language: some say specifically the drink called mead is permitted because it has much water and presumably the flour was nullified before Pesach; but honey as is that they cooked before Pesach — even though they were careful from leavening and strained it from the bees in it — forbidden to eat and to put into food; and it appears one who is lenient has not lost and one who is stringent — may he be stringent; and it requires further study. However honey they bring from the hive — all practice leniency, for there is no way to mix anything in it when they draw it from the hive — end of his words; he was precise and wrote they bring from the hive, and did not write they bring in a barrel from the hive, as written, because that is forbidden to eat as is, and only what they bring with small whole combs as they rest in the hive that was not yet crushed is permitted; and some practice to cook honey water until it returns and becomes thick like honey and eat it; and according to the Bach it is forbidden as stated; and all is according to custom:`,
  "magen-avraham:8:ג": `And dried grapes. Maharil wrote in responsum siman 105 not to eat dried grapes; and if a person wishes to fulfill what is written in Shir HaShirim open the pomegranate, he can fulfill with our grapes — end of his words. And in Maharil it is written one should be careful with thin grapes, for they mix them with raisins. In the holy community of Kalish they practice leniency from old times to buy small dried pears from gentiles and make a drink from them — meaning they pour cold water on them and soak until it becomes a drink; but to cook with the pears they practice prohibition, and also the pears themselves they do not eat; and the vessels with which they draw the drink they practice not to use for anything else. It appears the reason is concern lest a little chametz adhered to them — therefore they do not eat them, but the drink they drink, for that little is nullified in sixty; and even though they soak also on Pesach, nevertheless they were not stringent from uncertainty; and what they practice prohibition to cook them is because through cooking it will impart more taste; and even though there are gentiles who dry pears in an oven after they baked bread in it, nevertheless it is taste imparted by taste of the permitted; and even though the Rama wrote in siman 447 to be stringent on this — here it is uncertainty, for many dried in heat or after the oven was lit — therefore they practiced leniency, as stated, to justify their custom; but in other places they practiced stringency:`,
  "magen-avraham:8:ד": `Forbidden to keep it. Shach wrote, and this is its language: it requires study, for it appears to me from the Talmud Yerushalmi the reverse — specifically small combs made from kneading tsuker are forbidden, but stam tsuker is permitted to keep; and so I saw my teacher rule that tsuker that passed over Pesach he permitted — end of his words; and so in Hagahot Maimon: Maharil wrote in responsum siman 25: tsuker — they are accustomed to be careful if it is not great need for healing, for merchants mix many types in it — end of his words; and those made like small combs from tsuker kneading are forbidden to keep (in the name of Maharil and Moharash); and in Talmud Yerushalmi it is written one should be careful in all types of spices made from tsuker kneading, like legumes and coated seeds — end of his words; Hagahot Maimon implies he holds stam tsuker is permitted to keep; and so in the Levush — see there; and so I heard from the craftsman who prepares it that that made like a hat has no flour (and so the author of the Shulchan Aruch); however l'chatchila they practice to sell it to a gentile before Pesach, and b'dieved it is permitted; and Mahari Bruna wrote in a collection: I found a matter in taste that is the reverse — white tsuker has no concern for leavening, and tsuker kandiya has concern for leavening; and so I heard practiced in the holy community of Krakow — end of his words; and Maharil: Maharaz permitted his students to eat on the last Yom Tov tsuker he brought from Candia that was clear to him there is no concern for leavening — end of his words:`,
  "magen-avraham:8:ה": `Saffron. In responsum of the Rashba siman 133 it is written they are concerned for flour with water and pour on it; and the Levush wrote: what grows in his house garden is forbidden because of a decree (Maharil) — meaning because saffron is not common to grow in these countries, and they will think it is saffron that comes from afar; but fruits a Jew dried we are not concerned for substitution because it is common; and so with honey from the hive, as stated above — therefore Mahari Cohen; and Tuv Ta'am: the way is to soak it in beer and one must seal it in a room or make a partition before it — end of his words; and if so, the same for saffron:`,
  "magen-avraham:8:ו": `Their mixture does not forbid. It implies even if much mixed in a cooked dish it is permitted, since the concern is distant; however in honey Darkei Moshe wrote, and this is its language: however it appears that if a little of it mixed in a cooked dish on Pesach one does not forbid it, for it is fit to rely on the Rosh in such a case, as in siman 447; and regarding rabbinic prohibition in any amount we do not establish prohibition b'dieved — it implies if mixed in less than sixty it is forbidden, and so is essential; but in saffron and the like one should not be stringent even in less than sixty, for the taste of chametz already passed and went, as in siman 447 regarding dry meat, and Yoreh De'ah siman 69 regarding blood:`,
  "magen-avraham:9:א": `If they did not split open. For since there is a dispute in the Gemara — some say permitted without splitting open, and rabbinic prohibition in any amount — we follow uncertainties to leniency (Rif); it implies if mixed in less than sixty it is forbidden — see Yoreh De'ah siman 98; and Hagahot Maimon questioned: behold in seif 2 he ruled even if they did not split open it is forbidden; and one might say specifically to eat wheat kernels as they are, but to forbid the cooked dish they were not stringent; and it is difficult, since they are lenient only because of dispute, and necessarily they disagree only regarding what descends below, but where they rested at ease all agree it is forbidden — if so why were they lenient in the cooked dish? And one can say: he wrote above it comes to leavening — it implies they will come to leavening but are not yet certainly chametz, only uncertainty — therefore they permit the cooked dish; see Tur. The Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Kol Bo: if three barley kernels are found in a cooked dish, presumption there is much and perhaps they split open (Darkei Moshe); and it appears to me they omitted this intentionally in Shulchan Aruch, for we do not hold thus, for the Taz wrote in Yoreh De'ah siman 84 seif 9 in the name of Rashal that we do not say thus except regarding worms that multiply from their kind, but regarding a matter that comes from outside no; and further, one can check if barley is in it; however in a pile of wheat over which rain fell, one can say this reasoning — see there seif 2:`,
  "magen-avraham:9:ב": `They forbid everything. The cover and the spoon — and similarly other cooked dishes mixed from that cooked dish; and so they practice (Bach); and so the Tur in the name of Rokeach: a wheat kernel found in a pot — the spoon with which they stirred in it is forbidden; if placed in other pots, all are forbidden — end of his words; and so siman 12; and the Taz in Yoreh De'ah siman 92 wrote that if a piece was forbidden from chametz in any amount and fell into another pot, it does not forbid through its release — see there; and he was excessive in his measures that everywhere they practice to forbid; and so Mahari Cohen in Hagahot Orach Chayyim of the Rama; and even though the piece does not impart taste, nevertheless regarding the prohibition it forbids in any amount; and there are ramifications, such as a thing made for its taste is not nullified, and where it is not made for taste it is nullified — chametz within its time in any amount, and after its time in sixty; or regarding if mixed in its species and not in its species — we say it ascended from it as if it is not — end of his words; and so the Bach; and see in the Drisha — nevertheless what he wrote chametz within its time, etc. — explanation: if any amount of chametz mixed in a cooked dish during Pesach and he kept it until after Pesach and mixed from that cooked dish into another cooked dish, sixty is not required, only against the any amount; and it is difficult, for the Bach himself wrote in siman 447 that a thing forbidden in any amount that passed over Pesach is permitted without mixture; and in Sefer Nikudat HaKesef it is written: and if it was intentional — meaning when it passed intentionally it is forbidden; but according to siman 447 even if intentional it is permitted — see there; also what he wrote siman 55 that chametz b'dieved forbids in its species in any amount because it is deishilam — the Talmud Yerushalmi already decided it is not deishilam; and so siman 461 regarding double matzah; also what he wrote dry with dry is not nullified on Pesach because deishilam, and not in its species is nullified in sixty — he did not examine the Mordechai that wrote explicitly the reason: as moist in any amount, so dry; and so Yoreh De'ah, and so Talmud Yerushalmi; and if so even not in its species it is not nullified; and even a piece forbidden because of any amount that became mixed with others dry — all are forbidden:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:1:א": `(1) Grain that was submerged, etc. — in the Tur it is written and the chametz is not recognizable, etc. It implies that if the chametz is recognizable it is permitted both when selling to a Jew and when selling to a non-Jew, for there is no further concern that he will return and sell it to a Jew and he will eat it on Pesach, for the Jew will recognize it; and there are among the poskim who are stringent lest the non-Jew grind it and sell them — however, for our custom that we do not buy flour for Pesach from the market, several Acharonim lean that there is no concern; and in a place of pressing need one may be lenient:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:1:ב": `(2) And he informs him — but one does not rely on the proclamation, for perhaps there is someone who did not hear the proclamation. And when he informs him it is permitted to sell to him even a large amount; and without notification, even a small amount — it is forbidden to sell to him lest he come to eat it on Pesach:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:1:ג": `(3) So that it will be consumed, etc. — that is, even if you say he will sell it to a Jew, the Jew will eat it before Pesach; and according to this, on the eve of Pesach in the fifth hour in a matter where it is impossible that it will be consumed before Pesach, it is forbidden to sell to a gentile even a small amount, lest he sell that small amount to a Jew; and nevertheless in a place of great loss several Acharonim agreed that all this is with an ordinary gentile; but a gentile known not to touch it but only to guard it until after Pesach and return it to him, as was explained in siman 448 — a Jew may sell him a large amount of leavened grain:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:1:ד": `(4) Before Pesach — and in other prohibitions where their prohibition is not recognizable, it is forbidden to sell to a gentile even little by little; only here where there are several uncertainties they were lenient [Acharonim]:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:10:א": `(41) Permitted to sell to a gentile — after he removed the wheat kernel from it and only the taste of chametz that the wheat kernel released in the chicken remains; and he may not retain it in his house lest he come to eat it:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:10:ב": `(42) And in these countries, etc. — and in a place of very great loss the Acharonim agreed to be lenient according to the Mechaber's view, as written above at the beginning of siman 447:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:10:ג": `(43) And if it was found, etc. — that is, even if the mixture occurred from before the eighth day; and the same applies even if one transgressed and retained until this day:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:10:ד": `(44) Permitted to retain it — because it is an uncertainty of Yom Tov or weekday, and a mixture of any amount is rabbinic; therefore we are lenient to retain it until after Pesach, and then it is permitted even in eating [and some say that even on the seventh of Pesach if a split wheat kernel is found in a cooked dish or in a chicken, one covers them with a vessel until after Yom Tov, and then one eats the cooked dish or the chicken and burns the wheat kernel; and in a place of loss one may rely on this]; and the same applies regarding flavor imparted for deterioration — even though we are stringent on Pesach as explained in siman 447, nevertheless it is permitted to retain it; but complete chametz forbidden d'oraisa — even on the last day it is forbidden to retain it:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:11:א": `(45) Split open — and for us, if it softened even though it did not split open — and specifically regarding eating; but in benefit one may be lenient in great loss without split open, even without removal of place:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:11:ב": `(46) The amount of removal of place — for its edge is judged like roast, which forbids the amount of removal; and in dough, even though it is cold, nevertheless we say that through the pressure of kneading something releases around its place:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:11:ג": `(47) The entire dough — for through kneading it heats somewhat, and the water in the dough carries the release of the wheat kernel that released through the pressure of kneading throughout the entire dough:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:11:ד": `(48) To forbid that matzah, etc. — and even though the implication of the Rama's rule is that he forbids all of it even in great loss, nevertheless if it softened and did not split open, perhaps one may be lenient in great loss through removal of place:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:11:ה": `(49) That a wheat kernel was found in it — that a wheat kernel was found in it when it was baked, as it should read. And this is whether the wheat kernel was found in the matzah after baking, or when it was still dough after being formed, or in a piece of dough; and we do not say that the wheat kernel was initially in the large dough and all of it became forbidden — rather here it was found, here it was, meaning where the wheat kernel is found now in this matzah or in this piece of dough, there it was initially when it fell (that is, after this matzah was cut from it the wheat kernel fell into it), and it never fell into the large dough — for we do not establish prohibition from place to place. And if the wheat kernel is full of water and moist through and through, then it is recognizable that it was in water, and in the dough one must forbid the entire dough; and nevertheless if there is room to suspend that it was swallowed from elsewhere from water and fell into the matzah — everything is according to the circumstances, for we suspend in what is common both to be lenient and to be stringent, and it is not called an uncertainty at all. And one who had dough and cut from it several matzos and gave to others, and one matzah remained in his hand and a wheat kernel was found on it — all the matzos from that dough are forbidden, for we say perhaps it was in that dough before he cut it into pieces; for although we do not establish prohibition from place to place and we say here it was found, here it was — we do not say from piece to piece from time to time do we establish prohibition, and we say perhaps it was in this dough before it was cut [Magen Avraham and other Acharonim]; and there are among the Acharonim who hold that even here one may be lenient regarding the other matzos because of a double uncertainty, lest it fell now; and if you say from before, perhaps it did not become leavened until now; and in a place of great loss one may rely to be lenient. And Magen Avraham cited above that this is the law in one who took cabbage from a barrel in a bowl and brought it to another house or another place, and there a split wheat kernel was found in the cabbage in the bowl — the cabbage in the barrel is permitted, for we do not establish from place to place and we say here it was found, here it was — meaning it fell in the bowl now; but if the wheat kernel was found in the barrel, then even what is in the bowl in the other house is forbidden, for this is from time to time and we establish prohibition retroactively. And in any case in a place of great loss one may be lenient even if found in the barrel for those householders who took previously, for there is a double uncertainty — lest it fell in the barrel now; and if you say from before, perhaps the wheat kernel did not become leavened until now; if they did not take immediately when found in the barrel, there was no time to leaven in that period. And there are Acharonim who wrote that all this is when the wheat kernel is split open; but if it did not split open, only softened — one may permit even the cabbage in the barrel if there is great loss there, by force of double uncertainty. And if the brine does not rise upon the cabbage, in any case one may permit the cabbage in the barrel, for it is not considered pickled there but ordinary salting, and it is forbidden only at the place where it is found, and the rest is permitted, as the Rama wrote in the gloss in seif 4. Chayei Adam wrote: pickles and kvass — the custom in our country is to make them for Pesach; one must be careful to sort the pickles before adding water, for it is common to find kernels in them:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:11:ו": `(50) And specifically during Pesach — when chametz on Pesach is in any amount; but before Pesach the taste of the wheat kernel is nullified in sixty; and nevertheless for removal of place one must remove from it, lest it released only around it:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:11:ז": `(51) Only the amount of removal — even if found in baked matzah, the amount of removal suffices:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:א": `(52) If they scalded, etc. — even though it was explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 68 seif 1 one may not scald even in a second vessel, here it deals that it was scalded beforehand on the feather [Peri Megadim]:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:ב": `(53) The chicken is forbidden — for we say perhaps the wheat kernel was in water at the time of scalding; we do not say here it was found and here it was, except regarding from place to place or from piece to piece, as above in seif 11; but in one place, such as where there is room to suspect in time and say perhaps the wheat kernel was in water previously — we do not say it fell now. And behold the Mechaber wrote here split wheat kernel, and the Rama did not gloss on this at all as he glossed above in seif 9: some say here he agrees with the Mechaber that specifically when split open and not when softened, because there is another uncertainty lest it fell now; and some say the Rama relied on his gloss above in seif 9; and so is implied in the Taz that he did not permit in our matter when softened except in benefit and not in eating; however I found in Beit Me'ir that this law that the Mechaber left in dispute is stated (apart from the view mentioned in the Rama); and therefore it appears in my humble opinion that one who relies to permit in this when softened in a second vessel even for eating has not lost. And if it did not soften, even if the scalding was in a first vessel, it is also permitted:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:ג": `(54) And there is not in it, etc. — the water is cold; meaning, two things are required: that the chametz be minimal such that it does not impart taste in water where there are sixty in the water against it, and also that the water be cold; and therefore we are lenient even during Pesach, for since any amount is rabbinic we are not stringent in cold:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:ד": `(55) Permitted — and if it is known that the wheat kernel fell from the outset into water, it is forbidden, for pickled is like cooked. And even if there is room to suspect lest it fell from the outset into water, the view of some Acharonim is to be stringent; and nevertheless in a wheat kernel that is not split open, only softened — one should not be stringent in uncertainty. And even in split wheat kernel — specifically when the wheat kernel was found and afterward they cooked in that water; but if the wheat kernel was found in the water after they cooked, one may be lenient in any-amount prohibition, for it is a double uncertainty — lest the wheat kernel fell afterward; and if you say from before, perhaps it did not fall from the outset into water and is not pickled — it is permitted according to this view in cold, and even according to the one who forbids in cold during Pesach; at least in a place of pressing need or great loss one should not be stringent, as below:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:ה": `(56) If it is during Pesach — those who hold that even in cold it does not impart taste — nevertheless there is any amount; and therefore during Pesach, when its prohibition is in any amount, it is forbidden; and specifically when the wheat kernel was found and afterward they cooked in that water; but if found after they cooked, one should not be stringent in cold because of uncertainty:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:ו": `(57) For we say perhaps, etc. — meaning, since any amount is rabbinic we are lenient in its uncertainty and suspend that it fell afterward; and the same if the chicken is still in the water but the water cooled — we suspend that it fell when the water cooled, and it is permitted according to those who permit in cold:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:ז": `(58) In other meat — in such a case where they left it in hot water after salting and rinsing, and after they took it from there a split wheat kernel was found there — we establish from time to time and say the wheat kernel was there before they took the meat:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:ח": `(59) By force of double uncertainty — lest it was in the chicken itself and was already digested, and leavening is not applicable to it; and if you say it is not from the chicken, lest it fell now; and the Mechaber's first view holds that even in a wheat kernel that is from a chicken, leavening is applicable:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:ט": `(60) Whole — for in a whole one we decide there is leavening, because it is recognizable that it was not digested:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:י": `(61) And they removed the chickens from there — and after the chickens were removed from there they found a split wheat kernel there, even whole; and he cited chickens not specifically, for the same applies to other meat too — one does not forbid because of uncertainty, and we suspend that after taking the meat from there the wheat kernel fell, since the water was cold; and some permit entirely in cold:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:כ": `(62) One does not forbid because of uncertainty — but if it is certain that the wheat kernel was when the meat fell into the water, his view is that one should conduct according to the one who forbids in cold during Pesach; and see in Levush and Taz and other Acharonim whose view is like the Mechaber's first view mentioned, that one does not forbid in cold in every manner; and therefore in a time of pressing need or great loss one may rely to be lenient according to them, and especially if the wheat kernel is not split open, only softened — certainly one should not be stringent. And all this is with a wheat kernel; but a slice of bread or dough found in the water — the Acharonim wrote to forbid even in cold, for the nature of a slice of bread and dough is to crumble and dissolve in water:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:ל": `(63) And one may permit other cooked dishes, etc. — it implies that that cooked dish itself, even in great loss and prevention of Yom Tov rejoicing, is forbidden with a single rabbinic uncertainty [and see Biur Halacha]; however in the double uncertainty mentioned above it is permitted even without great loss; and all this is in a single uncertainty of any-amount prohibition which is rabbinic; but in double uncertainty of Torah prohibition one may permit only through retention, and all the more so to sell to a gentile but not in eating; and specifically where retention is possible; but if retention is impossible lest it spoil thereby — it is permitted even in eating; and especially where there is certainly a medicinal drink — certainly one should not be stringent:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:12:מ": `(64) That fell into water or was scalded — it is not in one manner: if the meat fell in cold, even if the kernel is found while the meat is still there, it is also permitted, for there is a rabbinic uncertainty lest in cold there is no release and absorption at all. And was scalded deals when found afterward a wheat kernel in the water after they removed the meat or chicken from there — and it is permitted even if scalded in a first vessel; and the reason is there is uncertainty lest it fell now, and it is any-amount prohibition which is rabbinic — therefore they permit where there is a medicinal drink:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:13:א": `(65) Day-old — and it deals that they performed hag'alah before its time of prohibition; for otherwise one may not perform hag'alah on a day-old vessel, as explained above in siman 452; and the Mechaber cited day-old because if the pot was not day-old, even the hag'elah water itself is permitted on Pesach, since they hag'eled before Pesach and they are not day-old, as explained above in siman 451 and siman 447 seif 5 in the gloss. See there. And know that if the hag'alah was during Pesach, then even if the pot is not day-old, the water in the pit is forbidden according to the Rama above in siman 447 seif 10 in the gloss:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:13:ב": `(66) And they descended, etc. during Pesach — that is, the descent was during Pesach, but the hag'elah was before Pesach; and if the descent was also before Pesach, the water was nullified in sixty in the water in the pit; and there are those who are stringent that even if there are sixty against them, one may not use that water l'chatchila, since one can easily find other water. Forbidden to drink its water — and if one transgressed and cooked from them a cooked dish, Peri Megadim wrote one may permit if it is great loss, since the hag'elah was before Pesach:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:13:ג": `(67) Because they became mixed — explanation: even for those who permit in cold above because it does not impart taste at all — nevertheless here it is forbidden because through becoming mixed, behold the body of chametz is mixed in it. If grain kernels are found in a water well on Pesach, the Acharonim wrote it is proper to be careful in everything possible not to use that water on Pesach even for those who permit in cold, lest it was pickled in it from the outset and is like pickled; and nevertheless if he has no other water, since his life depends on it, one may permit because of double uncertainty — lest it fell now; and if you say it fell from the outset previously, perhaps it did not split open and soften until within the outset and did not become pickled from when it became chametz; and we rely on the poskim that cold does not forbid without pickling. And a slice of bread found in a well — see above in note 62. One who wishes to draw on Pesach from wells of non-Jews or from wells of Jews not guarded from chametz all year — it is proper that he strain the water through a clean garment each time he draws:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:14:א": `(68) Split wheat kernel — see in Biur Halacha that for us this seif applies even when only softened:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:14:ב": `(69) Rinsing suffices — for cold without gravy, according to all it does not forbid; and even if the chicken was rinsed, nevertheless since the wheat kernel was not steeped in water it does not forbid; and nevertheless rinsing is required, and one may not rely on the rinsing they rinse after salting:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:14:ג": `(70) Peeling suffices — and nevertheless peeling is required even though it is cold, for we rule salting is like boiling and it absorbs the moisture of the wheat kernel into the meat to the depth of peeling. And all this when found while still salting on it; but if found after it was salted and rinsed, we say it fell now, and rinsing alone suffices; for if it had been on it previously it would have fallen during rinsing when they rinsed the meat in water. And specifically when found on the chicken externally; but if found inside the chicken, presumably it was previously, even at salting time, and what prevented it from falling during rinsing was that the chicken's edges held it back:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:14:ד": `(71) And there are those who are stringent to say one deepens — meaning, not like the first view that it suffices to peel the meat around the place of the wheat kernel alone; rather one deepens all around it and cuts; and the reason is since on Pesach its prohibition is in any amount, we say something spreads in all the surroundings of the place of the wheat kernel:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:14:ה": `(72) He peels all of them — and it deals when the wheat kernel is found on the chicken, for there is room to suspect lest the wheat kernel touched all of them; but if found inside the chicken, the others do not require peeling:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:14:ו": `(73) And there are those who are stringent to forbid all, etc. — because we are stringent to forbid in all prohibitions in salting in sixty [because we are not expert between weak prohibition and fat prohibition] — evidently it spread throughout; if so, automatically in chametz whose prohibition is in any amount, it forbids all of them:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:14:ז": `(74) But the essential view — because those hold we do not say in any-amount prohibition which is rabbinic that it goes out from piece to piece through salting; in chametz which is weak, they were stringent only at the measure of sixty to forbid from piece to piece even in weak, and not in any amount. And further that specifically in a prohibition where fat applies do we estimate weak even at sixty, because we are not expert between weak and fat, and we say it spreads until sixty — unlike chametz where spreading is not applicable at all, and our judgment is if we are stringent to forbid that entire piece:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:14:ח": `(75) Through some peeling — and this is in a manner where there is room to suspect lest the wheat kernel touched them too, as above in note 72; and even if we know for certain it touched only one of them and we do not know which one it touched — all require peeling, for chametz on Pesach is not nullified in the majority, as written siman 447 seif 9; and if they were cooked without peeling, their mixture is forbidden:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:14:ט": `(76) To sell all, etc. — see in Acharonim whose view is that the Rama refers only to the other pieces; but that piece on which the wheat kernel was found — one may not permit to sell according to the gloss above in note 10; and from other Acharonim it is implied that here where it is only through salting we are lenient to sell even that piece, only that one must peel first from the meat at the place where the wheat kernel was found:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:14:י": `(77) To a gentile — see in the Taz who wrote he ruled practically that the same applies: permitted to retain the other pieces until after Pesach, since they are only uncertainty of contact; and also that piece one may retain, only that in it one must peel first the place of the wheat kernel; and from the words of Magen Avraham it is implied that his view is like the Rama's view that that piece is forbidden to retain; and it appears that when softened and did not split open, one may rely on the words of the Taz; and Kaf HaChayim agrees to the words of the Taz:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:14:כ": `(78) As written siman 447, etc. — it is a textual error and should read: even though we are not accustomed thus in other chametz mixtures, as written siman 447 seif 1; and its explanation is that there it is explained that in absorption of any-amount chametz one may not sell to a non-Jew even after redeeming the value of the chametz; here in salting we are lenient in this. And know that all this where the Rama is lenient regarding the other pieces through peeling or through selling to a non-Jew — specifically when the wheat kernel is found on salted meat at the time of salting, or after salting and rinsing and they salted again for the pot and they found the wheat kernel on the meat without brine; but if the wheat kernel is found inside the brine, then everything is considered cooked, for in a slight measure it is like cooked through brine — then everything resting in the brine must be forbidden and requires peeling; but what rests above the brine one does as the Rama wrote, that in brine we do not say it spreads upward or that one retains until after Pesach — as we wrote above according to the Taz; and likewise if a wheat kernel is placed in a barrel of salted cabbage, the entire barrel is forbidden, for pickled is like cooked; and specifically when the brine rises upon it; but if the brine does not rise upon it, it is ordinary salting law, and its law is as above in notes 76 and 77. If a little beer remained in a vessel and they took water into it and rinsed other meat in it after its salting on Pesach, the view of Kaf HaChayim is to permit the meat even without peeling from essential law, for although the water was forbidden because of the beer mixed in them, nevertheless the meat did not absorb anything from those waters, since it is cold; and even though the meat was salted, nevertheless at the time they rinsed it, and salting is like boiling — in any case it did not absorb from the water, because the water with which they rinsed nullifies the power of the salt and it has no power to absorb water into the meat; however because of the stringency of chametz on Pesach, one should peel a little of the meat where those waters touched it. And the view of Chayei Adam is that by law the meat requires peeling, since there is release; and even if the meat was not salted at all:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:15:א": `(79) That was found in it, etc. — Chayei Adam wrote: meaning specifically that it was found inside it, for one can explain that it became leavened in the water in which it was rinsed; but if found on the chicken, then if it had been at rinsing time it would have fallen — therefore it fell now; and chicken fat is like fruit and does not leaven:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:15:ב": `(80) Split wheat kernel — and according to the Rama's words above in seif 9, the same applies even if it did not split open, only softened; but if it did not soften — nothing is required:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:15:ג": `(81) One cuts the place of its spread, etc. — that is, as written above that one deepens all around it; for in roast, according to all, one must be stringent more; and specifically if the chicken was lean; but if it is fatty, at the place of contact of the wheat kernel the whole thing is forbidden, for the permitted part goes and seasons the wheat kernel, and afterward the wheat kernel releases something throughout the entire chicken:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:15:ד": `(82) Because when they turn over, etc. — and specifically in chametz prohibition which is in any amount we are concerned for this, unlike other prohibitions:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:15:ה": `(83) The spit — it implies that if it was roasted over coals, one does not forbid more than the amount of its spread; and according to the Rav in the gloss mentioned above, certainly one may not be lenient in roast more than in salting [Acharonim]:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:15:ו": `(84) Touching one another — and if they do not touch one another they are not forbidden, and we do not say that absorption of any amount goes and spreads from one to another through the spit; and if the chicken is fatty it forbids everything even if they do not touch [Magen Avraham]; and Mekor Chaim is lenient in the matter; and even according to Magen Avraham one should not be stringent in this except with split open and not when softened:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:15:ז": `(85) For through turning over, etc. — and without turning over the others are permitted even without peeling; and Peri Megadim wrote that according to the Rav above one should rule that roast is no worse than salting, and even when the chicken is entirely lean it is entirely forbidden even without turning the spit; but the others one may permit in great loss if known that he did not turn the spit:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:16:א": `(86) The bird is permitted — for roast does not forbid more than the amount of peeling, and the crop is at the place of peeling and they discard it; and even for us who forbid in roast up to sixty — nevertheless one should not be stringent in this more than the amount of peeling, for singeing is not at all similar to roast, since one does not singe much in one place but passes it constantly back and forth, as explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 68; and if the wheat kernel was found outside the crop after singeing — l'chatchila one must peel at that place; and b'dieved if one cooked thus without peeling it is permitted. The meticulous are careful on Pesach before singeing and before salting to open the chicken well or cut it in two and check well that no wheat kernel is found inside; and one who does so is zealous and rewarded [Knesset HaGedolah]:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:16:ב": `(87) One must burn them — for we suspend they became leavened from the salt found in the crop [Levush]:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:16:ג": `(88) For they are not considered digested — for although regarding impurity they are considered digested, regarding prohibition it is different; and see in Biur HaGra who wrote that according to this there is no distinction between whole or broken; but in essential law he tends at the end that one should not distinguish between prohibition and impurity, and even whole ones are considered digested. It is written in Sefer Tanya: all Passover matters should not be done through a non-Jew; and likewise it is proper they should not be done through minors [Peri Megadim]:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:2:א": `(5) In a river — and whether it is permitted to nullify this grain in the majority or in sixty before Pesach, since it is a time of permission — see above in siman 447 seif 1 in Mishna Berurah and siman 453 seif 3 in Mishna Berurah:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:2:ב": `(6) That water fell on it — and even a small amount; and see in Biur Halacha:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:2:ג": `(7) Even though it did not split open — behold in seif 9 the Mechaber wrote even though it did not actually split open — that is, even if it softened such that they are close to splitting; and here he changed his language and wrote even though it did not split open, to teach that even though it did not soften at all, nevertheless it is forbidden:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:2:ד": `(8) For since it rested at ease — meaning, even though regarding sweetness there are poskim who hold it is not forbidden unless they split open, nevertheless according to all we require that they be close to splitting — that case there is different because for sweetness one is engaged with it and does not leave it so much to become leavened, unlike here where it rested at ease:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:2:ה": `(9) Leavening — and specifically when it rested the measure of time for leavening; but if it has not yet rested the measure of time for leavening, it has not yet leavened; and if so, even if it occurred on Pesach it is permitted to sell it to a gentile before it rested the measure of time for leavening; and specifically with intent to eat immediately. A non-Jew who told a Jew after he bought wheat from him that it is for sweets is not believed, even if he speaks guilelessly, since he said this after he sold them and they left his possession; however, if the non-Jew has a presumption of trustworthiness in his eyes, it is forbidden, even if he was not speaking guilelessly:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:2:ו": `(10) And rain descended — that is, enough that there is room to suspect that much became moist; but if a small amount of water or snow fell on the grain before grinding and its place is known, one scrapes off the upper wheat kernels and the rest is permitted:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:2:ז": `(11) And a double uncertainty does not help — and see in the Acharonim who agreed one should not be stringent in this except in a place where there is no loss in retaining them after Pesach; but in a place of loss or prevention of Yom Tov rejoicing, one may permit even in eating:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:3:א": `(12) Dry and hard — and nevertheless if it is known that water came upon them, one must forbid the wheat kernel itself even though its appearance did not change at all:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:3:ב": `(13) And its appearance did not change — but if its appearance changed or they are moist, it is forbidden even to retain them:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:3:ג": `(14) We do not establish presumptive prohibition — meaning, and it is certain that water did not come upon them, and it is permitted even in eating. And see in Sefer Beit Me'ir who wrote that all this deals with those ships where the wheat is placed on decks in the middle of the ship and the wheat does not come into the water that the bottom of the ship draws; and also above it is covered — unlike ships (called canal) where the bottom of the ship is known to always be full of water and the wheat is submerged in the water, and also above it is exposed; and it is likely that while it is on the way rain fell upon them, and also when the sailors constantly raise the oars from the water to move the ship, spray certainly splashes upon them from the river water on all sides of the ship — therefore l'chatchila it is certainly forbidden to buy that wheat for Pesach in every manner; and b'dieved in a place of great loss one could be lenient because of concern for rain from above or what splashed from the oars, lest it is only slight dripping; however, because it is known that the wheat placed at the bottom of the ship where there is always water was certainly steeped in them for a long time, only now they dried — they are forbidden even if they did not split open — therefore one must inquire from expert merchants whether they know that if there are sixty against the wheat above relative to what is at the bottom of the ship, it is permitted b'dieved to eat them on Pesach; and if not, they are forbidden even b'dieved. See there:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:4:א": `(15) We are not concerned, etc. — any that we do not see in them a sign of leavening, since we follow the majority and the majority do not become leavened; and also those stacks where we saw rain fell upon them — we suspend to be lenient, lest the rain entered only in the upper sheaves but not in the belly of the stack; and if you say it entered, perhaps it did not have time to leaven. And nevertheless if one sees in the wheat brought before him that there are sprouts, the Mechaber too concedes one must check whether there are sixty against them; and it is obvious when one brings wheat to sell and rain descends on the sacks until the sack is almost entirely steeped in water and the wheat too became moist — they are forbidden by law on Pesach, for the corruption is revealed to the eyes, and one must warn about this because sellers are not careful about this since most of them are not Torah scholars; and one must also warn grain sellers that in a year when rains were abundant or water fell on the sacks — then they are obligated to sell the grain according to the law of selling chametz, and it is forbidden to sell from them to a gentile after its time of prohibition [Chayei Adam]:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:4:ב": `(16) For ordinary wheat, etc. — Knesset HaGedolah wrote practical halachah that he permitted buying wheat for Pesach even though they were held in pits, as long as we did not see in them a change in appearance and they are not moist; and the Acharonim cited this:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:5:א": `(17) That it dried completely, etc. — Chayei Adam wrote that for this reason the scrupulous are accustomed that all their matzos are guarded from harvest time, for sometimes they leave them attached to dry excessively because they have no time to harvest — and this is what they call shmurah: they harvest them while still somewhat moist. See there:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:5:ב": `(18) If rain descended upon it — that is, abundant rain; but through slight dripping that descended on the sheaves we are not concerned, and it is permitted even for matzah of mitzvah [Ma'amar Mordechai in the name of Rabbeinu Menachem]; and see in responsa what he wrote in the name of responsum Ma'al Tzedakah:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:6:א": `(19) Those that are not split open, etc. — and nevertheless one must clarify which are split open, or at least that there are sixty against them:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:6:ב": `(20) Are permitted — and specifically when they are not moist:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:7:_": `(21) A Jew, etc. — for it is like chametz upon which rubble fell; and further there is a double uncertainty, lest they did not become leavened; and if you say they became leavened, perhaps they spoiled:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:א": `(22) We do not establish prohibition — to say that it is forged with flour mixed into it; for we do not establish presumptive prohibition; and further that even if they mixed in flour, behold honey is fruit juice and does not leaven:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:ב": `(23) And they eat it on Pesach — there are those who wrote that this is specifically to eat the honey in its own form as it is; but to knead dough with it is forbidden, for it is common at the time of extracting wax from honey for water to become mixed there, and it becomes water with fruit juice and they leaven:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:ג": `(24) However there are those who are stringent — that they are accustomed to mix flour into it for sweetness:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:ד": `(25) Not to eat honey only, etc. — explanation: in this honey that they bring in a barrel from the hive where wax is still mixed in, the custom is to eat it even in its own form, and likewise to make from it a medicinal drink; because there is no manner of forgery; and even to make from it a medicinal drink on Pesach is permitted because of Yom Tov rejoicing if he has no other drink. But what they bring in small vessels cleaned of wax — the manner of non-Jews is to mix flour in them; therefore it is forbidden to eat it, and even to make from it a drink on Pesach. And there are among the Acharonim who are stringent in this even before Pesach, because it is common that they mix much flour in them until there are not even in the water mixtures sixty against the flour to nullify them; and likewise they are stringent not to eat honey in its own form on Pesach even from those that come from large barrels, lest one come to be lenient with all honey [except those they bring with the small rooms whole as it lies in the hive that has not yet been crushed]. And regarding practical halachah in making a medicinal drink before Pesach, it is implied from the words of the Acharonim that they are now accustomed to be lenient even from those in small vessels, for we suspend that the flour will be nullified in sixty [unless it is known that in those places the manner is to mix much flour]; and nevertheless one who is stringent in this not to make a medicinal drink except from honey that comes in a barrel with wax — praise to him. And regarding eating on Pesach honey in its own form from the barrels — it depends on local custom, and in our country the custom is to be lenient; and nevertheless one must investigate and know the local custom, for there are places where they put bread in the hive at crushing time or other chametz matters; and in a place where there is concern for this, the honey is forbidden to eat in its own form on Pesach, but it is permitted to make from it a medicinal drink before Pesach [Chayei Adam]:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:ה": `(26) And dried figs — for there is concern they sprinkle flour on them when they dry them; and grapes are accustomed to dry them in an oven near bread or after they removed the bread from the oven; and there are places where they do not sprinkle flour on them and dry them in the sun — and this is what he wrote that it depends on local custom:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:ו": `(27) Not to eat any fruits, etc. — that is, for the above reason. And nevertheless the custom is lenient to make from them a drink on Pesach — that is, they soak them in water until their taste is absorbed in the water and they drink the water on Pesach; for since there is in them only a general concern, they are not so stringent to forbid even their soaking water; but they are not so lenient to cook them on Pesach in order to drink their cooking water on Pesach, because through cooking their taste is absorbed in the water more than through soaking; and in some places they are accustomed to be stringent with soaking water as with cooking water [unless they soaked and cooked them before Pesach]; and likewise on the eve of Pesach in the name of Noda B'Yehuda agrees that l'chatchila it is proper to be stringent, and therefore it is good to strain the liquid from the fruits on the eve before nightfall — then even if a little flour adhered to the fruits and became mixed in the liquid, it is nullified in sixty:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:ז": `(28) In a manner where there is no concern for chametz — such as if it is known to him that it dried in the sun or in an oven after kashering it:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:ח": `(29) And even to retain it is forbidden — for there is concern they mix flour into it; and the Acharonim agreed that that which we call good sugar, made like a hat, has been clarified through investigation of craftsmen that they do not mix flour into it; and nevertheless they were accustomed to sell it l'chatchila [and b'dieved it is permitted after Pesach, and also on the last Yom Tov they eat it]; and that which has a hechsher certificate from a rabbi from its place of manufacture — they are accustomed to eat it l'chatchila. However sugar made very fine has more concern for leavening from flour mixtures, and it is forbidden to retain it; and nevertheless b'dieved if one transgressed and retained them, it is implied from Acharonim not to forbid this either; and see in responsa the manner of certification in this regarding eating l'chatchila. And know that in sugar mixtures where they coat types of fruits and spices, and all the more so those made like small balls from sugar mixtures — the Acharonim agreed that one must certainly be concerned lest there is a chametz mixture in them, and it is forbidden to retain by law, and all the more so to eat them even on the last Yom Tov of the diaspora:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:ט": `(30) And on the last Yom Tov — of the diaspora; since it is rabbinic, we are not stringent regarding something that has only a general concern:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:י": `(31) And candy sugar that does not have, etc. — this is not the cooked sugar cut into pieces that we call candy sugar, for in those there is greater concern for leavening and it is forbidden to retain them and forbidden to eat them even on the last Yom Tov; rather it is sugar that comes from the land of Canada, and it is like our good sugar, and it was clear to them that there is no concern for leavening at all; and nevertheless on other days they may not eat them except through certification, so that one should not come to be lenient also in sugar mixtures. And the Acharonim wrote that even l'chatchila one must certainly be careful not to retain our Canadian sugar and sugar mixtures; nevertheless if one transgressed and retained them, one does not forbid after Pesach, for there are several uncertainties in this — see Kaf HaChayim:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:כ": `(32) And the custom in these countries not to eat, etc. — but to retain them is permitted:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:ל": `(33) Turmeric — for they forge them with flour to establish its appearance; and likewise they place se'or on it to restore its appearance; and the Acharonim wrote that even turmeric grown in one's garden and guarded from leavening — the custom is to treat it as forbidden because of appearance, for turmeric is not common in these countries and they will think it is turmeric that comes from distant places; unlike other fruits that were dried by a Jew and guarded from leavening, where we are not concerned because of appearance, since they are common and everyone knows that sometimes they dry them through a Jew. They further wrote that tobacco — the manner is to soak it in beer in some places, and one must seal it in a room or make a partition before it; and likewise turmeric in places where the custom is to treat it as forbidden. And Chayei Adam wrote that he investigated among craftsmen who make snuff tobacco (that is, they sniff it through the nose) — they moisten the tobacco package with wine, and it is known that yayin nesech is forbidden in benefit by law; and if so it is proper to be careful not to sniff that tobacco all year. And behold all this Chayei Adam wrote in his time; but now one must investigate among craftsmen whether they do so:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:מ": `(34) Or negeliach — because they soak them in barley water before drying them [Darkei Moshe]:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:8:נ": `(35) However they do not forbid their mixture — since the rule implies even in less than sixty; and this applies to all the above in this gloss; and the reason is that we are not so stringent because of a general concern; however regarding honey and sugar mixtures there are those who are stringent to forbid unless there were sixty in the dish against it; and nevertheless in a place of great loss or prevention of Yom Tov rejoicing one may be lenient:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:9:א": `(36) If they actually split open — behold in the Gemara there is a dispute regarding soaking barley in water: some hold it is not chametz until they split open from it; and some hold that if they are swollen so much that they are close to splitting, they are also forbidden; and we decide stringently regarding eating them in their own form, for it is forbidden even if they did not actually split open; but regarding forbidding the cooked dish in which the kernels are found, which is only a slight rabbinic prohibition — we decide leniently that one does not forbid until they actually split open; this is the explanation of the Mechaber's view:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:9:ב": `(37) Forbidden — even in benefit. And also all vessels used with this cooked dish while it was still hot where the hand is scalded in it — they are forbidden to use on Pesach; rather one must set them aside until after Pesach, as was explained at the beginning of siman 447. And if a little of this cooked dish became mixed into another cooked dish, that cooked dish too is forbidden; and likewise if one used with it a spoon with which he stirred in this pot and placed it in another pot while it was boiling — all is forbidden, for since its prohibition is in any amount, we say that here too it releases something:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:9:ג": `(38) Rather they forbid everything — that is, the cooked dish and also all vessels used to cook it, as stated above according to the Mechaber's view when split open. And behold the view of the Taz in note 15 that the cooked dish is forbidden even in benefit; however Kaf HaChayim and several other Acharonim agreed that in a place of great loss one may rely to permit at least in benefit:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:9:ד": `(39) Even if they did not split open — since the Rama's rule implies we do not distinguish whether it is close to splitting or not, because we are not expert in this; nevertheless several Acharonim agreed that at least if it softened we require; and if it did not soften, even if found in hot food, one does not forbid. The Acharonim further wrote that even if three kernels not softened are found in the cooked dish, we do not say that presumably there are more and perhaps they are softened:`,
  "mishnah-berurah:9:ה": `(40) They burn them — see above siman 446 seif 1:`,
  "netiv-chayim:1:_": `{Rama seif 2} Those that became moistened. I will copy for you the words of the Agur, from which the words of the Rama are taken, and thereby we will come to the explanation brought in the Beit Yosef, and this is its language: The Agur — his father was asked about wheat that was spread in an attic under the roof and water passed through the roof and dripped on the grain in some places where there was so much water that they were touchable to touch back, and he answered that Ra'em and Ribba were stringent and said regarding wheat (since they have splitting) that if there is an abundance of water, even if it did not split open the wheat is forbidden; nevertheless to rely on the permission that Ra'avyah wrote and that the Mordechai brought at length in the name of Ra'avyah — to eat some of it before Pesach or to sell to a gentile little by little and the rest is permitted by force of a double uncertainty — perhaps rain did not descend upon them, and if you say rain descended upon them, perhaps they did not split open (meaning: perhaps they did not leaven); but the lower wheat that is at the floor of the attic — it is nearly certain that water descended there to the thickness of a finger, and those one discards and the rest is permitted to keep; but to eat from them on Pesach — for even though regarding "it shall not be seen" the uncertainties are nullified in their majority regarding eating, which is in any amount, we are not lenient — end of his words. And at first glance these words have no understanding. And it appears to me that the question was thus: through dripping of the roof water drips on the grain in some places, and there was also a dripping gutter near the spread grain until water descended on the attic floor under the grain; and now you can understand the answer to it. And behold what the Rama wrote — those that became moistened are forbidden, but other places, etc. — this is not explained in the words of the Agur. Rather it was difficult for him if we explain the words of the Agur as referring to those that did not become moistened, for without that there is already a double uncertainty that permits even if they actually leavened — one, perhaps they already ate from the forbidden ones, and if you say they did not eat, one can say they will now eat from the permitted ones, as with a ring of idolatry that became mixed and one of them fell into the sea and all were permitted to benefit together; and the Rama explained this that the Agur wrote to eat some of it or to sell to a gentile little by little — meaning those known places that became moistened, and the rest of the pile need not eat or sell some of them, and everywhere else is permitted by force of uncertainty. And with this the difficulty of the Taz is resolved — that which the Rama does not permit in eating refers to a place where there is no uncertainty that he has other kosher wheat without doubt for matzah of the commandment, as written in Kol Bo; however if he has no other wheat they are permitted even in eating, for a double uncertainty helps even for a d'oraisa prohibition — end quote:`,
  "netiv-chayim:2:_": `{Magen Avraham seif 12.} And therefore they do not disagree except regarding soaking. I do not know what he is questioning, for in seif 2 even though they did not split open it is not certain this reasoning, since it rested at ease it is chametz; nevertheless since it is an uncertainty of Torah law — perhaps even though they did not split open it is chametz — but here where it deals with rabbinic prohibition in any amount it is a rabbinic uncertainty. Also what he answered that the language "comes to leavening" is an uncertainty — therefore here it is permitted because it is a rabbinic uncertainty — is difficult from itself to itself, for there in seif 16 he wrote that the uncertainty that it did not leaven is not an uncertainty, for it is an uncertainty of lack of knowledge:`,
  "netiv-chayim:3:_": `{Magen Avraham seif 15} If so it is proven that he forbids even in cold. And it is not so, for dough is different, for through the work it leavens through a leavened wheat kernel that was in water; and so wrote the Taz — see there; and his words are correct. And so is proven in the Beit Yosef; but it is difficult why he does not say that we are not concerned perhaps the wheat kernel was in water, for we say "here it was found, here it was," which is from place to place:`,
  "netiv-chayim:4:_": `{In Magen Avraham seif 21} That if a wheat kernel is found in dough they forbid, as explained:`,
  "netiv-chayim:5:_": `{There} But in dough it is from time to time — so it should read:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:א": `Introduction to siman 467:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:ב": `First one must investigate siman of leavening in dough — when is it complete chametz and when stiff. And the sign of leavening in wheat and the five species of grain: what are they, and if whole wheat — is it complete chametz whenever it has a sign of leavening, or stiff? And behold in dough the Sages gave a measure like grasshopper horns — complete chametz liable for karet; if its face turned pale — it is stiff, as in siman 459 in Tur and Shulchan Aruch seif 2 and Pesachim 46a and 48b. And there is further stiff from fruit juice with water as in siman 462 — there we explained that pale face for us is d'rabbanan; but stiff from fruit juice with water where leavening is recognizable in them — one might say even according to those who say stiff it is in any amount; see there and nevertheless it is possible in Beit Yosef there is no practical difference regarding transgressing Pesach upon it — at end of siman 447. And it appears specifically mostly fruit juice with little water — behold mostly water more than half and little fruit juice — we follow the majority; and it has power to leaven; and if recognizable like grasshopper horns — liable for karet; but fruit juice alone without water — even if it stood all day and its cracks intermingled — that is not leavening in them. And Pesachim 38b: cooking leavens it, etc., meaning even within the measure of a mil in lukewarm water according to there. And it is difficult: say cooked in fruit juice alone without water — even if afterward water was mixed with it, still it does not leaven, for it is already cooked; and the law is the true law even for us in 462 in pressing circumstances permitted in fruit juice to cook — if afterward water is mixed it does not come to leavening; and in a whole grain so too one might say in pressing circumstances that we are not concerned it did not cook well — see Magen Avraham 463:4 and it will be explained from this inside. And for Rashi who explained fruit juice alone is stiff — if so. However cooked in fruit juice as it happens and afterward water is mixed — it appears it is complete chametz; and proof from 463 there: perhaps it did not cook well:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:ג": `And if dough stood the measure of a mil even if there is no sign of leavening at all — it is complete chametz liable for karet, even though not found thus in explicit explanation — nevertheless such it is; and in my humble opinion chapter 5 siman 13 from pressing circumstances the measure of a mil is complete chametz; and so pale face is forbidden — it implies at first glance measure of a mil and pale face are one; on the contrary infer from the beginning the opposite in deaf dough: he measured with it the measure of a mil — as Magen Avraham wrote that grasshopper horns with making a sound are one; and from it the same applies to measure of a mil. And this is when he left it without occupation — behold if occupied all day even any occupation it does not leaven; and such is that of dripping dripping there 39b and Orach Chayyim 459. And this is all when signs of leavening were not born — if born, even pale face does not help — occupation in dough and grasshopper horns liable for karet; and from the ordinary case they said according to the majority: all who are occupied do not come to leavening. Also casting cold helps for dough as in siman 457 seif 2 — see Magen Avraham there 6; he left b'dieved uncertain — see end there letter 11; nevertheless certainly lukewarm like the heat of saliva does not nullify leavening. And behold casting cold helps even all day and even from above, for if he read on the first Yom Tov day of Pesach the casting of cold helps until the end of day 2; and even though soaked is like cooked and cooked leavens — this is an error, for for purging and absorption it is like cooked not for leavening; and so if he sees a sign of leavening casting cold does not nullify it, as stated:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:ד": `However all this is in dough; but whole wheat and five species of grain upon which water came — it was not explained explicitly what is their clear sign to be complete chametz and liable for karet on them — only this alone we find in the words of the Sages Pesachim 40a: if wheat grains split open they are forbidden; if not split open — permitted — see there dispute of Shmuel and Mar Ukva. And if no sign of splitting arose and grains fell into water and did not stand the measure of a mil — it is obvious a whole grain is no worse than dough in such a case that there is no leavening; and so in Rosh chapter 30:40a in a split grain found in dough — since the dough did not leaven before a mil, so too the grain; and see end of siman and Magen Avraham letter 4 brought proof on this and it will be explained inside; but casting cold on a grain — one might say it does not help, for it enters all parts of the dough unlike a grain; but measure of a mil — obviously a grain is no worse than dough. And if a sign of leavening arose in whole grains — that is splitting — it must be judged whether they stood the measure of a mil from when water came upon them or did not stand a mil — if it is complete chametz clearly liable for karet or stiff. For us stiff is d'rabbanan; nevertheless without any splitting arising and the grains stood the measure of a mil from when water came upon them — it implies it is obviously not chametz at all; and proof from what Rabbenu Efrayim challenged from the incident of the ship that sank in a basket that Rava did not ask if they split open and needed to distinguish between resting at ease and placing — see Rosh chapter 30:40a there the grains stood more than a mil — we learn all that did not split open are permitted even if they stood the measure of a mil; and below I will explain further. And the Levush in seif 9 wrote: a cooked dish in which wheat and barley were found — even if they split open literally it is not complete chametz from the Torah but only complete chametz d'rabbanan; if not split open there is no complete chametz d'rabbanan — nevertheless people practice to forbid the cooked dish — see there; it implies even if they split open in cooking and split literally — nevertheless it is only chametz d'rabbanan; and in cooking less than a mil or less — see Penei Yehoshua Pesachim 39b. And behold it requires study; and from all codifiers it implies splitting is complete chametz; and the Rif would eat grains in their substance — it is a Torah safek whether Mar Ukva or Shmuel, and in any case split open literally it is complete chametz liable for karet in any amount even within measure of a mil — it implies; and so Rambam brought Mordechai chapter 30 and Bach in seif 2: grains that split open are complete chametz liable for karet — see there. However Maharam chapter 5 from pressing circumstances 13 and 18 wrote: grains that did not split open are not complete chametz in any amount — there is no proof to say behold they split open it is in any amount, for he holds stiff in any amount is forbidden and stiff is as one who sees there in chapters 4-5; nevertheless there too he equates Maharam's view with his teacher the Rif. And Magen Avraham 447:5 stiff is nullified in sixty — therefore a grain split open literally is stiff and forbids in any amount; and for us who are not expert whether it softened — forbidden:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:ה": `From this manner we learn: whole grains upon which water came and they split open and stood the measure of a mil — in any case it is stiff chametz liable for karet. And what the Rosh wrote Pesachim 40b: a split grain in dough is safek chametz before measure of a mil or all the more so since he occupies it — since the dough did not leaven, so too the grain; unlike after measure of a mil split literally — complete chametz in any amount and liable for karet; and in a cooked dish less than measure of a mil and split literally — it is stiff in any amount liable for karet; and if they stood measure of a mil and did not split open — in this Rabbenu Efrayim distinguishes between placing and resting at ease — Magen Avraham 3-4 and Taz 3 that it is forbidden and still safek whether stiff forbidden within measure of a mil without splitting, or d'rabbanan; and whether casting cold helps or not — Magen Avraham letter 12 wrote to Rabbenu Efrayim: there is no certain chametz but safek chametz — see there and it will be explained inside regarding casting cold — see Tur in the name of Avi HaEzri and Beit Yosef there that the ship incident — one might say it was lifted from water; and Peri Chadash agreed: all that rest in water even many hours from hour to hour do not leaven (and that is without splitting) — and accordingly in pressing circumstances when resting in water and did not split even more than a mil — one might say to sell to a gentile on Pesach before it was lifted from water. And it will be explained inside seif 12 regarding a whole grain found in cold water that stood long without salting and brine — one might say to permit similarly in pressing circumstances. And see siman 453 in Magen Avraham 2 regarding types of herbs — see there:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:ו": `And one may still question: whence this matter that the Sages said (Rif chapter 39 and Maharam chapter 5:13) a cooked dish with an unsplit grain is permitted — they brought Taz in seif 9 and behold from Pesachim 40a placing or even if stood not like Rabbenu Efrayim, or as Magen Avraham letter 12 wrote this is in water — here in cooking one cannot compare to cold water; and in Pesachim 39b cooked leavens and does not answer regarding whole grains that did not split — and still this requires study here; and it will be explained in Magen Avraham 12; I will explain further:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:ז": `Know that baking that does not come to cooking — behold bread is baked chametz; and if afterward he put to flour with fruit juice — it is possible it leavens; and the baraita of bread that molded and was disqualified from a dog's feed is fit to leaven doughs — there is no proof, for there it speaks with water; and through the chametz it leavens quickly — see Magen Avraham letter 15 in the name of responsum of Rashba in what he wrote: and it is possible — and it will be explained there:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:ח": `And know further that it is fitting to explain the matter of gravy that carries and releases from all of it and in all of it is every liquid thing — water, fruit juice, oil, and dissolved fat; everything cooked in it releases and absorbs throughout. Whence do I derive this? From this I derive it: Yoreh De'ah 104:2 — a mouse in soft fat that moves is like soaked — evidently fat too is called soaked; so too all the more cooked such; and even fat has pulsation — one authority says there is no permitted flavoring for prohibition; and nevertheless we learn everything that is like soaked is like cooked; and from the Torah it forbids until sixty in its non-species. And responsum Beit Yaakov siman 99 implies from his words: when there is a fingerbreadth on it to smear and a grain rests upon it — it is like soaked — requires study; one: Yoreh De'ah 105:61 in Shach 1 what is outside soaking is permitted; and in Taz 64 in gravy itself there is dispute whether it carries what is outside the gravy; and Pesachim 76b: smearing any amount is in the peel; and Yoreh De'ah 105:68 by fire — so it appears; and Magen Avraham 15 in the name of responsum of Rashba 176: a raw sheet like circumcision in salting also 110 degrees — and it will be explained there:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:ט": `Further I will speak on a whole grain whose sign of splitting is leavening as stated: if found in fruit juice even with splitting we do not say presumably it leavened beforehand, for fruit juice is not accustomed to split; one in a thousand splits from fruit juice (and if it splits from fruit juice there is no splitting and like grasshopper horns in dough is a sign of leavening in them, for we hold fruit juice does not leaven). And at first glance from what the Ran wrote in responsum siman 59 regarding grains found split: there is safek from moisture of the stack it split, or from rain that fell on them at harvest — he answered it is far-fetched to attribute splitting to fruit juice — see there; and if so even if we say it occasionally happens, nevertheless we attribute to the common case; but in siman 462 seif 6 a grain in honey is permitted and stam implies even if split — nevertheless one might say without splitting and stood long therein like Rabbenu Efrayim in resting at ease: one, there was no reason to be stam; and further in cooked dish the Mechaber permits in any amount; and from what the Ran wrote there that it is not reasonable to attribute to stack moisture — namely what is common from stack moisture is not common in fruit juice such that it rests therein and splits. And responsum Tzitz Eliezer 123 and it will be explained inside Taz Yoreh De'ah I will explain further in an incident from this; and proof from what the Rosh wrote regarding a split grain in dough — here one cannot expand:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:י": `And this grain in honey — even if there are not sixty b'dieved fruit juice does not leaven — see Levush there and Orach Chayyim Shabbos. And what is still fit to discuss — whether chametz is davar sheyeish lo matirin or not davar sheyeish lo matirin — see siman 447 and here and Levush and Yoreh De'ah 102 Shach 14 and Taz 13; and behold one might say leniently: since for us everything requires burning it is not davar sheyeish lo matirin; and accordingly on the last Yom Tov on the eighth hour of the day people practice to be lenient; and so in other things permitted to retain — one might say davar sheyeish lo matirin; and accordingly in 467 Taz 4 in safek sefeika permitted to retain and forbidden to eat because it is safek sefeika in davar sheyeish lo matirin — see Yoreh De'ah 110 Shach 57 from this; and here in seif 12 some say to permit in safek sefeika to eat for a time of need when one cannot wait on a roasted chicken — then safek sefeika is significant. And know safek sefeika in davar sheyeish lo matirin in its non-species requires study in it and in Shach there letter 57 — it implies somewhat the reason that anything in a thousand is not nullified — so too in safek sefeika — one might say — see there letter 56. However all that we are stringent from custom — if there is another safek one might say to permit like ready safek on the second Yom Tov — and it will be explained inside. Further one might say mixtures before Pesach in 467 seif 2 permitted to retain from safek sefeika and forbidden to eat — unlike what was made during Pesach since people practice to burn everything and forbidden to retain — we do not distinguish to permit retaining in safek sefeika and forbid eating like that of seif 12 in plucking the chickens — and this son:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:כ": `We hold in Yoreh De'ah that a thing forbidden in any amount — we do not say in it: flavor is not flavor; practical difference for us: a pot with cooked dish and a split grain found — everything forbidden; and afterward Avi HaEzri placed it in an oven — this pot Avi HaEzri with dish and other pots — one might say it is aroma in any amount and purging permits — see Magen Avraham 447 letter 41 and it will be explained in Taz letter 17 in two any-amounts I will explain this:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:ל": `In salting — see seif 14 and it will be explained there whether eaten because of its saltiness or not; and brine from salted meat even if eaten because of saltiness is considered boiling as in siman 91:65 in Yoreh De'ah. And if it fell on a vessel — it will be explained there with God's help. And if he left meat in a vessel that had a grain in brine, or left meat there and poured boiling water on them — see Yoreh De'ah 95 Shach letter 24 — and it will be explained below seif 14 from this; and here one cannot expand:`,
  "peri-megadim:1:מ": `Or Taz and Magen Avraham who do not follow Orach Chayyim Shabbos who is concerned lest the gentile grind and sell to an Israelite — so too the Ran for his view that splitting is not permitted in grains at all placing — and he establishes in split ones unlike for us like Rabbenu Efrayim in seif 2 resting at ease; and so Rashi Pesachim 40a: these grains that are not recognizable, etc. — for if recognizable he would not challenge from kilayim to perhaps he will grind. And behold the Tur doubled his words and there is concern for leavening and it is not recognizable — one does not sell to a gentile lest he sell to an Israelite; since leavening is not recognizable — so in Levush; and it should have said grains that sank — one does not sell to a gentile if leavening is not recognizable, only at the beginning a leniency for an Israelite permitted without informing even if not recognizable; and for a gentile forbidden when not recognizable — behold if recognizable permitted for a gentile too; and one might say at first since not recognizable there is concern he will sell thus; but to be concerned he will grind and sell all this — not in concern for leavening; also for Rabbenu Efrayim there is no certain chametz only safek and perhaps only stiff — Magen Avraham 12 and letter 4 — except concern he will sell thus since not recognizable; and if they split open obviously forbidden for it is d'oraisa and we are concerned he will grind; leniency at beginning and end; and end of siman and Ateret Zekenim 1; and for what Chayyei Adam wrote: transgressing Beit Yosef — Rabbenu Efrayim there: nullified because not seen — see there. And end of siman 2: if he transgressed and kept and sowed after Pesach the shoots are permitted 463 letter 9 — requires study without splitting for it is only concern for leavening and safek whether stood measure of a mil — Magen Avraham 4; and perhaps stiff is all that did not split open at all — Magen Avraham 12; if so safek chametz after Pesach is d'rabbanan — see siman 448-449 and we wrote there from this. And do not err to explain concern for leavening as meaning concern he will sell to an Israelite — that stretch cannot be said; only concern for leavening, not full chametz as stated. And the Mechaber who omitted that it is not recognizable — it implies for a gentile in every case forbidden lest he grind flour; and for an Israelite a little is obvious permitted when recognizable; and 448 Taz and Magen Avraham agreed to permit when splitting recognizable even much to a gentile; and Magen Avraham 1-2 and it will be explained there further:`,
  "peri-megadim:10:א": `Even Taz in the name of Bach there in seif 11 that softened — at least require; reason some stringent concern to rule like Mar Ukva Pesachim 40a all that placed on opening of roaster and would split — we are not expert; but not softened hard ones we are expert — no leavening reached — see there and Peri Chadash here. And it appears to me: only when there are sixty — any amount d'rabbanan; but without sixty like eating grains in their substance — Torah safek for all who say d'oraisa; and in seif 2 grain sunk in river though not split forbidden — Rava did not ask splitting — also did not ask eating in their substance forbidden Torah safek; all the more in hot cooked dish hard kernels not permitted even without sixty — Magen Avraham letter 12. However if grain fell into dish above boiling — hilat — see 454 Magen Avraham 4: if very hard even without sixty lenient — ruling authority must be careful. And regarding benefit: softened not split — Taz letter 15 implies forbidden in one safek for benefit burn all; Ateret Zekenim letter 18 agreed forbidden for benefit; Eshel Avraham letter 26 leaned b'dieved to permit benefit — requires study:`,
  "peri-megadim:10:ב": `An incident occurred in our community K'K Kamerani: they made round loaves called knaidlach from ground matzah kneaded with goose fat and salt and pepper without any water and fried in fat rising on all sides; afterward placed in a bowl and poured boiling gravy from kli rishon; found in one a softened grain; they asked me the law of the food and vessels on Pesach:`,
  "peri-megadim:10:ג": `Answer: softened alone grain not split — food appears permitted; what concern from fruit juice with eggs does not leaven; little salt even in our place since form changed does not leaven — see 462 Magen Avraham 6 goose fat after melting water of rinsing gone — see there; 463 Magen Avraham 1; and for us b'dieved permitted — see there; 462 pressing circumstances — see there; pepper without water does not leaven — see 455 Magen Avraham 19; 462 salt specifically water significant. Pouring gravy did not reach middle of rounds; pouring 110 degrees works; even for chametz all the more; nevertheless did not leaven in brief moment by gravy that did not reach inside — discard that bread with the grain not the rest. If split grain found in one — judge: splitting sign of leavening; fruit juice does not leaven — perhaps before leavening though we do not strengthen prohibition such case impaired — nevertheless Mechaber 462:6 grain in honey does not forbid — therefore split here 467:9 permits cooked dish without splitting — strained to say taste even without sixty in honey — Magen Avraham 12; see Tzitz Eliezer 123; not impossible split from fruit juice and egg dough swelled perhaps like grasshopper horns more in fruit juice — no chametz; in water that is sign. In seif 11 split grain in dough Rambam forbids dough even without standing — attribute leavened beforehand — splitting impaired presumption; Rosh Pesachim 40a if dough did not leaven so grain; we do not say leavened beforehand; possible splitting in grain not clear sign; for us splitting complete chametz — so in fruit juice too; not impossible split from fruit juice; Ran responsum brought here seif 6 (responsum 59) not from stack moisture — little; rests in fruit juice many days splits; examiner in Peri Chadash seif 16 incident Tzitz 123 grain in goose fat — he expanded there: in honey and wine if stood long perhaps split from wine and honey permitted unlike fat frying — if split in rounds common attribute from outside; softened alone we permit; Magen Avraham 12; 462:6; Mishnah Berurah roasted chicken:`,
  "peri-megadim:10:ד": `If placed rounds in bowl cut and poured gravy found softened grain — forbid pouring; all the more stringent without measure of a mil in hot no measure. Even already cooked in fat perhaps not cooked well; especially if cut grain and white flour seen — not cooked well — see 463 Magen Avraham 4. In introduction we questioned Pesachim 39b cooked leavens — not established in fruit juice then water does not leaven; for Rashi stiff fits; Ran responsum 59 such is fruit juice and water — stiff nullified in sixty; Magen Avraham letter 21 will explain:`,
  "peri-megadim:11:א": `On Yom Tov — Taz for Mechaber in 447:10 permitted; for us custom to forbid — obvious permitted to retain on eighth all the more from any amount. But less than sixty forbidden to retain — Magen Avraham 14. General rule: all permitted to retain — permitted to sell and benefit; but some forbidden to retain permitted to sell as Mechaber wrote; see Levush here. And since Taz did not learn only a fortiori — implies other two d'rabbanan like stiff less than sixty (Magen Avraham 447:5, Taz 19) or dry in dry in its species in majority or species in wet less than sixty; and imagine burned Pesach wine with chametz wine (though essence not its species we follow taste for leniency and stringency — see 98 Shach 6; until nightfall wine for its taste and smells in thousand — decree unlike not its species) — forbidden to retain on eighth of Pesach; and what more from any amount one might rely on questions, etc. However Levush wrote last Yom Tov d'rabbanan and retaining also d'rabbanan — two d'rabbanan they were not stringent — implies every two d'rabbanan do not combine in retaining. However other days even two d'rabbanan purging and any amount or other two d'rabbanan — do not learn from eighth of Pesach; see 447 Magen Avraham letter 41. And know prohibition of retaining in half measure is still stiff; all the more in wet in its non-species less than sixty that reverted to prohibition — palate tastes and tastes — for all perhaps still stiff; Peri Chadash here and Magen Avraham 447:42 and 44. And if nullified by mouth and said all chametz I will have on Pesach is nullified — for those who can nullify even what did not yet exist — see 446 Magen Avraham 1 and responsum of Moreinu from Lubin and responsum Bach; nevertheless if less than sixty on eighth in its non-species forbidden to retain for retaining d'rabbanan — nevertheless if not nullified d'oraisa also for Rashbatz in Maamar Yavin Shemuah and so Maharan beginning retaining stiff because of eating prohibition though stiff nullified suffices — nevertheless close to Torah prohibition. And it will be explained Magen Avraham 18:`,
  "peri-megadim:11:ב": `And know roasted by fire forbidden from chametz — so it appears — see Yoreh De'ah 105 distinctions: where sixty required by law no removal needed; where stringency required sixty and by law amount of removal — on eighth such forbidden to retain must remove as it appears and discard — rest permitted to retain. And if nullified by mouth until nightfall forbidden as it appears; and afterward cooked in pot before nightfall even if sixty in piece against prohibition — as it appears we say flavor is not flavor — see 96 Shach 9 — then needs sixty against removal; forbidden for purging does not apply to food — see 106 there Taz and Shach 1-2. Therefore piece forbidden by law on eighth by amount of removal — transgressed and retained after Pesach and cooked in kli rishon afterward — all without sixty against removal — depends on dispute 447 Magen Avraham 44 whether after Pesach needs sixty or less than sixty also not acquired. There we wrote one might say only nevelah through d'rabbanan transgressed — therefore for all no sixty needed against absorption; perhaps permitted reverted to prohibition — same for Beit Yosef; perhaps Beit Yosef no palate tastes. Understand this — requires study; end of siman I will write more laws scattered to this; in opening beginning of book part 3 we expanded laws pertaining to this siman:`,
  "peri-megadim:11:ג": `And know on eighth of Pesach in complete chametz or without sixty — even though did not transgress stiff Beit Yosef for we are expert in fixed month — nevertheless R' Shimon acquires after Pesach as explained Tur 448 bread chametz gift to Israelite — two days equal lest come to treat lightly — see 448. And even if he made dough on eighth of Pesach and saw no time to bake unless it leavened first and preceded and nullified and transgressed afterward and retained — we do not say two d'rabbanan such as written; Magen Avraham 447 letter 46. And what was questioned how burn on Yom Tov and also seventh of Pesach how burn — see 446 Magen Avraham letter 2 and responsum Chatam Sofer; Magen Avraham 14 will explain; responsum Penei Yehoshua part 1:15; also Magen Avraham 14 with God's help:`,
  "peri-megadim:12:_": `And so Taz's holy words need explanation: Rosh Pesachim page 40 on that which split open, etc., wrote: split grain in matzah — one might say amount of removal not needed since splitting is not complete chametz only close, etc.; and since dough did not leaven so too grain; we are not concerned perhaps grain was in water — waters are accustomed to guard; some forbid that matzah and some forbid those baked with it — not clear; so Rambam wrote cut place of pulsation — see there brought in Tur. If so his conclusion: in safek chametz no removal needed; and Rambam in recognizable sour grain — removal; in roasting and baking and Beit Yosef implies Rosh agreed finally split is sign of leavening; as Maharif — Torah safek, etc.; as in introduction split grain complete chametz; therefore Chullin retaining in water necessarily or in flour already leavened beforehand and forbids amount of removal in baking — in dough forbids nothing in cold per his view. Bach questioned from Rosh that waters guard — implies sour grain forbids all water; below wrote chametz in cold does not forbid — no taste in cold (brought seif 12 some forbid during Pesach) — resolved: here during Pesach properly if was in water something purged and forbids all its water from water — see there. Mechaber brought Rosh for halachah: split grain in dough or baking removal — even in dough or from distress of kneading where grain touched pulsates removal from split grain in dough not more for waters guard them; therefore fell at kneading or flour grain was and to lessen time when water poured to knead we are not concerned waters leavened only in kneading grain pierced and amount of removal suffices. Some forbid all dough or that matzah — appears Rashba responsum 176 brought in Beit Yosef forbids all dough not that matzah alone from two reasons: grain touched through stirring in all dough (in cold too from distress of kneading something purges) and waters carry taste in all — see there; said some forbid all dough Rashba and Tur in others all matzot (not baked only what kneaded in water Avi HaEzri) or that matzah Tur in some great ones — here found here was in kneading and through its stirring touched all dough in hand that matzah; Magen Avraham 16; see Beer HaGolah; understand. Rambam glossed during Pesach forbid; before Pesach not — appears Taz intent: during Pesach concerned grain in cold waters purged something forbids all dough; before Pesach any amount nullified as seif 12 Taz 16 — unless what distinction between dough or matzah; Magen Avraham 15-16 if found in one matzah in hand here fell forbidden in cold during Pesach through compression pressing purges in cold; if in whole dough all dough forbidden — during Pesach in any amount; before Pesach nullified what fell in sixty until measure. Taz answered Rosh Avi HaEzri Derishah Levush Darkei Moshe there Rosh holds grain in cold forbidden grain does not forbid water (reverse Avi HaEzri in Tur explained inside seif 12) — if was in water grain leavened afterward through compression of kneading cold dough forbidden from what purged in such case. See letter 16. What Rambam ruled from Maharil and Maharash already written; what softened though not split for us and Rambam who did not gloss seif 11-12-14 relied seif 9; Peri Chadash 16 leaned b'dieved if not split only softened perhaps Rambam wrote custom but b'dieved rely on Mechaber; if so softened b'dieved perhaps permitted eating — requires study; benefit perhaps even dissenters seif 9 here permitted; Taz 17 Rokeach see there; if in water safek stood above — b'dieved perhaps forbidden; Maharil incident knaidlach letter 12; 462:6; goose fat; Peri Chadash 16:`,
  "peri-megadim:13:א": `If Taz: see seif 8:11 Shach 27 and 105 Taz 4 all the more forbid without pressing circumstances for it purges and absorbs unlike together; siman 447 Magen Avraham 9; 454 letter 3 and 13 see there; chametz in any amount forbids more; one hard grain to chicken if Yom Tov in water; before Pesach equals other prohibitions; this plucking — though Yoreh De'ah 88:11 forbids l'chatchila in any amount all the more here salted on feathers rinsed plucked in any amount:`,
  "peri-megadim:13:ב": `Avi HaEzri end of siman brought Mordechai: plucked chicken on mouth of chametz vessel — forbade chicken — see there; Chayyei Adam letter 33; indeed until nightfall in any amount should permit; pouring only 110 degrees until nightfall; perhaps this authority until nightfall in any amount even for those who purify and absorb unlike Ran; Magen Avraham 454:3; Chayyei Adam 8; even Beit Yosef permit; all the more Avi HaEzri; Eshel Avraham; Eshel Avraham 14:95 letter 24:`,
  "peri-megadim:14:א": `Forbidden Taz Magen Avraham 16 Chayyei Adam; also from time to time we establish prohibition; Tur incident plucked — Rabbenu Raavan forbade — did not mention splitting; Mechaber for his method seif 9 specifically split; softened even in kli rishon in any amount dish permitted; meaning before boiling before chicken boiled (not in cold from place to place we do not establish prohibition); align Taz with Magen Avraham distinguish time and place; see 16-17; will explain. Taz: do not question safek perhaps now fell digested in chicken — split in whole grain not digested; what perhaps did not leaven hard — for us split clear chametz Magen Avraham 15:`,
  "peri-megadim:14:ב": `If softened alone not split — food permitted; fruit juice with eggs; little salt form changed; 462:6; 463:1 b'dieved; 462 pressing; pepper; gravy did not reach middle; 110 degrees; discard one bread; split — fruit juice; 462:6; 467:9; knaidlach; Rosh; Ran; Peri Chadash; letter 12:`,
  "peri-megadim:14:ג": `Further Mechaber lenient softened in any amount one safek d'rabbanan lenient; here one safek stringent; perhaps time to time worse establish on presumption lack of knowledge in rabbinic safek lenient Yoreh De'ah 105 safek soaked; Kaf HaChayyim Shach 1 in any amount perhaps we say it is different; therefore same for other meat:`,
  "peri-megadim:15:_": `Split grain Taz for Mechaber seif 9 even one safek in dish in any amount without split dish permitted Magen Avraham 12 all the more safek sefeika; except Rambam agrees here one safek forbids benefit not only softened unlike Chayyei Adam letter 10; split grain for us complete chametz no safek unlike Rosh Pesachim split grain; see seif 11 Peri Chadash end Magen Avraham:`,
  "peri-megadim:16:_": `If Taz as letter 12 cold does not purge and absorb at all even any amount; Rosh waters guard else grain sour forbids water; appears his view; see 17; Levush 11-12; examiner Tur Bach sees Avi HaEzri disputes Tur: sour grain in cold waters nogeia possible even cold; Tur reverse: grain sour in cold waters no taste in water; Avi HaEzri there split, etc.; Mechaber split grain or chametz bread permitted during Pesach — Tur view even Pesach no purging in cold; some forbid — Rosh waters guard Taz 12; if before Pesach and who is anonymous permitting even during; Avi HaEzri did not permit chametz bread anyway forbids; Beer HaGolah letter 70 requires study; Magen Avraham 19 will explain:`,
  "peri-megadim:2:_": `And we inform you: see Turei Zahav Pesachim 40b and Maharam chapter 5:19 and as written here in Shulchan Aruch. And the Taz understood from the simple sense of Maharshal that he explained the words of Maharam thus in his version: keva keva stam — meaning both for Israelite and gentile; and for Israelite with informing and for gentile without informing because there is safek sefeika with a gentile unlike Israelite on the contrary there; there the prohibition for an Israelite is also with informing because perhaps a stumbling block will come from not informing — therefore they did not mention in the Gemara permission of much with informing; and on this the Taz questioned: certainly Maharam and the Mechaber hold specifically for a gentile a little; for an Israelite whether much or little with informing permitted specifically; and the Tur who wrote and it is reasonable like the author of the Ittur for his view that reads keva for Israelite. Avi HaEzri. However for halachah he agreed to Maharshal and as the Tur's version: for Israelite keva and with informing specifically, for we do not rely that he will eat before Pesach — for in presumption and majority too we do not rely wherever it can be fixed easily like that of R' Papa: ask him — behold much with informing not lest he forget to inform; and for a gentile little permitted because safek sefeika perhaps he will not sell to Israelite and perhaps Israelite will eat before Pesach; even l'chatchila one does not make safek sefeika in one's hands — this is preferable that presumably he will eat for his need. And there is a corruption in Taz — it should read: unlike when selling little to a gentile there is safek sefeika; and for Israelite much and specifically little with informing; and Bach seif 2 stringent as it appears for gentile little forbidden and for Israelite little with informing; and Netziv permits little to gentile as safek sefeika; and on the contrary — and it will be explained in Magen Avraham:`,
  "peri-megadim:3:_": `For since Taz and Magen Avraham 4 brought the long Mordechai and it will be explained there; and behold what the Taz wrote to benefit from them together — meaning two by two not all together — see Yoreh De'ah 110:67; however in safek prohibition that became mixed and one fell to the sea one might say all permitted together too as safek sefeika perhaps there is no prohibition and perhaps the prohibition fell to the sea — see there and here in Magen Avraham. And what he wrote there is a leap in the Agur — Bach questioned thus; that Mordechai deals with recognizable splitting unlike here where those upon which rain fell remove them and the rest have safek sefeika; and he explained there: the question is as there permission if eating some is safek sefeika — so here in the middle of the grains there is safek sefeika all that removes the upper ones; and what the Taz wrote in any amount d'rabbanan requires study — those that became moist remove them and the rest safek whether rain fell on all or not. Also what he wrote in 453 if grinding permitted because does not return and revive — flour wet in wet; as Magen Avraham wrote there 6 and Taz there 2; and why here rule returns and revives, etc. — meaning why forbid eating too — requires study; and to say the difficulty is why take those that became moist and grind all before Pesach like grain that sprouted where there is sixty — there is effort to clarify and also b'dieved as there; unlike here where one should take those that became moist — one might say presumably rain did not fall on all the grain but safek perhaps fell on some and dried — for this he said if certainly there are sixty against safek that perhaps rain fell on some it is obvious permitted for eating and to grind before Pesach; and therefore safek — still difficulty: since it is safek why not permit eating like every safek sefeika; and the language is somewhat garbled; and what he wrote as there if sixty the reason is not to strengthen prohibition — we do not strengthen from safek sefeika but it deals presumptive prohibition and as there permitting eating from safek sefeika and here forbidding eating; and Magen Avraham 5 and Taz 5 and Peri Chadash here and in Darkei Moshe Florence edition I saw in gloss: Agur's law does not contradict Rashba's law — namely stacks are in the stalk and hard and upper parts of the stack like a roof and covering and recognizable rain did not reach the middle of the stack — see there:`,
  "peri-megadim:4:א": `However from responsum of the Ran siman 59 brought in Shulchan Aruch seif 6 there is difficulty — except there too it implies the reason like Rashba's responsum that it is common when resting on sheaves rain drips on the upper parts of the stack and in the middle water does not reach — unlike the Agur incident of grain in an attic where there is safek in all of it — he properly said not to rely to permit for eating. And what the Rambam wrote in seif 12 to permit through safek sefeika — one might say in any amount d'rabbanan; but to eat grains in their substance not; and further one might say safek sefeika born on Pesach permits safek sefeika, for for us chametz is not called davar sheyeish lo matirin — if we forbid we must burn everything and to permit retaining and forbid eating is not seen; therefore we are lenient in everything unlike before Pesach where davar sheyeish lo matirin is such as to permit retaining and not eating — we wrote this in the introduction. And for halachah the Taz wrote in pressing circumstances permitted; and Chayyei Adam 10 and Magen Avraham 2 questioned two by two; and here concern perhaps rain fell on all; and see Orach Chayyim Shabbos what he wrote that safek sefeika like majority and chametz in any amount they did not follow majority; and until nightfall safek sefeika requires distinction between that of seif 12 — see there; and safek sefeika that reverses — see Yoreh De'ah 110 on this and it will be explained in letter 5 on this:`,
  "peri-megadim:4:ב": `And indeed from responsum of the Ran siman 59 it is difficult — except there too it implies the reason like Rashba that it is common in sheaves that rain drips on upper parts of the stack and in the middle water does not reach — unlike in the Agur incident. And what the Mechaber wrote in seif 3 if their appearance did not change — behold if changed forbidden — Avi HaEzri and Levush also forbidden to retain them — behold they did not come in a ship; even if appearance changed if there are no others one might say permitted:`,
  "peri-megadim:5:א": `There is Taz: behold flour in honey without moistening is like siman 462:6 except only for eating thus; but to make a drink from the start — even flour in honey all that initially lacks sixty then leavens in water before cooking well; however before Pesach permitted and nullified in sixty with water; and from the fifth hour until nightfall if known flour was mixed or small vessels customary to mix flour and concern perhaps no sixty against flour or perhaps in one place not blended well — behold for us flavor is not flavor in other prohibitions; and soaked in soft honey is like soaked — see Yoreh De'ah 105 and 104; if so honey became nevelah and afterward needs sixty against all the honey and there are not sixty in water against all the honey for it is Yom Tov and enhanced. Therefore for one who permits small vessels to make a drink from them before the fifth hour — from the fifth until nightfall not from what we said; however from not nullifying prohibition permitted all where there is safek whether they mix — similar to vinegar — see Yoreh De'ah 114 Shach 21 and 116:28 — see there; except from nevelah such — perhaps fine flour or flour mixed with water as he wrote; and b'dieved if cooked thus one might say in pressing circumstances if not known — perhaps we do not say flavor is not flavor l'chatchila must cook before fifth hour; and large hive barrels not customary to forge — even rooms not recognizable — Taz's view implies permission even to eat during Pesach that honey; and so to cook during Pesach from those large barrels not customary to forge — as appears from Rambam's conclusion that not to eat only from the hive in a barrel — implies permitted to eat on Pesach from it and so drink if fit for drinking on Yom Tov else no melacha of another's Yom Tov. And behold what he proved permitted to make drink on Pesach from large barrels — though obvious since even for eating Rambam permits — all the more so drink — one might say drink is stricter lest flour inside and not blended well and Yom Tov in water in any amount on Pesach unlike eating in their substance fruit juice does not leaven — therefore he wrote no concern at all in large barrels where they never mix flour:`,
  "peri-megadim:5:ב": `And what he wrote regarding saffron — at first since we see saffron dissolved in the cooked dish and nothing remains — necessarily even if nevelah meat threads are mixed custom is to invert and all becomes like saffron — therefore we follow present state; somewhat strained to explain Taz's words; also there is concern Yom Tov — see there; and Magen Avraham letter 3 and Chayyei Adam here letter 16 wrote novel fat that did not change to a permitted thing — nevertheless one might say we do not permit forbidden fat for it is not such a change as blood which the Torah forbade and did not change to another appearance — and blood that was cooked is permitted for most codifiers and so is the halachah unlike forbidden fat; no verse needed — see there. And if known flour or fine flour was put in honey and fell into a dish in less than sixty — Chayyei Adam relying on leniency of Rabbenu Yonah that it turned to honey did not lose — see there and Magen Avraham letter 11 — meaning when it stood some time in the honey — for in short time it does not turn to honey:`,
  "peri-megadim:6:א": `And know in Yoreh De'ah 84 and Shach letter 37 honey is accustomed to preserve and sustain things hidden in it; as Rashba wrote in responsum 80: whole things preserve; like Bava Batra regarding Herod who hid seven years in honey; and cut things quickly consumed — see there; therefore whole grains found in honey on Pesach even though long time — soaked preserves; and soaked in soft honey honey becomes chametz and forbids the dish in any amount — no distinction whether there was living soul in it or one grain unlike fine flour long time turned to honey — permitted b'dieved as written. And it will be explained in Magen Avraham 7:`,
  "peri-megadim:6:ב": `And figs — Taz wrote flour — one might say fruit juice and fine flour concern — see Levush; and in my humble opinion zafarin too forbidden lest substitution; and see Levush: honey from a barrel is common and we are not concerned for substitution unlike zafarin in my view not common — they say it came from overseas; and so dried fruits from Israel people buy known and common to dry for Pesach and no substitution. And fruit a gentile dried with chametz bread in one oven — from aroma one might say b'dieved forbid the mixture — we hold bread aroma does not forbid — see siman 447; nevertheless one might say touched chametz bread. And raisins red — not eaten; small black ones some permit — see there and Magen Avraham letter 8 and it will be explained there:`,
  "peri-megadim:6:ג": `And know in honey and wine if it stood long — one might say split from wine and honey and permitted unlike frying in fat which sense testifies splitting does not occur from fat frying — if found split in the rounds one might say common and attribute from elsewhere; but in softened alone we permit for us — as stated; and it will be explained letter 12; and in Mishnah Berurah regarding roasted chicken with grain found — also:`,
  "peri-megadim:7:_": `And figs — Taz wrote flour; fruit juice and fine flour concern; Levush; zafarin forbidden lest substitution; dried fruit from Israel not substituted:`,
  "peri-megadim:8:א": `And tsuker kandil — Taz wrote in sour vats; stam vessels Avi HaEzri and Yom Tov against Yom Tov is from siman 447 from this; and end of siman; and what tsuker Candia they eat on last Yom Tov — hot tsuker without real cooking; experts cook in special vessels — product is cane grown in ground boiled in water extracted like flour — so merchants said; Magen Avraham letter 9; l'chatchila practice to sell even hot tsuker; b'dieved not forbidden; even our kandil cooked in sour vats if stood — not forbidden after Pesach for nevelah ben nevelah; Avi HaEzri siman 447 there. And Taz: not common among us — properly concerned for substitution like figs from his garden; and in my view zafarin letter 9 and 7 — recognizable distinct form:`,
  "peri-megadim:8:ב": `And if pressing circumstances on Pesach market day and much selling tsuker — one might say permitted to sell hot tsuker on pressing circumstances; merchants testified no flour mixed in hot tsuker — everything by custom; where practice prohibition do not permit before them; where practice permission permitted. Now Israelites make hot tsuker for Pesach with some change lest substitution — well done and fixed per great ones of the generation and Peri Chadash. See Levush: some lenient in kandil tsuker; on last Yom Tov do not be so stringent — see there:`,
  "peri-megadim:9:_": `Saffron — Taz and Levush; see letter 7 distinction between common here and not common — concerned:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:1:_": `Siman 467 seif 8 in the gloss — not to eat any dried fruits; the law of orange peel is like dried fruits — Chayyei Adam general rule 127 seif 2:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:2:_": `Magen Avraham seif 8 here — it is an uncertainty because many, etc.; one should examine — but in siman 447 too it is an uncertainty, perhaps the pan is a second vessel, and nevertheless they forbid taste imparted by taste:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:3:_": `Magen Avraham seif 12 — rabbinic prohibition in any amount, we follow uncertainty to leniency; in my humble opinion from the words of Tosafos Chullin 97 in the name of Rava it is clear that he holds that in uncertainty one forbids in any amount:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:4:_": `There — and perhaps they split open; practical difference for us when they did not soften:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:5:_": `Magen Avraham seif 15 — that it is baked — a small thing does not leaven; only my inclination is not toward that, that a small thing does not leaven the entire dough — so it should read:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:6:_": `Seif 12 — and there is one who forbids if — see in Chiddushei Rabbi Akiva Eiger Yoreh De'ah siman 91 in the Biurim seif 3:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:7:_": `Magen Avraham seif 19 — he forbade him because there was a crack; in my humble opinion, nevertheless it is proven that the water is forbidden, for otherwise what of it that there is a crack — certainly nothing of the wheat actually entered the crack; if so the water is forbidden because it releases even in cold; only the meat is perhaps permitted because it does not release and absorb, and it is forbidden only because of the crack that the water enters the crack; and so too as Magen Avraham wrote siman 447 seif 9; and if so automatically if they cooked in forbidden water:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:8:_": `Magen Avraham seif 22 — afterward it broke — forbidden; and this is according to the opinion of the Taz Yoreh De'ah end of siman 83 that if he chewed it is digestion, but if he did not chew, even if afterward it was crushed and diminished, it is not digestion; but the opinion of the Shach there is the reverse — that even according to one who holds one does not derive prohibition from impurity, and if he chewed it is not digestion, nevertheless in what diminished from what it absorbed there is digestion — see there:`,
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger:9:_": `Magen Avraham seif 25 — and so too the Derech HaMelech; in my humble opinion, nevertheless, although if we come to permit the chicken from the law of sixty rabbinic — perhaps it was in the matzah — with this the reasoning would be correct, since this permission applies also to the matzah that perhaps it was in the chicken — automatically both are forbidden like the case in siman 111; and if so the permission of sixty rabbinic is removed because I say differently — it is forty against sixty rabbinic; but nevertheless the permission of double uncertainty is not removed, for regarding the chicken there is no double uncertainty — for if there were uncertainty here in the matzah there is no double uncertainty; and if so, since if this were uncertainty that forbids, we could permit the chicken from double uncertainty — for the matzah is certainly forbidden from Torah-level sixty — if so from the Torah because it is rabbinic, and it would also be on the matzah from rabbinic sixty; and this is indeed the permission removed from rabbinic sixty because of "why do you see" — but the permission of double uncertainty was not removed — end quote; and it is different from the case in siman 139 where on the entire house there is double uncertainty, and again "why do you see" applies — end quote:`,
  "shaarei-teshuvah:11:_": `We establish presumptive prohibition. And see in Be'er HaGolah general rule 4 siman 10 that in Egypt they bring wheat in ships and the sailors mix water, mud, and dust into them; even though some wheat leavens they are accustomed to leniency because they are nullified in the rest of the wheat; and similarly when they stack the stack after they thresh it with a threshing sledge, dew falls on the wheat night after night for at least about a month until they winnow and bring to storage — and dew leavens; but they are accustomed to leniency because it returns and revives, and they bake even on Pesach and are not concerned for what Mahari\`t wrote in part Aleph Chayyim siman 1 that one must bake before Pesach; or one can resolve Mahari\`t that in dry such as flour one may not be lenient — see there; and therefore one should not be stringent for them when some matter occurs that depends on this dispute of returning and reviving, such as the dispute of shaking in a sieve that I brought above siman 461 — see there:`,
  "shaarei-teshuvah:12:_": `Regarding ordinary wheat. Shaarei Teshuvah — and he wrote in responsum of Maharan Rotenburg siman 35 that the Gaon Maharam son of the Mechaber answered regarding rains that descended on heaps — there are wheat kernels that sprouted, but if there are sixty against the permitted ones, one may permit even l'chatchila not in a time of distress to grind them, and there is no matter of nullifying prohibition without intent, as written in Yoreh De'ah siman 84; only the Bach wrote that one must bake before Pesach; and this matter of siman 467 regarding grain placed in an attic — since it was not ground and baked before Pesach it is dry with dry and returns and revives on Pesach — therefore one should sell it before Pesach like other chametz mixtures — see there; and see in responsum Me'il Tzedakah siman 69 that he wrote in the year 5472 there were rains during harvest days and we saw that wheat harvested a day or two later for matzah of the commandment were finished and fully dried, and we were uncertain perhaps they were thus finished and dried at the time rain was on the ground; the Rashba ruled that it accepts leavening even while attached to the ground, and he brought that one need not be concerned for this even if they dried completely in their husk, unless when rains were abundant day and night — strong rain that knocks the stalks and sheaves to the ground and rain drips on them strongly, and it is as one who rests barrels full of water — but with rain that is not so strong and does not last a long time one should not be stringent unless we see them split; and know that they required guarding only from harvest time and not from when they finished while attached; and further, behold dew does not stop and falls on them every day, and it is not mentioned in any posek, etc. — see there:`,
  "shaarei-teshuvah:14:_": `Dried fruits. Shaarei Teshuvah and in Noda B'Yehuda Mahadura Tinyana siman 91 he wrote on what Magen Avraham wrote to permit even in an oven in which bread was baked, etc. — perhaps Magen Avraham did not pay attention to see that it is customary to scatter much flour under the bread, and perhaps some remained in the oven in its own form, etc. (in my humble opinion one can say that most of that flour remained stuck to the bread that was only at the time of its going from the peel to the oven, and if a little falls from there the heat of the oven makes it ash and in any case very charred until no taste of chametz remains when they put the fruits in the oven — uncertain); and in the land of Poland in all the provinces they are not accustomed to permit, neither in prunes called plumen nor in apples nor in dried pears — only what was dried in one's house and in one's oven for the festival of Pesach; and when he came to this country they were accustomed regarding fruits bought from those ovens standing in the gardens of the nobles where they do not bake there, but from those brought to sell in the market there is no permission; and in lands of the Ger they were accustomed to prohibition even from gardens — it is difficult to permit even though there is permission on that which is in the garden to make wine from them before Pesach; nevertheless one may not permit if there is concern that a breach will emerge to treat customs and prohibitions lightly — see there:`,
  "shaarei-teshuvah:15:_": `And tsuker. Shaarei Teshuvah — and see in responsum Noda B'Yehuda part 1 siman 23 and in Mahadura Tinyana siman 72 that he wrote regarding powdered tsuker brought in barrels and some are concerned for mixtures of flour, and also perhaps the barrels are from barrels in which they carry flour to that country where it grows; and he wrote that if the seller is among the great merchants one should not protest in a place where the custom is to be lenient, and in any case they should be careful to take that powder immediately when the gentile opens the barrels and take from the middle of the barrels — see there; and see there in Chayyei Adam siman 24 that he wrote that in the place of its growth one need not be concerned for forgery because there flour is twice as expensive as tsuker, therefore the custom when it is brought in a ship to Hamburg or Amsterdam is that the rabbi sends his agents to seal the barrels and from there they bring them sealed with certification from the rabbi — see there; and see in She'elat Ya'avetz part 2 siman 65, etc., that kistin tsuker from the note — its permission was released by our teacher the Noda B'Yehuda; and see in responsum Tiferet Tzvi simanim 18 and 19 that he also wrote to permit that brought in barrels from distant places from the place of tsuker growth itself, such as to merchants of the land of Israel, and the tsuker is bronze dye and one need not be concerned for a mixture of flour, which is a distant concern and is not equal in appearance; but if it is not bronze dye one should be somewhat stringent even though from its place of growth there flour is cheap — nevertheless there is a decree lest it be confused; one should also buy from other merchants who store on the rabbi's route and pour from vessel to vessel, and there is concern that perhaps where flour is cheap they mix flour there; we are concerned even for a gentile merchant — see there; and it appears that in a place of need or loss one may permit in this when it is known that all year barrels of tsuker come to him from their place of growth as they are even if not bronze, and they give him a certification on the barrels that come to him on this route, and there is no concern for confusion to buy from one who has no certification, for it is known that certainly no certification was given to him except after investigation that there is no concern — unlike one who has no certification, the buyer should be concerned for himself:`,
  "shaarei-teshuvah:16:_": `{In the gloss seif 10} They are accustomed to burn everything; and see siman 447 and in the Acharonim what is written on this; and see in responsum Chacham Tzvi siman 86 that his opinion is that even on the seventh of Pesach if a wheat kernel is found in a cooked dish or in a chicken they force them with vessels until the end of Yom Tov, and then they eat the cooked dish and the chicken and burn the wheat kernel — unlike what the Acharonim strained to be stringent to burn it through a gentile, for even on chol ha-mo'ed there is no prohibition here (Beit Yosef); and what they were accustomed to burn it is because of damage; and on the seventh of Pesach forcing with vessels suffices — see there; and see there that if the gentile himself comes to take it, then on the seventh of Pesach it is forbidden to protest against him taking it even regarding rabbinic prohibition in any amount; but on the eighth day of Pesach one can protest against him:`,
  "shaarei-teshuvah:6:_": `In a river. Shaarei Teshuvah — and he wrote in responsum of Mahari\`t Tzahalon siman 45 that a crate full of wheat stood and he passed a bowl of water from place to place and the water fell on the crate, and it was before Pesach; since there was only one bowl that does not contain enough to soak even a tenth of the wheat and they flowed via an incline — therefore if there are cracks in the edges of the box between board and board through which water flows and does not collect so that the wheat becomes soaked, they were permitted; and all the more so here where it fell only on a little, one can say it was nullified before its time of prohibition; and if there are no cracks there, one discards what is at the edges of the box in the place where there is concern that water collected there, and the rest is permitted — see there; and it appears that l'chatchila one should grind the wheat before Pesach:`,
  "shaarei-teshuvah:9:_": `Forbidden. Shaarei Teshuvah — and see in the place of Shmuel siman 58 that he wrote in resolving the difficulty of the Taz on the Rama, he brought as the ruling of the Rama — see there:`,
  "turei-zahav:1:א": `Or water fell upon them. In the Tur he wrote: and they have concern for leavening and it is not recognizable in them — end of his words; for if it were recognizable there is no concern, for the buyer will see the leavening:`,
  "turei-zahav:1:ב": `And one informs him so that he will eat them, etc. In our Gemara it says except one — Rava: let us buy them measure by measure for Israelites so that we consume them before Pesach. And this seif is the language of the Rambam, and his version does not read to Israelites but to a gentile one sells little by little; but to an Israelite one may sell even a large amount once one informs him of the matter — so wrote the Maggid. But the Rosh and Tur have the reading to Israelites, and the author of the Ittur wrote: even if he did not inform the Israelite it is permitted as long as he can eat it before Pesach; and the Rambam wrote that one must inform him, and the Tur wrote, and it is reasonable like the author of the Ittur — from the fact that it is permitted only for a small amount, if one informs him even a large amount is also permitted, etc. Rashal wrote, and one might say: have our mouths been sealed so that we say to the Israelite that it is chametz — why should we rely on the permission that it will be consumed before Pesach not for the need of the matter, since we can inform him? Therefore it appears that it deals with informing, and nevertheless one may not sell more lest he forget and come to a stumbling block — end of his words. And I wonder greatly at the words of the rabbi, for the Rambam holds that one must inform him, and for him it is truly permitted even a large amount, as he states here explicitly; and the Tur's difficulty is according to his reading that reads to Israelites — therefore he questioned even a large amount; but according to what the Maggid said that the Rambam's version does not read to Israelites — the Tur's difficulty is resolved, for if so the large amount is also permitted to an Israelite, only that Rava teaches us that even for a gentile, where informing is not applicable, nevertheless one sells to him measure by measure; and it appears that one should be concerned for the Tur's reading, which is the reading of most books — namely to Israelites, and it is not permitted except with informing; and nevertheless one should not sell except a little, for the reason that when he sells a large amount and will rely on the informing there is concern lest he forget to inform one of the buyers and a stumbling block will come — unlike when he sells a little to a gentile, where there is a double uncertainty that perhaps he will not sell it to an Israelite; and if you say he will sell it to him, perhaps he will eat it before Pesach, as stated above — for practical halachah:`,
  "turei-zahav:10:_": `On the eighth day of Pesach, etc. It appears all the more so regarding taste imparted, for we are stringent on Pesach as written in siman 446 — it is permitted to keep it on the last Yom Tov, for it is lenient regarding rabbinic prohibition in any amount:`,
  "turei-zahav:11:_": `And so they practice during Pesach. The distinction of during Pesach is in the Beit Yosef, who questioned what the Tur wrote in the name of the Rosh regarding a wheat kernel found embedded in dough or matzah — even though permitted, etc., and we are not concerned lest it was in water, because Israelites are accustomed to guard the dough, etc. — it implies that if it had been in water the matzot would be forbidden; and afterward he wrote an incident where an embedded wheat kernel was found in a vessel of water and the water is permitted; and one might say that here it deals during Pesach and that incident deals on the eve of Pesach; and they already questioned on this, for certainly that incident also deals on Pesach as the Tur implies; and I wondered — for according to the Tur, behold the Tur wrote afterward and so Avi HaEzri permitted wheat that fell into a pit, and wrote they do not leaven, etc.; and it appears the case is not comparable to the proof, for that one is specifically in dough, but an embedded wheat kernel certainly leavens in cold water like a stalk that leavens in its husk; but it appears to permit from the Tur because the water is cold the wheat kernel does not impart taste in them, and even full chametz that fell into a pit is permitted for this reason — end of his words; behold the wheat kernel is forbidden in cold and does not forbid — therefore there is difficulty on the Rosh, for if the wheat kernel had been in water it would certainly have been forbidden, and you have no permission in matzah except because the matzah is not forbidden in cold; with this it appears the Rosh holds this is specifically when resting in cold, but in kneading the dough warms somewhat through the work and compresses the wheat kernel in the hand or vessel — certainly there is concern it squeezed some taste from it and forbids; and all the more so in baking; and if so it is not necessary to distinguish between before Pesach and during Pesach. And in Maharil it is written: a wheat kernel was found on the eve of Pesach in matzah — Semak permitted removal of the place; and if a wheat kernel was found on Pesach in matzah before baking, Maharil ruled to burn it by itself in a bonfire; and if they removed the wheat kernel from it and baked it, they must burn it, and the rest of the matzot baked with it he permitted. And a wheat kernel found in dough — the entire dough is forbidden; so ruled Maharash, and Maharshal agreed in responsum siman 79, because through kneading it leavens and gives some taste; and so too the Rama's words here, and so the author of the Or Zarua — if the wheat kernel softened. And what the Rama wrote here that we say here it was found, here it was — the Levush questioned: if the wheat kernel fell there automatically it was not in water and is not forbidden at all — difficulty on the Rama; the Rama's intent here is that we are concerned lest a wheat kernel fell like a chametz kernel from elsewhere into this matzah — here it was found and here it was. And the Levush explained there is prohibition only from the wheat kernel to baked matzah, but in dough he permits because it does not leaven, and wrote the custom is not like the Rama's words to forbid in dough or in matzah that is not baked. And behold in the Tur he compared here dough to baked matzah, and it implies that if it had been in water there would be prohibition; and certainly the author of the Levush did not see the books' words that we mentioned, that forbid even in dough; and the essence is as Magen Avraham wrote here:`,
  "turei-zahav:12:א": `If they scalded a chicken, etc. Stam scalding is in a second vessel; and so explicitly in Sefer Rokeach siman 211:`,
  "turei-zahav:12:ב": `The chicken is forbidden. For perhaps the wheat kernel was in water before scalding and leavened and absorbed from the water — so too in the Tur in the name of the Ramban; and it is difficult why we do not say here it was found here it was; and after scalding the chicken it came there. And it appears he derives prohibition from impurity, for we say in chapter Kol Me'arvin (Eruvin page 35): one touched one at night and does not know if alive or dead, and the next day he found it dead — the Sages render impure, for all impurities are as at the time of finding; and for this reason Rokeach wrote: a wheat kernel found in baked matzah and it is unknown if it leavened while still dough or never leavened — that matzah alone is forbidden, but other matzot we establish on their presumption and permit; here too we say the water retroactively as at the time of finding now; and one should not say here too the chicken on its presumption, for since the water was certainly in those and certainly leavens, and the chicken was certainly there — its presumption was impaired, unlike other matzot above, for even though we say retroactively as now, that is only in that matzah, but it is not necessary for other matzot, as appears to me. And one should not say it is double uncertainty that perhaps the wheat kernel was digested in the chicken — as the Rama wrote afterward one can say it deals with a whole wheat kernel; and also one should not say perhaps it did not leaven, since one who is expert can discern — this is not called a sufficient uncertainty, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 84:`,
  "turei-zahav:12:ג": `An embedded wheat kernel. It implies but if it is not embedded there is no prohibition, for it is double uncertainty — perhaps it did not leaven, and if you say it leavened, perhaps afterward it fell; all the more so here where it is visible that it is not embedded — only we are stringent from uncertainty; but the Rama wrote nearby seif 9 that we forbid in any amount even if they did not split open; and it appears here where there is double uncertainty we permit selling the chicken to a gentile, and we are stringent only after the second uncertainty, like that seif 9:`,
  "turei-zahav:12:ד": `If it is during Pesach. This is written according to his answer to the Tur's difficulty contradicting itself in seif 11; but according to what is difficult anyway, it is permitted here even on Pesach, for cold does not impart taste at all:`,
  "turei-zahav:12:ה": `By force of double uncertainty. The author of the Or Zarua questioned that this is not double uncertainty, for the mind of fools is not a mind, and it is possible that one who is expert knows to assess that it was not digested; and I do not know the difficulty, for this matter cannot be known — it is like entities in a full stomach; and all such cases are certainly uncertainty, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 81. And one can say: here in this seif regarding grain on top he wrote the Rama that one may not permit for eating because of double uncertainty; and one should not say here there is another leniency that there is only rabbinic prohibition in any amount, but regarding grain there is uncertainty in all the grain — this is not so, for above too in some places it implies the majority is permitted; and so is the law in that very responsum of the Rashba that the Beit Yosef brings in a year when rains were abundant — what is written in Shulchan Aruch seif 4, and there it is explained that the majority is permitted; and further, wherever double uncertainty helps, it helps even for d'oraisa prohibition; and therefore it appears he was stringent there for eating only because there is possibility without loss — it is like a matter that has permitters; and as the author concluded in that law that we copied in seif 2, concluding: since there is no loss in keeping it, etc. — unlike food needed for Pesach, where we follow practical halachah, as appears to me. Rokeach wrote: a piece of chametz was found in a bucket of water and it was unknown when they took the water from the bucket whether chametz was in it or not, and they cooked in it and they poured out the cooked dish — but the water in the bucket is forbidden, for perhaps the gentile who brings water put chametz in at that hour — end of his words; and the Beit Yosef brought these words and they have no explanation; and it appears there is an error, and it should read: when they took water from the well — the intent is they drew water first from the well to cook, and afterward drew water in a bucket and chametz was found in the bucket, and it was unknown whether chametz was in the well when they drew to cook — automatically they cooked in chametz water; with this he ruled the cooked dish permitted, but the water in the bucket alone is forbidden, for the gentile drawing to the bucket put chametz at that hour, but that chametz was not in the well at all. And it is astonishing on the Beit Yosef who copied and did not notice; and how can it be — difficult, for it implies if the chametz was before it was cooked it would be forbidden, but chametz in cold does not forbid, as above; and it is possible he holds like the Rashba that the Beit Yosef brings, who argues on the Rosh and holds cold also forbids, and we hold like the Rosh and Tur that cold does not forbid; and it appears if chametz soaked in cold water from the eve — forbidden to all, for soaked is like cooked; and so the author of the Or Zarua in the name of Rashal. The Tur wrote, and this is its language in Sefer Rokeach: a wheat kernel found in a pot — the spoon with which they stirred in it is forbidden; if he placed it in another pot, all are forbidden; and the Beit Yosef on this: it is simple, and why did he not copy it in Shulchan Aruch? And I say this requires deep study, for at first glance this passage is not halachah, for the Talmud Yerushalmi siman 93 in the name of Rabbeinu Tam wrote: what is forbidden in any amount does not impart taste, and any amount from it does not forbid in any amount afterward — meaning from what it releases; and as the Tur wrote there: even if forbidden in its species in any amount — for R' Yehuda we do not say two any-amounts; and if so here, where the spoon forbidden from the pot containing a wheat kernel is forbidden because of any amount — why should it forbid another pot in any amount? And so in the Levush siman 92: chametz and yeast forbidden in any amount do not impart taste, and if it fell again into another pot it is nullified in the majority if unrecognized, and if recognized one discards and the rest is permitted — end of his words; and there we wrote that this if unrecognized nullification is not the intent, and they said this only when recognized one discards; and its release is not forbidden in any amount since it itself was forbidden in any amount; and if so this case here is very difficult; and it appears to me to answer properly: here with the spoon we are concerned lest the spoon touched the wheat kernel itself in the pot and was forbidden from it, not from the cooked dish in the pot. And we find such in Yoreh De'ah siman 95 regarding bowls washed in a dairy churn — they forbid there because the bowls touch the griddle and receive taste from it, and we do not follow the water in the griddle; here too the spoon receives from the wheat kernel itself; and one should not say here as well that the way of the spoon is to touch everything in the pot when stirring, and it was forbidden by taste imparted, not by any amount, for there had to be sixty in what was absorbed in the spoon against the wheat kernel; and it is possible there is not so much absorption, even though we assess the whole spoon everywhere — that is for stringency to assess against it because we do not know how much comes out, but for leniency no, for perhaps there is not so much absorption that there would be sixty in it against the wheat kernel — therefore it properly forbids (but if one inserts) another spoon into that pot that was forbidden because of inserting the first spoon — that spoon is not forbidden so as to forbid another pot when they insert it, since it is forbidden only in any amount from the cooked dish, it does not forbid others in any amount; and similarly if they removed the wheat kernel from the pot it fell in and someone inserted a spoon — it does not forbid afterward by insertion into another pot in any amount, unless there is sixty against the spoon, and it is permitted, as appears to me clearly; and this is a peg that will not move in seif 4:`,
  "turei-zahav:13:_": `Into the pit during Pesach. In the Tur it is written stam, but the Beit Yosef explained it deals during Pesach, for if before Pesach behold it was nullified before Pesach and does not return and revive; and if so Shulchan Aruch holds like the first view in siman 447 seif 4; and the author of the Or Zarua wrote that nevertheless l'chatchila it is forbidden even before Pesach, since it is possible to draw water from another pit; and in my humble opinion here sometimes it is forbidden even b'dieved even before Pesach, for the leavened water remained above — even though a little mixed above, nevertheless they stood and endured and are as if in their own substance, and it resembles that law in R' Yehuda siman 92 regarding did not stir and covered, for it does not mix entirely except if they already drew from there several times before — then it is like stirring the pot and it is nullified before Pesach; but during Pesach there is no distinction, as appears to me. And the Mordechai wrote in chapter 20: this law of hag'alah of a pot also applies; and afterward wrote: but wheat that fell into a pit of water, etc., that it does not leaven while the wheat kernel is cold — end of his words. Once a servant brought a vessel with honey called mead to drink and a wheat kernel was found in it; I said it does not forbid in cold, even though it leavened, as the Tur wrote: since the water is cold the wheat kernel does not impart taste in them, and even full chametz that fell into a pit is permitted for this reason — end of his words. And even though it is written in Shulchan Aruch seif 12 that some forbid during Pesach — we already proved there is no distinction found except the Beit Yosef wrote thus as an answer to explain the Rosh's words, and we are not concerned lest it was in water, etc.; and we already mentioned the difficulty; and if so we do not find anyone who disagrees on this matter; and on the contrary, from the Mordechai we mentioned nearby it is proven that on Pesach itself the wheat was in the well, and nevertheless he wrote it does not impart taste in cold; and also there is no concern here lest the wheat kernel was when the honey was boiling, for then what was released from it was already nullified before Pesach and does not return and revive — even though the wheat kernel is still inside, nevertheless at that time it was cold and does not impart taste, as stated:`,
  "turei-zahav:14:א": `One peels all of them. In responsum of the Mishna Berurah siman 69 he disagrees on this, that peeling is not required for the rest, for if because of concern lest they touched the wheat kernel itself we do not establish prohibition except here it was found here it was; and he expanded somewhat on this. And it is astonishing on him that he did not examine Shulchan Aruch in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 seif 9 that wrote there: and if it is unknown whether milk touched all of them, all of them, etc. Further difficulty: here peeling is not required at all, since the wheat kernel does not spread at all like a weak thing; and he expanded that we are not stringent on chametz on Pesach to say it spreads. And certainly this is true, as we mentioned in siman 461 seif 4 regarding adhesion to matzah — only that case is different, for there is nothing that spreads entirely, unlike when there is meat that spreads — we are concerned lest it went and seasoned the wheat kernel and it returns to spread in the meat, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 98 seif 5; and even a thin piece of prohibition and the permitted of its kind, etc. — we can say here too with matzah and meat; but I already cast a stone on this passage in Yoreh De'ah there. And I mentioned Rashal's words that in salting at least we do not say this reasoning; and nevertheless peeling is required even for the rest because of concern for contact, as stated; and in that responsum he mentioned there that wherever peeling is required and not possible it is permitted like Ra'avad's view. And I brought bundled proofs in Yoreh De'ah siman 91 that we do not hold like Ra'avad, but sixty is required against the peel:`,
  "turei-zahav:14:ב": `And there are those who are stringent. The reason we hold in all prohibitions salting is like cooking — so too regarding chametz; and the Rama decided to be lenient in the other pieces, since also in other prohibitions it is only a stringency of the custom that peeling suffices in salting according to most poskim; and therefore on chametz on Pesach, which is rabbinic prohibition in any amount, one may be lenient; and it would have been fit to be lenient even in that piece that peeling should suffice; only since we see the wheat kernel and it rests on it, one should be stringent on it like the stringent ones; and there is an error in what he wrote, etc. — and it should read to sell everything to a gentile, even though we do not practice thus in other chametz mixtures, as in siman 447; and he means that here in salting it is more lenient than other chametz mixtures mentioned in siman 447. And for the law we saw dispute among the early ones in the Mordechai chapter 20 whether to forbid in salting like cooking; and the later ones — Rashal in responsum siman 79 — rule to burn everything, and so too the author of She'erit Yosef and the author of the Or Zarua. And the Mishna Berurah disagrees with him to rule like the lenient ones, and he is lenient in other pieces even without peeling; and the Rama in responsum siman 128 wrote to permit other pieces, and this is its language: even one who wishes to be stringent should rule as he heard some sages instruct to burn that piece and keep the others after Pesach; and in my humble opinion one should practice thus: if a wheat kernel is found on salted dry meat without brine, one may permit keeping everything this way — where it was found one peels, and the rest one keeps, since there is only uncertainty of contact and one does not eat from it on Pesach but keeps it for after Pesach; and this certainly has no stumbling block, since for many great early and later authorities it is eaten, and we do not keep it; but if the wheat kernel is found inside the brine of meat or fish, then it is considered like cooked, and all the more so if it rested there from the eve — its law is as all that rests in the brine is as if cooked in one pot and all is forbidden and requires destruction, as in Yoreh De'ah siman 105 regarding soaked in brine or vinegar that in a small measure it is like cooked; but what rests above the brine one may also keep until after Pesach, for spreading does not go upward in brine, as explained in Yoreh De'ah seif 9 that there is no spreading upward in brine; and therefore what touches the meat in the brine one peels, and the rest one keeps or does as the Rama wrote at the end to sell everything to a gentile; and it appears here too one should peel the place of the wheat kernel first, for this is full chametz, as appears to me; and this is when it is not in the brine, but if it is in brine it is forbidden to sell, for it is full chametz, and one should do as written nearby. And what Shulchan Aruch wrote in this seif: if an embedded wheat kernel is found — it is plain that for us the law is thus if it softened, even though it is not embedded, as stated:`,
  "turei-zahav:16:_": `The bird is permitted. For roasting does not forbid more than peeling, and the crop is peeling — so too in the Beit Yosef in the name of the author of the Or Zarua; and even though we hold roasting requires sixty, here where spreading in a wheat kernel is not applicable peeling suffices; and further, searing is better than roasting — therefore even for us this bird is permitted:`,
  "turei-zahav:2:א": `For since they rested at ease. The Tur wrote this in the name of R' Eliezer: even though regarding what descends below they are not forbidden unless they split open — that case is different, for there the Torah law is under discussion, therefore they do not come to leavening until they split open; but here, since they rested at ease, they come to leavening; and the Ran in the name of Ra'ah wrote that here splitting open is not required, for it deals with wheat that splits open in its natural growth, and specifically regarding barley splitting open is required; and Ra'am distinguished between small dripping and abundant soaking, as explained in the Beit Yosef; and since he mentioned here the view of R' Efrayim, it implies that we hold there is no distinction between wheat and barley nor between small dripping and abundant soaking.`,
  "turei-zahav:2:ב": `By force of double uncertainty. It requires further study regarding the Rama's view in this, for he wrote these words — the Agur brought them in the Beit Yosef regarding wheat over which water passed via the roof, that we rely on the permission that Ra'avyah wrote; and the Mordechai brought at length in the name of Ra'avyah to eat some of them before Pesach or to sell to a gentile little by little and the rest is permitted by force of double uncertainty — perhaps water did not descend upon them, and if you say water descended upon them, perhaps they did not leaven (meaning: were not leavened); but the lower wheat that is near the floor of the attic — it is nearly certain that water descended there to the thickness of a finger or half a finger, and those one discards and the rest is permitted to keep; but not to eat from them on Pesach, for even though regarding it shall not be seen uncertainties are nullified in their majority regarding eating, which is in any amount — we are not lenient — end of his words. That which he wrote to eat some of them, etc. — it appears there is an error in his words, for this permission to eat some of them the author of the Or Zarua brought in the name of Ra'avyah in the lengthy Mordechai — that it is permitted even on Pesach, for we can say it is a double uncertainty: perhaps they already ate from the forbidden ones, and if you say they did not eat, one can say they will now eat from the permitted — as with a ring of idolatry that became mixed and one fell into the Great Sea and all were permitted to benefit together, for certainly there is permission; and even if they did not eat from it, one may take from it and throw it into the sea, or eat from them before Pesach, and the rest are permitted; and there is no nullifying prohibition l'chatchila since it is before Pesach, and you have no matzah that is not more than the prohibition — two prohibitions — and in double uncertainty we follow leniency even for d'oraisa prohibition — end of his words. And in the Agur that we mentioned he mixed two reasons for permission — namely to eat some of them, which applies even if there is certainly chametz among them; and he also mentioned double uncertainty from perhaps they did not leaven — and it appears to me there is some leap in his words. And in any case, since the essence of the permission here is by force of double uncertainty, why is it forbidden in eating on Pesach? For even in d'oraisa prohibition it is permitted in double uncertainty — all the more so in rabbinic prohibition in any amount; and further, the Rama himself who wrote here that double uncertainty does not help for eating — he wrote in siman 453 seif 3: if there is grain among them that sprouted — for if there are sixty in what did not sprout it is permitted if one grinds them, and that is even to eat on Pesach — behold they do not say it returns and revives; and why did he rule here forbidden because it returns and revives? And necessarily one must say that here it deals where there are not sixty against the prohibition, and the essence of his emphasis teaches that it is not permitted even in double uncertainty. First difficulty: why does double uncertainty not help here in truth? Further, Ra'avyah truly permitted because of double uncertainty even for eating, as stated in his name, and also in the next seif it is proven thus — it requires study. And for the law, it implies even according to all that the prohibition for eating is because there is no loss in keeping them — it implies that if there is loss, such as when he has no other grain, it is permitted even in eating.`,
  "turei-zahav:4:_": `We are not concerned for stam, etc. — so wrote the Rashba in a responsum that the Beit Yosef brought, and this is its language: and even those stacks that we saw rain fell upon them — we suspend to leniency that perhaps water did not enter except in the upper sheaves but not in the belly of the stack; and if you say it entered, perhaps it did not have time to leaven as long as it did not come in water — therefore there is double uncertainty, and for leniency — end of his words. From this too there is contradiction to the Rama's ruling nearby, for behold here it is permitted even in eating:`,
  "turei-zahav:8:א": `There are those who are stringent. It is the Mordechai that we are concerned lest flour or fine flour was mixed in. And the Beit Yosef wrote that there is no prohibition in this, for it is fruit juice and only fine flour leavens; and it is possible that they are concerned that it is full chametz since they knead wheat first. And the Rosh wrote in a responsum general 24 that he did not see a custom of prohibition in honey, for mixing flour is not common and also it was nullified before Pesach; and if we come to be concerned because of mixtures we should forbid it all year — some say they put nevelah meat into it and it turns into honey and is permitted, for after the fact we follow — end of his words. And nevertheless the Rama concludes that one may not eat it on Pesach except what they bring from the hive, even though in the Mordechai he forbids this too because of substitution with other honey — it is an extra stringency. And I saw in many places that they eat it, and also in the holy community of Krakow there is no custom to forbid — unlike the Rama's words here that one should not eat honey except what they bring from the hive that they make a drink from, etc. — meaning even to eat it as is it is permitted, for they make a drink from it l'chatchila; but one should not say that specifically the drink is permitted because it was made before Pesach, for he did not mention before Pesach; and further, he began with eating — therefore this too even in eating is permitted, only that he mentioned the drink as evidence that people practice leniency. And based on this it appears to me there is full permission to cook on chol ha-mo'ed a drink called mead from honey that is recognizable as coming from the hive to the sight of people — anything fit for drinking on Yom Tov; even though the author of the Or Zarua wrote to forbid making the drink on the festival because of concern for mixtures, and wrote that so they practiced — nevertheless it appears to me that one who is lenient for the honor of Yom Tov in these countries where we have no other drink on Pesach has not lost; and there is no applicable custom of prohibition here, for each one prepares for himself before Pesach; but if one comes to cook on the festival and has no drink other than that — one should not protest him, and honoring Yom Tov is in his hand, as appears to me. And based on the words of the Rosh's responsum that we mentioned in the name of R' Yonah, it appears to teach merit on what is written in Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah siman 114 that the Rashba was careful from saffron because they mix dry meat in it; and according to R' Yonah's words there is permission since the saffron dissolves in food and the prohibition is nullified, and it is literally like honey here all year:`,
  "turei-zahav:8:ב": `And dried figs. For there is concern they scatter flour on them; and even those that grew in his garden are forbidden because of what an observer sees — because of other figs; and the prohibition of grapes is that they are placed near figs:`,
  "turei-zahav:8:ג": `And tsuker kandil. This is not what we call tsuker kandil — that has more concern and is full chametz, for they cook white tsuker in sour vats and it reddens; and even to keep it is forbidden because there is a mixture of chametz. Rather it is tsuker kandiya that Maharil wrote they ate on the last Yom Tov, and R' Nasan gave the students tsuker he brought from Candia that was clear to them there is no chametz in it. And it is difficult why they did not decree because of other tsuker like with figs; and it appears that tsuker is a distinct species and its form is recognizable, and we do not decree on it; and it appears this is what we call kaner or hot tsuker that certainly has no chametz and no cooking was done on it — only they bring it as its natural product; that is what they eat on the last Yom Tov, as stated:`,
  "turei-zahav:8:ד": `Saffron that they call zafarin. And even that which they call lond zafarin that is certainly without mixtures is forbidden because of what an observer sees, lest he not know to separate — so I found in an old collection:`,
  "turei-zahav:9:_": `Even if they did not split open. It implies in any case the matter was softened; and so too the author of the Or Zarua regarding a wheat kernel found in dough:`,
  "yad-ephraim:1:_": `467 in Taz s.k. 2 — but Ra'osh and Tur's version is "to an Israelite"; and Tur wrote — and this is its language: and the Ittur wrote even if he did not inform him it is permitted, as it should be:`,
  "yad-ephraim:10:_": `(Taz s.k. 12) And he wrote there is no leavening as we say "the dough will rise in cold" — evidently cold prevents leavening; and it appears to me the case is not, etc. — as it should be:`,
  "yad-ephraim:11:_": `There in Levush he explained, etc. — specifically for matzah its baking — for he holds we do not say here "found" — and we are concerned perhaps it was in water and leavened and gives taste of something through baking; but in kneading he permits from the reason it does not impart taste in cold even something; so is Levush; and what Taz wrote "it does not leaven" — meaning the wheat kernel does not leaven the dough:`,
  "yad-ephraim:12:_": `(In Shulchan Aruch seif 12) And afterward they found in water a kernel — here one must cite s.k. 15 in Taz and erase from below:`,
  "yad-ephraim:13:_": `(Taz s.k. 14) So too Tur in the name of Ra'avan; and it is difficult, etc. — for we say in chapter "In every case one mixes" daf 35 — if one touched one, etc. — since the water was as if certainly, etc.; and if you say it fell now — perhaps it became digested, etc. — as it should be:`,
  "yad-ephraim:14:_": `(There) s.k. 15 — perhaps afterward it fell — only we are stringent in this double uncertainty as written in the s.k. before this — all the more so here where we see it is not split open; only Rama wrote nearby, etc. — and it appears here since it is a double uncertainty we permit him, etc. — as it should be:`,
  "yad-ephraim:15:_": `(Magen Avraham s.k. 18) If a wheat kernel was found next to the chicken, etc. — meaning Mesoret Binyamin explains it deals that he took a cooked chicken cold (or boiling but he holds all the more it does not forbid on Pesach) and placed it in the bowl; and now there is uncertainty whether the wheat kernel was in the chicken — if so it cooked now and is forbidden — or it was not, only in the bowl, and permitted; and on this he challenged — let us say "here it was found," etc. and it was not in the chicken, for one does not establish presumptive prohibition from place to place; and Magen Avraham resolved it deals with a boiling chicken — and all the more he says on Pesach; and if so even if you say the wheat kernel was in the bowl when placing the chicken there it is forbidden because in any case it is a second vessel; therefore he wrote to permit because of a double uncertainty that perhaps it became digested and perhaps it fell now; and there is no difficulty — on the contrary let us say "here it was found," etc. and certainly it fell to the bowl — and uncertainty whether it was in the bowl at the time of placing the chicken while still boiling — uncertainty — or afterward when it cooled — one uncertainty forbidden; one may say this is the explanation — for in any case we do not say "here it was found," etc. except regarding not establishing presumptive prohibition from place to place — meaning since the second place stands in presumption of permission and presumptive prohibition was not born in it we do not rely on prohibition from this place — for we do not establish presumptive prohibition; and specifically from time to time we are uncertain properly since in this place presumptive prohibition was found — perhaps this badness was from before; and if so specifically regarding establishing presumptive prohibition from place to place we do not say; but not regarding if we come to permit because of this that we rely on the chicken; and see in Mesoret Binyamin there; but still requires study — for what double uncertainty is here; in any case if there is no prohibition only because we said perhaps in the chicken — fine to say if you say from the chicken perhaps it became digested; unlike if there is a side of prohibition even if it fell to the bowl — if so there is only one uncertainty whether before or after; and if they come to permit and rely that it came from the chicken — there is also uncertainty whether it became digested or not; and one must say this uncertainty whether it cooked with the chicken and was not digested is one uncertainty; and the essence of the double uncertainty is uncertainty whether this wheat kernel is chametz — perhaps it was digested; and if you say it is chametz that was not in the chicken's belly or was and was not digested — nevertheless there is still uncertainty perhaps it did not impart taste in the chicken at all since now it fell in cold; and so this double uncertainty reverses — perhaps it fell now and did not impart taste; and if you say it fell before and imparted taste — perhaps the wheat kernel was digested and has no leavening; but in my humble opinion Mishna Berurah's difficulty is correct according to what appears in Mordechai — that now at the time of finding it does not touch the chicken; if so we have to say we do not establish presumptive prohibition and it never touched it — as Mishna Berurah wrote there regarding pieces salted with milk; and if so even prohibition from second vessel is not here; however in my humble opinion one may say in Mishna Berurah's difficulty — since in any case this wheat kernel fell from elsewhere to the bowl — if so there is properly concern it fell from the chicken since sometimes there are wheat kernels in it; and since the chicken is close to wheat it is properly considered an uncertainty; and see what I wrote in responsum on the words of Mekom Shmuel; and as in my books responsa Beit Efrayim:`,
  "yad-ephraim:16:_": `There s.k. 17 — it implies its majority is permitted; at first glance there is no proof because there when they grind it imparts taste; and according to what I wrote above in the margin of Taz there s.k. 4 that even by adding until sixty it does not help; however what Taz wrote "and so is the law of that case in responsum of Rashba," etc. — requires study, for behold Rashba in truth permits even in eating — as Taz wrote s.k. 5:`,
  "yad-ephraim:2:_": `There. And I wondered greatly at the Rav's words — meaning that what Maharshal decides for law that much is forbidden even when informing — for behold according to Rambam and his version even much is permitted when informing; and nevertheless according to the Ittur's and Tur's version Maharshal's difficulty stands in its place; and so Taz himself concludes for law; and it is possible what Taz wrote "and it appears to be concerned," etc. comes to resolve Maharshal's opinion — which is a stringency for the Tur's version since such is the version of most books — requires study:`,
  "yad-ephraim:3:_": `(In Magen Avraham s.k. 2) For there they sense the taste of wine, etc. — I did not understand, for behold it is explained there the concern lest he buy it for his workers — see Beit Yosef there; and in what he does not sell much together we rely that he will not sell it to an Israelite except for his own need as buyer, as Hagahot Maimoni wrote and in Rabbeinu Yerucham I looked — and his words require study, for there in Rabbeinu Yerucham it is also written regarding if stam wine of gentiles became mixed with others — that some wrote it is permitted to sell except for the value of yayin nesech that there is no concern an Israelite will buy from him — for it is not customary that an Israelite buys wine from a gentile; and Tosafot wrote that because of the stringency of idolatry we are concerned an Israelite will buy for his workers — and this is truly puzzling, for behold stam wine of gentiles is forbidden in benefit; and therefore one must sell except for the value of yayin nesech; and if so how do we say an Israelite will buy for his workers; and on this Magen Avraham did not correct anything in what he wrote to explain the intent of Riaz that he should not sell much together — the explanation of "much together" is not selling all of it except for the value of yayin nesech (and the explanation of the term "together" is like the term stated in the Gemara "he sells all of it" — because through transferring benefit he can sell afterward only to her alone; but if he comes to sell except for the value of yayin nesech he must sell everything together — for this he said that here he does not sell everything together except for the value of yayin nesech and he has no permission except through transferring benefit); and in truth this is the explanation — for behold in Riaz it is explicit that certainly it is permitted to sell except for the value of yayin nesech — if not we are concerned he will return and sell it to an Israelite for his workers; and however in stam wine of gentiles it is puzzling — since when wine comes to a gentile's hand how will an Israelite buy from him even for his workers; only Rama deals with wine mixed in water — this reasoning is possible. And however according to this one may say Rama wrote specifically much together; but a little we say he buys for his own need; and even though there is no necessity in Riaz's words — nevertheless there is room to say thus; but what Magen Avraham wrote to decide this is not Riaz's intent — for otherwise it is difficult, for behold they sense, etc. — requires study. And see in my responsa Beit Efrayim part 1 siman 39:`,
  "yad-ephraim:4:_": `There s.k. 4 — before Pesach it is nullified in majority — if so, etc. Meaning since it was nullified in majority before Pesach and it is not something that will become leavened as found in siman 447 — if so it is again permitted to add to them to grind and nullify the leavened in sixty as found in Yoreh De'ah siman 109 — that whatever was nullified from the beginning dry in dry in majority — afterward it is permitted to add until sixty to cook them together and it is not called nullifying a prohibition — see there:`,
  "yad-ephraim:5:_": `Taz s.k. 4 — they were all permitted in benefit two together — certainly, etc. — that there should not be more than the prohibition and it would be two, etc. — as it should be; and therefore one must say here it deals when there is not sixty against the forbidden, etc.; and he holds that even to add until there is sixty is forbidden — like the opinion of Rashba that Magen Avraham brought siman 447 — for otherwise it should teach the tikkun that one adds until sixty; and accordingly one may say that even where there is a double uncertainty it is still forbidden in eating by adding until sixty; and see Taz s.k. 17 and what he wrote there:`,
  "yad-ephraim:6:_": `(In Taz s.k. 6) Some say they put nevelah meat in it and it becomes honey — and R' Yona z"l wrote that even if they put nevelah meat in it, since it dissolved it became honey — permitted, etc. — as it should be:`,
  "yad-ephraim:7:_": `In Magen Avraham s.k. 11 — and as written siman 447; and all the more so according to what Magen Avraham wrote siman 447 regarding prohibition of something, etc. — as it should be. Meaning in siman 447 regarding if something from a matter cooked in chametz vessels mixed — he wrote there one should not establish prohibition in something d'rabbanan — see there; however what Magen Avraham wrote thus in the name of Darkei Moshe regarding honey — requires study, for in Darkei Moshe it is explained thus regarding saffron; and what he wrote "it is fitting" Ra'osh, etc. — because Ra'osh in responsum permitted saffron and Magen Avraham had another version in Darkei Moshe's words; but the version before us is primary; and what is written here in the gloss "they do not forbid their mixtures" — it appears he means only in small mixtures; and it is possible that even though in Darkei Moshe he wrote "a little mixed," etc. — and all the more so according to what Magen Avraham wrote siman 447, etc. — nevertheless here in the gloss he decided to be lenient even when much mixed, from the first reason he wrote there that it is fitting to rely on Ra'osh; and also his opinion in Darkei Moshe is thus — only that otherwise it is not customary they put in a cooked dish matters like saffron and the like in more than sixty to prepare for profit; another reason that in something otherwise one should not be stringent; but for law he is inclined to permit and rely in mixtures on Ra'osh:`,
  "yad-ephraim:8:_": `In Magen Avraham s.k. 13 — also what is written in Sefer Nikudot HaKesef that chametz on erev Pesach forbids, etc. — because it will become leavened and not in its species it is nullified in sixty, etc. — as it should be:`,
  "yad-ephraim:9:_": `There s.k. 15 — even though it is baked — nevertheless my mind does not lean that a small matter, etc. — as it should be:`,
};
